      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King and Queen of Portugal.—By a curious coincidence the King and Queen of Portugal were born on the same day of the year, and on September 28th their Majesties were the recipients of congratulations upon the anniversaries of theit birthdays. The King was born in 1863 and is therefore forty, but the Queen is just two years younger. She was the Princess Anunie of Orleans, the eldest daughter of the Comte de Paris, and was born at Twickenham. She was also educated for the most part in this country. Her marriage took place in 1886, three years before Don Carlos I. became King of Portugal, and she has two sons, the elder, the Heir-Apparent being sixteen years old. The Michaelmas Goose.—Many were the old Michael- mas customs, but of some we do Rot know much about their origin, and of others we have mostly forgotten their significance. There is an old saying that "If you eat goose on Michaelmas day, you will never want money all the year round." Why it should be so is not to be easily difuied, but a great many people if they taws any faith in the adage would not be loth to try such a pleasant specific for a very common and general complaint. We do not even know the origin of the old English institution—the Michaelmas goose. In all probability the goose formed part of the rent and ser- vices which had to be rendered to the lords of manors for grants of land. But by some it has been said that the custom arose from the accidental circumstance that Queen Elizabeth was dining off a goose when she heard of the victory gained by Sir Francis Drake over the ships of the Spanish Armada, then attacking the Thames. In grateful remembrance she afterwards dined off goose always on Michaelmas Day and hence it is said became an Englishcustom. The Man who Saved an Empress.—The Empress _Eugenie will be interested in the marriage of Sir John Burgoyne and Miss Gretton, for it was on Sir John's yacht—the Gazelle—that she made her escape from France, in the dark hours of" La Debacle." It was a perilous undertaking as the yacht was only a small one, and a heavy gale was blowing when they left Deauville. On the same night, the" Captain" one of the earliest of the ironclad men of war, with Sir John Burgoyne's brother in command, was lost in the Bay of Biscay, but the Gazelle reached Ryde in safety, and the Empress we may be sure, has never forgotten the gallantry of her preserver. Sir John is a yachtsman still, though turned seventy years of age, and he has been a widower since 1895. A Nobleman of the old School.—The Duke of Rich- mond and Gordon was in feeble health for some time before he was seized with a sudden and fatal illness, a few days before he had hoped to entertain the Prince and Princess of Wales at Gordon Castle. It was thought that his return to Scotland would benefit him, but the Duke was one of the oldest of the peers, having been 'born in 1818, and his age naturally told against him. He belonged entirely to the older aristocracy and had no sympathy for the race for wealth, the passion for elisplay, and the movement which has swamped society with self-made peers, millionaires, and American peer- esses. He belonged essentially to the Victorianageand there were few whose friendship our late Queen valued more than that of the Duke of Richmond and Gordon. Lady Warwick's New College.—Lady Warwick's college for training women in the less arduous branches of agriculture and gardening has more than passed the experimental stage. For the past five years it has been carried on with conspicuous success, at the Reading Hostel, but it is now to be taken a step further by the opening of a larger College at Studley Castle in Warwickshire. This is a beautiful estate of some 340 acres in extent about fifteen miles from Birmingham, and offers the students everything they could desire both from an educational and social point of view. "The castle with its noble hall and its drawing room 45 feet long, and with "a beautiful floor for dancing" is truly grand in its proportions, whilst the ornamental grounds, lakes, and park, constitute delightful surround- ings. Moreover the Castle is fitted with every modern convenience e,nd has ample offices classrooms, stables, glass orchards, and every facility for practical outdoor work'. Altogether 223 women have passed through the course of study at Reading, and the new College will give accommodation for sixty students. A Big Baeaar.—lt was hoped that King Edward's Hospital Fund would relieve the London institutions from the necessity for making special appeals forassist- ance. Instead of that it has in turn become the agency for ingenious devices and the latest of these is a Hospital Shopping Day which a special committee of the Fund are engaged in organising for November 3rd.— The proposal is, that the shopkeepers should give a sub- stantial percentage of the day's taking to the Fund, whilst the public would be invited to make additional purchases on that day. All that seems to be wanted is the cordial co-operation of the shopkeepers, tine, if that is secured, London will be transformed into a gigantic bazaar on November 3rd. A Royal Trousseau.—lt is a matter of considerable interest and pleasure, to note that quite a considerable portion of Princess Alice of Battenberg's trousseau has been prepared in these Isles. Taken as a whole the gowns are either white or in the palest tones, but a few black toilettes have been selected. Black is always modish, and for evening wear is now almost as much in evidence as white. Princess Alice is an exceedingly handsome girlwith a lovely complexion that can stand the proximity of either black, or white, or any of the most trying among our range of colours. Muffs.—Elaborate muffs are being carried by very modish women, though what purpose they are intended to serve, it would be difficult to imagine. They are generally built of materials to match the toque or hat, and are often costly and elaborate in the extreme. Linings for these muffs may be had to suit every occas- sion, those for dressy wear being sumptuous items of silk, ehiffon and lace. They are so arranged as to adjust themselves 'to ally length, and the frills are folded into the bag so that they cannot toss while being drawn through the muff. Home workers who are contemplat- ing building a dressy muff cannot do better than take al lesson at the Comedy Theatre, where some of the most , dainty and fascinating specimens are on view. Besides being one of the most delightful platys of the season' "The Climbers" is an education in the art of dress. Sloping Shoulders and Collars.-0 ur shoulders are being made to appear more sloping than ever before, and the effect in the generality of cases leaves:much to be desired Few amoneus look our best in garments built in this extreme style, and we should all do well to insist upon some slight modification being granted us. As regards collars, we are going to be more sensible this winter than we were last, for all those appearing on boleros, pelerines, and coats, fasten up underneath the throat in front. VireL—The largely increased sale of Virol has en- abled the manufacturers to make a considerable reduction in the price. The 3s. size lias been removed from the market,and the 4s. Gd. size reduced to 2s. 11d. At this popular price it Is much cheaper than cod liver oil, and is more efficacious in nourishing the system. There is in fact very, little genuine cod liver oil to be had, but whether it is genuine or adulterated it ie only the very few who can take it with advantage as the great bhlk of children and adults cannot assimilate it into the system. It is largely on this account that \Tirol has beets substituted in the principal hospitals all over the coun- try and particularly in those institutions set apart for children and consumptive patients, it has been used with remarkable results</p>
        <p>ITADGE.</p>
        <p>-FAILURES. BILLS OF SA.LE, AND Dra3F.NTI7RES Is " Kemp's Mer- cantile MID LES.—According to candle Gazette," the total number of failures recorded in England and Wales during tho week ending Fridsv, September 25th, was l:19, viz. :—New Bankruptcy PrO- ceedings published in the London Gazette, 69 ; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 70. The respective num- bers an the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 76; Deeds of Arrangement,7o—Totall46; being a net decrease in Bankruptcies of 9, and a net increase in Deeds of Arrangement in 1903. to date, of 249.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WRITER OF THIS WEEK'S "SPECIAL."</head>
        <p>THE WRITER OF THIS WEEK'S "SPECIAL."</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Samuel, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Samuel, M.P.. who writes our special article on "Social Reform," is one of the best known of the younger men on the Liberal side of the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>He is another student from Balliol College, Oxford, to add to the long line of politicians who come from that seat of learning. When at college he was a leader of Liberalism in the University, and was President of the Russell Club in the days when the members of that club used to address Liberal meetings in the surround- ing villages. After graduating with honours, he twice contested South Oxfordshire, which is a strong Conservative division, and each time reduced the majority. When a vacancy occurred at Cleveland the Liberals adopted him enthusiastically, and returned him by a very large majority. He soon made himself at ease as a speaker in the House.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel has served for several periods on the Eighty Club Committee, and has addressed meetings for the club all over the country; he has also acted as Hon. Secretary of the Home Counties Liberal Federation, and is the author'of a capital book on "Liberalism," to which Mr. Asquith wrote a very eulogistic preface.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CONSTIPATION FROM CHILDHOOD.</head>
        <p>CONSTIPATION FROM CHILDHOOD.</p>
        <p>Yields to Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Miss Mabel Grace Mills, of the Woodlands, Park-road Wood Green, London in an interview with a " Sun " reporter, told how bile beans cured her of severe head- ache and constipation, from which she had suffered for years. She said :---From the time I was quite a child I was troubled with constipation, and later with bilious- ness, which made me vomit as often as three times a day. This caused me to have dreadfui head- aches and I was also subject to a pain in my chest, which was so bad that it was just like a fire burning within me. "Many days I went about with vinegar bandages across the top of my head in order to ease the headache. HI stooped I would become so giddy that for a minute or two I would be completely dazed. At night I had terrifying dreams; and a curious thing was, that I always dreamed of death. In addition te these troubles I had acute pains in my shoulders and around my waist. When I WU.. fourteen I broke a blood vessel owing to excessive vomiting, and when I got older the periodical ailment which women suffer from was very troublesome and caused me much pain. I used to have had attacks of toothache also, and became quite sallow. "I went under several doctors who treated me for disordered liver and constipation. My parents and myself all along attributed my illness to constipation, but this was in turn due no doubt to liver complaint. Nothing seemed to relieve me. Some months ago my mistress advised me to try bile beans, so I commenced taking them. In a short time the constipation was removed and gradually every other ailment followed. For some months now I have been enjoying splendid health. These months have been the happiest of my life. I never before knew what it was to feel so well, or enjoy such good spirits." Bile beans, which effected this interesting cure' are stocked by all chemists at is. lid. and 2s. 9d. per box. They are a certain cure for constipation,h eadache, female ailments, debility, colds, chills, rheumatism, and all liver and stomach troubles. Beware of imitations and harmful substitutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE V. MIDDLESEX.-At Wesion-sdper-Mare, on Saturday evening. Lancashire soon showed aggressiveness, but several shots at goal wore splendidly defended. Gallagher scored the first goal for Lama - shire,. but Middlesex soon equalized through a clever shot by Wood. The Lancashire captain, Sharrocks,after taking the ball half the length of the bath, obtained a grand goal, and Lomax speedily added a third. At half- time Lancashire led by three goals to one. On chang- ing ends two goals were speedily added by Sharrocks and Davis, a sixth being almost immediately scored by the Lancashire captain, and Gallagher got the seventh. Wallace nearly scored and the Middlesex goalkeeper frustrated several tine shots. Result :—Lancashire, seven goals: Middlesex one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>WIG AN V. OPENSHAW.—PIayed at St. Helens on Tuesday night. rhe combination of Wigan was weak, and Openshaw commenced to attack. Bailey swam down and passed to Davies, Who opened the Openshaw score. Kearsley next tried a long shot which went wide. Peacock sent the ball into the net, but the referee would not allow the point,. as he was standing. A moment later Hague scored for Openshaw a second goal with a grand shot. Half-time: Openshaw, two goals; Wigan, nil. On resuming. Gallagher was prominent, and then Davies got hold and scored again for Openshaw. Wigan then put on pressure, and Swift obtained their first goal. Saunders adding a second. Afterwards Wigan tried hard to score again, but failed, and Openshaw won by three goals to two. •</p>
        <p>HAYDOCK REVISION COURT.—A NOVF.L OBJECTION. At the Haydock Revision Court. on Saturday morning Mr. Riley raised an objection, on behalf of Mr. Edmund Range, of Prescot,to the overseers of Whiston engaging Mr. J. J. Tickle as the registration agent for that township. Mr. Range has been assistant overseer since 1881, but the overseers and the Parish Council have de- clined to allow him to do the work on account of an alleged overcharge for the list in the year 1885. Mr. Smith, of Prescot, appeared for the overseers, and ul- timately the Revising Barrister ruled that he had no jurisdiction. The bill was fair and reasonable, and would be allowed, but as to the question of legal right to the position of registration agent, Mr. Range was told that the Local Government Board Auditor was -the proper person for him to appeal to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WHAT EVERY WOMAN SHOULD KNOW.</head>
        <p>WHAT EVERY WOMAN SHOULD KNOW.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Ann Chamberlain, of Bright's Corner,Burwarton, Salop, tells the following interesting experience which is addressed to her suffering fellow women every- where "Every woman who suffers as I did should know what to do. "I was a great sufferer from weakness, pains in my back, severe headaches, irritability, dizziness, ,and occasional attacks of Rheumatism in my joints. T was also troubled with a sharp pain on the top of my head, and at the back of my neck, and my household duties seemed a burden to me. My trouble began, as far as I remember, from a severe cold which settled in my back, and extended to my legs, often causing cramp in the muscles. " I happened to read in the paper about Dodd's Kidney Pills, and T decided to give them a trial. "The first box I took told me that I had found the right medicine for my complaint. I then resolved to persevere with Dodd's Kidney Pills, and the result is, that I quite recovered my health, and I am free from aches and pains of any kind." There is now absolutely no reason why any woman should suffer for Dodd's Kidney Pills are for sale every- where.</p>
      </div>
