      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>This year many people have, on 'account of the weather, delayed taking a holiday till now, preferring the touch of chilliness in the air of September evenings to the somewhat close atmosphere of an autumn night. There is much wisdom in this, for autumn is with- out doubt one of the most delightful seasons of the year, especially to those whose artistic sensibilities find a deep source of satisfac- tion in the wonderful richness of colouring which pervades a landscape when the first whisper of coming winter turns the greens of summer into gold and orange deepening into crimson. There is nothing which can compare in depth and gorgeousness of colour with the tones of the leaves whose doom is already sealed.</p>
        <p>•k•- 44 , .... . . ..-,5,,,..t..z.. r-v .. ~ . ‘L", ' !\' \\, 1 \\ ~../,' _,, _.• : ' li. \\,' :lk 7 ...._ , ~,. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SERVICEABLE COSTUME)</head>
        <p>A SERVICEABLE COSTUME)</p>
        <p>Man, people whose lot in life permits of such indulgences are departing northwards to the moors, and as the girl of to-day is sufficiently advanced in her ideas, she in many instances spends her days tramping over stretches of heather-covered country with her male relatives, gun in hand. Others, again, restrict their sporting tendencies to driving over in fetching toilettes to lunch with the sportsmen. But in either case a certain style of dress is absolutely necessary, and the tailor-made element reigns supreme. The costume sketched is eminently well 3uited for either of those occasions. It is made of brown homespun which •is slightly flecked with white. The short skirt is pleated to the waist, and the trim little coat is tight-fitting, with a red leather collar and belt. The edge of the skirt is finished with Leather, as this is necessary when tramping across a moor. Everything worn on such Dccasions must be made of some strong, ser- viceable material which can stand any sort Df weather and plenty of hard wear, but must also be cut and fitted to perfection, and rendered distinguished by its chic simplicity. Homespuns were for a time altogether out</p>
        <p>A SMART COAT.</p>
        <p>of fashion till they caught the fancy of some titled notabilities in the world of dress, upon whom their possibilities dawned; and this, together with their undeniably good wearing qualities, has brought them into a vogue which promises to be enduring. Besides, these serges are much in demand, and for street wear smooth-faced cloths in shades of tabac and brown and another dull red— which, for want of a better name, is called somewhat aptly "beetroot." Blue, both of a royal tone and the navy shades—which, though not startlingly new, are exceedingly becoming—are ever in request, and look vpry chic with a contrasting note of colour intro- duced by a lizard green or bright red leather waistband.</p>
        <p>The smart coat sketched is of a putty- coloured cloth, relieved by the introduction of Japanese silk embroidered in green, gold, cerise, and pale blue. The style is quite the latest, and lined with coloured silk or satin it makes a pretty and cosy wrap. No collar, save a strapping of the embroidery, is used ; for, indeed, collars seem to be going altogether out of vogue. Light Cloth jackets, especially those in different shades of fawn boxcloth, are much worn completed by military collars and cuffs, trimmed with a bright colour. It is quite noticeable how very far the loose sac-back coats have dropped out of fashion.</p>
        <p>A RENOVATED DLOUSM</p>
        <p>anu HI place thereof we have the close-fittirw .tight bodices, which I am told, are to be quite one of the leading features this winter. Coats of all kinds, whether tight or loose, are lined with dainty-coloured silks, and in many instances further brightened up by gold or otherwise ornamental buttons. It is not given to everyone to boast a figure which can stand a tight-fitting coat, and therefore a modified bolero is still en evidence ; but there is nothing which looks so smart and striking as a well-built English girl in an equally " well-built " tailor-made which fits the figure easily yet closely. I warmly recommend the subject of my last sketch to my readers, as in it is revealed the possibilities of an old blouse. This blouse originally was of cream crepe de chine, and was made with a transparent yoke of string-coloured lace cut slightly square at the throat. It had had little wear during summer, and showed little trace of even that, but was found unsuited for autumn because of the exposure it gave to the wearer's throat and chest, which were not over-strong. This difficulty, however, was got over by lining 'the :bodice up to the throat (after removing the lace yoke) with a fresh bit of material, and covered it over so as to obliterate all differ- ences between the old and the new with some fresh lace, which also covered the neck-bandi A long pointed end of lace was allowed to fall loose at either side of the yoke, and it was daintily finished by a small applique Pt white roses of nadded chiffoni •e - COQCBTIIi</p>
