      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EUENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EUENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE NEW AUSTRALIAN PREMIER.</p>
        <p>For a long time it has been well known that Edmund Barton was anxious to be quit of the Federal Premiership, and was only waiting a favourable opportunity for retirement from Dffice. Previous to his prominence in politics he was a most successful lawyer ; and at one</p>
        <p>me it was thought that he would be the first</p>
        <p>Federal Chief Justice. However; this post 'fell to Sir Samuel Griffith; and Sir Edmund Barton, and Mr. R. E. O'Connor have accepted the other two judgeships of the Federal High</p>
        <p>Court. His po-litic-al Mantle • has fallen upon Mr. DE2al:in, who acted as his loculn tenens</p>
        <p>when he was in England last year and he fias formed tle following ministry:—Premier .and Minister for External Affairs, Mr. Deakin; Minister for Trade and Customs, Sir W. J. Lyne ; Treasurer, Sir George Turner; Minister for Home Affairs, Sir John Forrest ; Attorney- Gencral, Mr. Drake: Postmaster-General, Sir Philip Fish; Minister for Defence, Mr. Chap- man ; and Vice-President of the Council, Mr. 1?;ay1ord. The new premier has for many years been the leading statesman in Victoria, and, like Sir Edmund Barton, has been</p>
        <p>, ~ :('-- ....,.... _. ky ( ~....... ....,,,.`,‘ N . ~,,„\,,. I%:''''''''':t??.9.-''' ,L. 0.... ---`,'•","7(Al'; ' ka:4, c r Y ' r \.'s \ ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>IIR. ALFRED DEAKIN. SIR EDMOND BARTON</head>
        <p>IIR. ALFRED DEAKIN. SIR EDMOND BARTON</p>
        <p>'prominently identified with the Federation movement. For ten years he was member of the Federal Council, and he took a leading part in all the Federal Conventions, which gradually elaborated the present Australian 'Commonwealth and its constitutiol He came to London in 1900 as Victorian dele- gate, to secure the passing of the Bill, arid, when the first Commonwealth Cabinet was formed by Sir Edmund Barton, he accepted the post of Attorney-General, from which he is now promoted to the premiership. Born in Melbourne in 1856, after a short period of journalism, he blossomed out into a legal and political career. He was elected to the Vic- torian Parliament in 1879, and first took office in 1883 as Minister of Public Works. Two years later, at the age of thirty, he became Solicitor-General, and in the follow- ing year Chief Secretary. As a lawyer, he will most popularly be remembered by his defence of Deeming, the celebrated Aus- tralian murderer, who buried his victims under the cement of the floor. One of his greatest interests in life was the question of the irrigation of Victoria, and, after having visited India, Egypt, and Italy, he published several books on the subject, and carried out large irrigation schemes in the Colony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>ROCHESTER'S NEW MEMBER.</head>
        <p>ROCHESTER'S NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>As regards personal favour things were fairly evenly divided between the Rochester candidates, as Mr. Tuff is extremely popular locally, while Sir Harry Johnston was a very strong outsider with a big reputation. The latter is an Imperial Liberal Free Trader, -while Mr. Tuff, the new mem- -ber, is a Bal- fourite. On the Tariff ,question he stated his -views explicitly in these words: "When free trade was first advocated it ,was stated that -within a few -years it would be reciprocal. But that has not come to pass. No one could object to free trade if universal. I think an inquiry MR. CHARLES TUFF., M.P. into the matter would be to the</p>
        <p>advantage of the trade of the country. But I could not support any measure which would increase the cost of living or tax raw mate- tial." Mr. Tuff, or, as he is more popularly ;,known, "Charlie Tuff," is one of the best- -known inhabitants of Rochester, and a native :of the borough, as were both his parents, who were owners of large prqperties in the neigh- bourhood. He is head of the firm of Tuff •and Miskin, of Rochester, Woolwich, and Dover, large Government contractors, build- ers, and barge owners, the firm poseseing the largest fleet of barges trading between the Medway and London. He is a member of the Rochester Town Council, served the office of Mayor of the borough in 1901 and 1902, and is a member of the Medway Board of Con- servators, a Governor of Sir Joseph William- -son's'School and of the Girls' Grammar School, and Vice-Chairman of the Rochester Educa- tional Committee. His father -in - law, the late Mr. George W. Gill, was also for many years a member of the Corporation. Mr. Tuff xesides at Boley Hill,'Rochester.</p>
        <p>ANTHER " COUNTY " CRUISER.</p>
        <p>Our first line of defence, as a nation, is by no means being neglected nowadays; and the lonstant launchings of new vessels from the</p>
        <p>largest to the smallest, are no sniall source of -..,atisfaction to the taxpayer, who sees in them orno tangible return for his money. They are concrete, not abstract like many other seturns for his payments, which are not so visible to the naked eye. Besides which, it •.5 an axiom of this country's policy since time .ramemorial that Britain must be paramount it sea. One of the most satisfactory of the .nnovations of new types in the Navy has ?roved to be the "County" cruiser;, so called each is named after a county. To this list • .:reach name was added last week by the launch of the first - class armoured cruiser Hamyshire from the Elswick .yard at NewcaStle-on-Tyne, where she was built by Sir W. G. Armstrong, .t 7 hi t wor t ,!?:. Co., who carry out many of the 3overnm,2nt nayal contracts. The Hamp- __ _</p>
        <p>ire in length is 450 feet and has a displace- .nent of i 0,750 tons. Her armament corn- two 7.5 in.. ten 6 in., and ten 12- :;!er guns. The engines will be made. by Hawthorn, Leslie &amp; Co., of Newcastle,</p>
        <p>-ncl will give 21,000 horse-power, with a Spied just over twenty-two knots. The steam</p>
