      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>went away on</head>
        <p>went away on</p>
        <p>Friday were all</p>
        <p>best of</p>
        <p>health and spirits. The': _ had a brilliant send the</p>
        <p>platform at St</p>
        <p>Pancras being thronged with</p>
        <p>their friends and</p>
        <p>well-wishers, All</p>
        <p>previous cricket</p>
        <p>Combin atio ns</p>
        <p>th,at have</p>
        <p>ed Australia</p>
        <p>have been organised by private individuals. The earlier of these enterprises were' of the nature of missionary efforts, and, of course, redounded to the credit of English cricket. The old All-England elevens, the Shaw and Shrewsbnry combinations, were veritable marches of triumph for England. The first serious check came when Lord Sheffield's team, including W. G. Grace and the cream of English talent, was defeated in a test match. Later on A. E. Stoddart's team met with mixed luck, and Archie MacLaren's teams have experienced a series of disasters. 'At last the M.C.C. came to the rescue. They have experienced several disappointments, espe- cially when C. B. Fry, who is easily at the top of the batting averages, declared his inability ,to travel. The next 'best batsman, Ranjit Sinhji, was not even invited to go, the infer- ence being that the Australians did not approve of his selection. Another great bats- man, A. C. MacLaren, declined an invitation an the ground that he ought to have been given the captaincy. It will, therefore, be seen that the present combination *6xcludes the three best batsmen in this country. All the same, the • fourteen men selected form a powerful combination, which, if not brilliant, may do better than most people imagine. Most of the men are new to Australia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FAMOUS CONNEMARA PILE.</head>
        <p>THE FAMOUS CONNEMARA PILE.</p>
        <p>Kylemore Castle, that wonderful structure on the shores of Lough Kylemore, standing white and solitary at the base of a steep, rock-faced mountain, is now the property of the Duke of Manchester. An American duchess will now preside over it, and, by the way, it was built—indirectly at least—by American dollars. There is a pretty little romance connected with Kylemore. It was was built, so the story goes, as the result of</p>
        <p>ICYLEHORE CASTLE HAS JUST BEEN PURCHASED</p>
        <p>BY THE DUKE OF MANCHESTER 4</p>
        <p>a chance' remark: Nearly forty years ago Mr. Mitchell Henry, who was for many years a member of Parliament for Galway, was travel- ling through Connemara with his wife, One afternoon they stopped at a peasant's cottage which nestled at the foot of a steep hill, and asked for a glass of milk: "What a lovely spot for a home," said Mrs. Mitchell Henry. Her husband said nothing at the time, but shortly afterwards he purchased ,i 4,000 acres of land from the Blakes of Renvyle ; and on the shores of Lough Kylemore, within a stone's throw of the peasant's cottage, arose the walls of a magnificent palace. Extra- ordinary difficulties were encountered in building Kylemore Castle. Its site had to be literally hewn out of the rocks, and the iso- lated location made the transportation of materials very expensive. The castle was years in building, but when finished it was and is without a peer among the many beauti- ful residences in the Emerald Isle. The erec- tion of the castle itself required seven years, ancf for long afterwards Mr. Henry was busy improving the estate, reclaiming waste land, and generally changing the face of nature. He spent at least half a million sterling on the castle, which is said to contain more rooms than any other building in Ireland. The castle is of granite, with limestone facings, and is in the Elizabethan style. Connemara marble, hewn from a quarry on the slopes of Lissoughter, a few miles distant, has been generously employed in the interior decorations. It was. Mr. Henry's pride that Kylemore was an Irish home, built by Irish labour, in the midst of the most charming scenery to be found in Conne- mara.</p>
