      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 3, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 3, 1903.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>HOCKBY.—If one firm of manufacturers more than another keeps abreast of the times in the matter of Impl.ments for sport, that firm is Geo. G. Bussey &amp; Co.. of London, who are leading the way again with their Hockey Clubs and Balls which near their well-known trade mark. Ask the local Sports Dealer for G. G. B. &amp; Co.'s Special Hockey List. If not procurable apply to 36 &amp; 38 Queen Victoria btreet, London. eat:tory -Peckham, S.B. 164).</p>
        <p>LE)- v</p>
        <p>swiNTroN.</p>
        <p>A erowl of som3 10, 0) p-ople a••nY:?. 1 on Haaclingley Groan(' to sae the match between Leedsantl Swinton, which WIS played in. lovely w3a,,-,hei-. Leeds won by one goal 2 tries (S piints) to S:vintou 1 goal (2 points). - HUM, KINGSTON lOVERS v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Eight thousand persons witnessed this match at Central Park, Wigan. The home team had distinctly an advantage in the initial half, bat repeated efforts to cross the Hull line proved futile, and in addition two fins kicks by Mason and Hilton went just wide of the mark. Result :—Hull Kingston Rovers 1 goal, 1 try (5 points), Wigan nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>HUDDERSFIELD v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>This match brought a crowd of 7,000 spectators to the Fartown ground. Half-time came with the score nil. The visitors made strenuous efforts to cross the line, and eventually, after a grand passing movement in which Morley, Gledhill, Hadwen, and Wedgwood took part, the latter scored a fine try. Little made a good attempt at goal. On resuming Sutcliffe had a penalty kick, but the ball struck the upright and rebounded into the field of play. Halifax won by 1 try (3 points), to Huddersfield nil. ftbl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" v. WERNETII.</p>
        <p>Warrington" A" placed another victory to their credit on Saturday when they encountered Werneth at the Sheephbot-lane enclosure, Oldham. Warrington were early aggressive, and neat passing and running between Jordan and Brooks resulted in Jackson open- ing the score. Werneth rallied, andafter a spell of even play in midfield Taylor equalized, and the teams crossed over with three points each to their credit. Turning round the visitors immediately assumed the aggressive, and the Werneth goal was subjected to a hot fusilade The visitors scored freely and ran out easy winners by 22 points to three.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNTON.</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY TRIAL TEANIS,</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County Union has selected the following teams for the trial match at Warrington on October 12th preparatory to choosing the fifteen to represent Lancashire in their first county match of the aason, against Cheshire, at Broughton, on October 28:— Probables—D. Smith (Salford), full back; -J. Fish (Warrington), R. Wilson (Bronghton Rangers), and A. Hogg (Broughton Ringers), three-quarter backs; S. James (Broughton Rangers) and J. Lomas (Salford), half-backs ; P. Tanney, J. Rhapps, and G. Heath (Sal- ford), J. Ferguson and G. Frater (Oldham), and G. Boardman (Leigh), forwards. Possibles.—R. L. Thomas (Oldham), full back; J. Dickinson (Warrington), P. Thomas (Oldham), and J. Leytharn (Lancaster), three-quarter backs; J. Baxter (Rochdale Hornets). and J. Molyneux. (Leigh), half- backs; A. S. Boardman (Warrington), H. Buckler (Salford), F. Lee (Sb. Helens), J. R.. Hilton (Wigan), W. Brown (Salford), and G. Aspey (Widnes), forwards. Reserves.—J. H. Tilley (Widnes), full-back; V. Hampson (Swinton), W. Blincow (Rochdale Hornets), and J. G. Liversa.ge (St. Helens), three-quarter backs; J. Lally (Widnes) and R. Banks (St. Helens), half- Nicks ; J. Beetham and G. Ruddick (Broughton Rangers), J. M'Donald (Widnes), and T. Mawsou (St. Helens), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>WINWICK ASYLUM v. WIN WICK RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Played on the Asylum ground on Sathrday, and re- sulted in a win for the Asylum by five goals to nil.</p>
        <p>FROGRALL ALBION'S V. NKEY GREEN.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the latter on Satur- day. From the start Froghall attacked, Leah putting over the bar. Leah and Jones took the ball down the centre and the former finished up by sending in a rattling shot which completely beat Broadhurst. Frog- hall were not allo 'red to keep the lead for long, for immediately afterwards Long put in a shot,and Spencer ran out to clear, with the result that the sphere bounced over his head into.the goal. Half-time: Froghall, one; Sankey Green, one. On resuming, Sankey went away with a rash, and the visiting custodian only partially cleared a shot from Foster with the result that Spooner headed the second goal. Cotterill equalized with a splendid shot from the touch line. Just on time Long put on the winning goal for Sankey.—Final: Sankey Green, three ;.Froghall Albions, two.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. RESULTS FOR SATURDAY. FIRST Drvisuys.</p>
        <p>Warrington Albions Latchford Brittania</p>
        <p>St. Mary's 0 Latchard 0 Newton Reserve _ 1 Oughtrington Park</p>
