      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WORKS BOWLING COMPETITIONS. Presentation of Cup and Prizes at Warrington. SPEECHES BY MR. A, H. CR3SFIES_,D.</head>
        <p>WORKS BOWLING COMPETITIONS. Presentation of Cup and Prizes at Warrington. SPEECHES BY MR. A, H. CR3SFIES_,D.</p>
        <p>The bowling season just cmcluded has been mem- orable in local annals for the special encouragement which enthusiasts in the healthful pastime havereceived ad for the intense interest which has been manifested in competitions promoted for the benefit of employees at the various works. It was a happy idea to form a Works Bowling Association in the town, and so far the results have certainly justified the experiment. The works team competition in which. players from 26 works displayed their skill was a complete success. The bowlers entered into it with zest and the keenly- contested final game between London and North Western Railway and Pearson and Knowles's No. 1 teams resulted in a creditable victory fir the railway representatives. Encouraged by the enthusiasm with which the first venture wa taken up, the association arranged an individual merit handicap and splendid prizes to the value of £35 were offered. The entries numbered no fewer than 288 and some fine exhibi- tions have been witnessed week by week. On Satur- day afternoon the final rounds were decided on the green attached to the "King and Queen" hotel and fortunately the weather was all that could be desired. During the afternoon the spectators who put in an appearance numbered about 500, which gives some idea of the extraordinary degree of interest which the handicap excited. There was some interesting play in the sth round James Shaw having little difficulty hi beating Fiddler. Starting from two behind and givinr, his opponent three, he was 15 tip by the time Fiddler had reached six. The feature of the round was the splendid bowling of the veteran, George Ball, who, from scratch, conceded W. Hill a point and beat him 15-9. They were level at five and eight, then Hill got a single, but after that Ball carried all before him. J. Higham and J. Makin, both starting from two played a capital game, the former being victorious by a • single point. G. Monks (2) de- feated A. Gleave (1) by three ; J. Dainteth (2) beat W. Stevenson (1) by a similar number, as also did S. IVla.ssey (1) win over J. Middleton (1). - E. J. Boardman (2) beat W. Barton (4) by 15-10; H. Dainteth (1) beat T. Davies (scratch) by 15-11 ; and W. Andrews (2) beat J. Firth (3) by 15-6..</p>
        <p>The'si.xtli round witnessed the defeat of Shaw. He owed 2, while his opponent G. Monks, received 2. Playing with capital judgment the latter was up by the time Shaw had reached 8. S. Massey (1) beat G. J.Board- man (2) by 15-10; H. Dainteth (1) beat J. Dainteth (2) by 15-6; G. Ball, sen. (scratch) beat J. Higham (2) by 15-4, while W. Andrews (2) received a bye. In the seventh round W. Andrews (2) distinguished himself and scored 11 before George Ball obtained a single, and ultimately won with a margin of 10. The game between H. Dainteth (1) and S. Massey (1) was exceedingly close, for they peeled at 3, 4,6, 7, 11, and 13. The former then got a couple. In the semi-final, H. Dainteth had a bye, and it was left between George Monks and W. Andrews as to who should appear in the final. Both started on the 2 mark. Monks obtained the first couple and then led by 7-4. Andrews placed a single to his credit, and Monks had a run to 13-9. Everything now pointed to the success of Monks, but Andrews by excellent judgment, forged ahead and passed his man at 14-13. By getting a single Monks drew level, but Andrews, amid great excitement, scored the winning point. The final round saw Andrews (2) away first with a couple. Dainteth (1) drew level at five, after which the former went to 11-6 and 13-7. Dainteth however, played strongly and again got level at 13. Men they were 14 each, and, as in the semi-final, Andrews won by the odd point. There were enthusiastic cheers for the finalists, and Mr. J. WILLETT, chairman of the committee, from the green thanked those who had taken part in the competi- tion for their sportsmanlike conduct throughout, and the assistance they had given to the committee. All were delighted that their first handicap had been such a pronounced suocessliV(Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SNIO-KING CONCERT.</head>
        <p>SNIO-KING CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The cup, medals. and prizes ware distributed at a smoking concert held in the club room at the "King and Queen" hotel in the evening. In the absence of the President, (Captain G. R. Crosfield,)the awards were handed over to the successful competitors by Mr. A. H. Crosfield. The room was well filled and Mr. Crosfield had a flattering reception, the company singing "For he's a jolly good fellow." Mr. Crosfield presided and the attendance included Messrs. S. Taylor, vice-presi- dent ;11. Willett, Joseph Harrison, George Ball, sea., T. Ellison, T. Lawton, H. Williams, J. Monks, W. Simmis- ter, members of the committee ; F, W. Scarisbrick, hon. secretary, and Fred Clare, hon. treasurer. A letter had been received from Mr. Charles Smith, of Croft, one of the vice-presidents, expressing regret at being unable to attend, and wishing the Association every success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>RAILWAY TEAM'S SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY TEAM'S SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>The first pleasing duty which devolved upon Mr. Crosfield was to present the handsome silver cup gained by the London and North-Western Railway team in the recent works competition. This trophy, presented by Mr. J. Finlayson, is beautifully designed and is sur- mounted with the figure of a bowler in the act of delivering his bowl. Mr. WILLETT apologised for the absence of the Presi- dent of the Association, Captain Crosfield, who desired him to convey to the members his cordial thanks for electing him to the position. Captain Crosfield hoped at some future time to have the pleasure of meeting them. (Applause.) Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD who was cordially received, said, he had first to present the cup to the North- Western team who had won a very great victory in a very successful competition. It was not the first time he had had the pleasure of congratulating the team because his old friend Mr. Willett kept him very well posted up week by week in the bowlingevents of Warring ton, and he (the speaker) had the pleasure of telegraph- ing to the North-Western team his very hearty con- gratulations. He was sure it was a, splendid match and the runners-up also did themselves great credit. He had great pleasure in asking Mr. T. Horrabin, the captain of the winning team, Co receive that magnificent trophy which would be the property of the team for the next twelve months. (Applause.) He would have been very glad indeed if his brother as President of the Association, could have come and made the presentation. It was however, impossible for him to do so. I was very nice for them to have made Captain Crosfield President of the Association, and he (the speaker) was very proud Ito be a .Vice-President. He:had donea little bit in furthering bowls in years gone by, and he was very glad they had got his younger brother into harness. (Laughter.) He had no doubt they would initiate him into the duties of President,. He did not know who invented the, definition which he (the speaker) quoted at a golf club the other day but he rather suspectedit had come from over the water There was a flavour of shrewd American wit about it, but he thought it !-as very apropos, and they might quote it to his brother if they liked. The duties of a President were to look bonny, to speak honey, and to spend money.j (Loud laughter.) Mr.rnosnELD then presented the Cup to Mr. Horrabin who briefly returned thanks. He said he was sure they would all agree that the win- ning team made a gallant fight. He congratulated the runners-up upon the gentlemanly manner in which they played. He did not think two .better teams ever met because to his knowledge there was no a wrong word spoken during the game. He hoped that next year the competition would be even more successful than it had bean this year and that more teams would take part. (Applause). Gold medals, presented by Mr. F. Clare, were then handed to the following members of the team :—J. T. Whittle, W. Bridgin, W. Gleave, R. Marrow, A. Peters, E. Taylor, R. Mercer J.. Hutchins3n, J. Millington, T. Horrabin (captain), j. Holland. C. Millington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNNERS-UP</head>
        <p>THE RUNNERS-UP</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFrELD then presented silver medals with : enamelled centres to the members of the Pearson and 1 Knowles team. He remarked that he ,was told to offer a few words of sympathy with the Captain and members of the Pearson and Knowles team, but he was afraid he ; could not offer them anysympathy at all, because the medals looked so very pretty that he thought the recipients were almost as fortunate as the members of the winning team. (Applause). Mr. G. Ball, the Captain, was first presented with a medal, and in returning thanks endorsed all that Mr. , Crosfield had said in reference to the competition. The . final was one of the best games that had been played in : , Warrington, and if Mr. Crosfleld had been there he ' ! would no doubt have appreciated it. The following members of the team were also recipi- ents of medals :—J.Worsley, W. Mee, J. Jennings, J. Worsley, T. Davies, J. Hardman, C. Littlewood, H. Williams, E. Gibson, P. Ball, J. Gleave, George Ball (captain). Mr. ANDREWS said the prizes were the best he had ever won. He hoped the other competitors would in future years be as successful as he had been, and, he added, work as hard as he had done." Other prizes were presented as follows :-2, marble clock, value £3 14s. 6d., H. Dainteth, Smith's Wire Works, gold watch, value £2 ss. George Monks, Rylands Bros., 3,4 and 5, value £1 hs. each, George Ball, sen., Thewlis's Works, and S. Massey., Whitecross Works ; 6,7, 8, and 9, value £1 is. elch,,..Joseph Damn- teth, J. Higham, J. Shaw, and G. J, Boardman; 10 to to 18, value 10s. 6d. each, W. Fiddler, Isaac Makin, W. Stevenson, A. Gleave, W. Hill, J. Middleton, W. Barton, J. Firth and T. Davies ; 19 to 36, value 6s. each, W. Gleave, Joseph Holbrook, jtm, B. Tomlinson, J. W. Monks,. W. Holloway, W. Hunt. G. Higham, E. Gleave, W. ' Sanders, James Hunt, W. Butterworth, J. W. Evans' T. Ellison, Torn Edginton George Craddock, J. Ditchfield, George Holbrook, and Alfred Scott. Prizes of the value of 3s were also .Won by A. Foster, John Gibbons, 0. Ellam, E. Littlewood, J. Clough, F. Miller, H. Erndrson W. Skelhorn, J. Wood,B. Williams, George Ball, jun.. C. Jordan, J. Jackson, E. Bond, A. Ball, C. Littlefair,W Ashall, W. Bowering, E. Taylor, T. Bridge, T. Bibby, George Hewitt, S. Fletcher, H. Whitfield, E. Dwaine].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>....., __. igti thillitl i ,: 6 liltiff.t. . ....... ....... .......</head>
        <p>....., __. igti thillitl i ,: 6 liltiff.t. . ....... ....... .......</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. KEIGHLEY.</p>
        <p>The above teams met for the first time under 'the Northern Union on Saturday, at Wilderspool, before 5,000 people. Keighley missed a railway connection at Manchester and arrived 50 minutes late. Warrington were without Harris and Edmunds, the place of the former being taken by Forster,and Mahoney being given a trial athalf-back. The visitors were well represented. Mr. F. Renton was referee, and the teams lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>Warringtott—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickin- son, and Forster, three-quarter backs; Mahoney and Davies, half backs; Thomas, Morrison, Boardanan;Cook, Swift, Harmer, Naylor and Edmondson, forwards. Keighley—Walker, back; A. Pearson' Hardwick, Mitchell, and Jagger, three-quarter backs; Myers and Caine, half backs; Holden,Fearnley, Tempest, Saunders, E. Peeason, Houlker, Dawson and Hall, 'forwards. Warrington soon forced the pace and for a time kept Keighley busy defending. Fish, Isherwood.and Dickin- son were all stopped on the verge of the line, the com- bination failing at a critical moment. The visitors re- taliated energetically and eventually the forwards, ably led by Fearnley and Holden, forced Warrington to their quarters, and after clever passing and running by Caine and Jagger, Myers picked up and dropped a fine goal. Keighley again made strong play onresaming, and after Dickinson had failed to clear his lines, Jagger inter- cepted a pass and raced through the Warrington backs. Walker landed the goal. The hornebacks next attacked strongly and Davies, Mahoney, and Dickinson combin- ing, the last-named raced between the posts, for Fish to add the goal, Keighley leading at the interval by 7 points to 5. _ _</p>
        <p>- Turning round Keighley were early on the attack. and Walker almost placed a goal in the first minute. Re- taliating, the " wire-pullers "forced the pace, and the Yorkshire goal was subjected to hot fusilade. Both Dickinson and Fish were pulled down by Walker at the corner, but soon after Dickinson put on the finishing touch to a round of passing. The same player was badly hurt in his effort and had to retire. Warrington still held the advantage, and the backs passing with judgment Fish scored a try, whilst Isherwood added another. Fish placing one goal, Result :—Warrington 2 goals 4 tries (16 points), Keighley 2 goals 1 try (7 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Great interget centred in the above match at Runcorn, there being about 5,000 spectators present. Aspey started for Widnes, the visitors at once taking up the attack, and after a scrummage Kennedy passed out to Taylor, who dropped a goal for Widnes. Runcorn were still forced to defend. Jolley found slight relief, but Kennedy again got possession and passed to Peacock, who scored a smart try for Widnes, which Tilley con- verted. Resuming Jolley, Butterworth, Price, and Moran gave a fine exhibition of passing, A. Richardson finally scoring, after the ball had passed through the hands of eight or nine Runcorn men. Jolley failed at goal. Half-time :—Widnes 2 goals 1 try (7 points), Runcorn 1 try (3 points.) The second portion was keenly contested. Jolley, Richardson, and Butterworth got away with passing, and the latter scored a fine try, but was tackled in such a foul manner by McCabe that the referee promptly ordered the Widnes man off the field. Jolley again failed at goal. No sooner was the game resumed than the home forwards rushed the ball over the Widnes line, Walker scoring. Jolley kicked a goal. Shortly before the close Jolley got possession and dropped a goal, a rough game ending in favour of Runcorn. Result : Runcorn, 2 goals, 3 tries (13 points) ; Widnes, 2 goals, 1 try (7 points). Teams Runcorn: Houghton, back ; Jones, Butterworth, Price, and Moran, three-quarter backs; Jolley and J. Richardson, half backs; S. Walker, Woods, Langley, Moulton, A. Richardson, J. Walker, Tomlinson, and Messenger, forwards. Widnes: Tilley, back ; Wailing, Whelan, Taylor, and Peacock, three-quarter . backs, Lally and Kennedy half backs; Aspey, Lynch, Leyshon, McDonald, Rigby, McCabe, Power, and Webster, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Priestley (Broughton Rangers). Touch judges, Messrs. Devereux (Leigh), and E. Bennet (Tuebrook).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LEIGH v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>Played at Leigh, before 4,000 spectators. Scott started for the Rangers, who pressed immediately, their forwards beating Leigh repeatedly in the scrummages. At the interval the Rangers led by 5 points' to 3. Immediately on resuming Boardman, of Leigh, got hurt and retired, but resumed after a while; then Johnson, the Leigh centre, was rendered useless for the rest of the game by an injury to his leg, and J. Roberts had to be brought from the forwards. Two or three clever attacks by Leigh were foiled, and after smart passing Hogg raced over at the corner, no goal resulting. Leigh made several attempts to get within scoring •distance, but were beaten off, and then after some little difference with Barrett, Burgess, the Leigh half back was ordered off, and Leigh played thirteen men. Towards the close, after Widdison had almost scored Wilson knocked off several opponents, and got a try, W. James failing at goal. Result :—Brought on Rangers 1 goal, 3 tries (14 points), Leigh I try (3 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BRADFORD v. OLDHAM</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. OLDHAM</p>
        <p>At Park Avenue on Saturday, before about 9,000 spectators. The first half was pointless, the advantage resting with Bradford. Of the second half twenty minutes elapsed ivithout either side scoring. Then Spottiswoode got over as o, result of good passing and slack defence, Ferguson fail- ing at goal. Bradford's chances looked very poor now, but they kept up the pressure, and were rewarded when Laidlaw got over near the goal posts, and Mosby kicked a goal, a few minutes from the call of time. Result : Bradford 1 goal 1 try (5 points), Oldham 1 try (3 points). .e HULL v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>This match was played befOre a crowd of fully 8,000 spectators on the Boulevard ground at. Hull. Hull made a good start with a try in the corner by Frank, which, bowaver' was unconverted. There was any amount of dash in the home play, and the game was extremely fast. Batley roused themselves from this point to a big effort. Hull were kept on the defensive, and Frank's kick b,eing charged, Hodgson had nothing to do but take it and plant it over under the posts. Wattle Pavies kicking the goal. Result :—Hull 1 goal and 1 try (5' points), Batley 1 goal and 1 try (5 points.)</p>
        <p>HUNSLET V. SALFORD • DISASTER TO THE PARK4DERS.</p>
        <p>The meeting of the 'Huns let and Salford teams,- both unbeaten when they faced each other at Wea4te in glorious weather, attracted 12,006 spectators to the Salford ground, and the onlookers saw a faut,enjoyable and—though the score does not suggest such a eircum- stan3e—evengame. Salford led by 13 points at the crossing.over. In spite of this tall score, Hunslet stuck gamelyto their work in the second half, and kept. Salford defending for fully three parts of the time Their scoring efforts however, all went Wrong, and the only success which came to them was a dropped goal by .Place. Salford won the game by 3 goals and 4 tries (IS points) to Hamlet 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>Ashton, T. Lawton, A, Latimer, W. josling, G. Ellison, J. Wilcock, H. Hankey, R. Dagnall, George Hatton, T. 1 Quarry, and C. Crozier. Mr. BALL remarked that one of the first games of bowls that Mr. Crosfteld played was played with him. He hoped there would be many more works commtitions.</p>
