        <p>cordially congratulated him upon a victory which he was sure Mr. Andrews would be proud of as long as he (Applause). At nine o'clock Mr. Crosfield had to leave to catch the train for Hoylake and the company sang "Auld lang syne." Mr. Taylor then took the chair.</p>
        <p>During the evening an enjoyable musical programme was submitted. The Firth Glee Party. under the con- ductorship of Mr. r, . Roberts, was in attendance, and rendered "0, who will o'er the downs go free," "Crown'd with clusters of the vine," and "By Celia's</p>
        <p>arbour" with taste and expression. Songs were effec- tively interpreted by Messrs. J. W. Willett, T. Humphries, Kenwright (Liverpool), A. Pemberton and T. Horrabin. The accompaniments were well played by Mr. W. Pemberton. In addition Mr. Bell created con-</p>
        <p>siderable mirth with his humorous sketches "The</p>
        <p>village concert" and "The Scotch christening" and was</p>
        <p>heartily encored,</p>
        <p>On tiie propoition of Mr. SCARISBRICK, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Taylor for presiding.</p>
