      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURaIES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURaIES.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>Master Clatworthy, a boy of l:; vontributed an organ solo entitled" Fanfare," which was a special feature of the harvest festival services held in the above place of worship on Sunday. The church was completely fill ed,and the solo was rendered with cleverness and showed excep- tional ability for one so young. The Rev.J.F.Matthews gave a special vote of thanks to the yOUlla organist who had by his playing showed undoubted talent, and encouraged him to persevere. The boy is a pnpil of Mr. Fred•Podumre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Wycliffe P. S. A. Sogiety.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P. S. A. Sogiety.</p>
        <p>'the meeting of this society on Sunday was presided over by Mr. Bannister, of Warrington who spoke upon the wOrds " Come unto me all ye that labour and are heavy laden and I will give you rest." Mr. Heaton, of Manchester also delivered an address on the words "The kingdom of Heaven suffereth violence and the violent take it by force." In referring to the P. S. A. Society he said they had the making of a great cause in Warrington and he trusted they would go on and prosper Master Pearce,of Dudley, presided at the organ and de- lighted the audience.with his skill. in addition to aocompanying the hymns he played an organ solo. Mr. Faa-rar (honorary secretary) announced that the member- ship now stood at 518. Six new members had. been en- rolled that day.. The saving S club had now G 5 members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Yankee Trick</head>
        <p>A Yankee Trick</p>
        <p>Grocers are selling Fels-Naptha soap and returning the money to all that ask for it.</p>
        <p>Everyone likes it so well, of course, that nobody takes the money.</p>
        <p>Good trick!</p>
        <p>• Fes-Naptha 3g Wilson street London</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DEAR BREAD.</head>
        <p>DEAR BREAD.</p>
        <p>Ever since the memorable occasion on which 'Mr. Chamberlain first advocated his policy of fiscal reforms the question of dearer food has loomed large in the public mind, dwarfing all other considerations by its OWII magnitude and importance. But while the apostles of Free Trade and protection have been prophesying respectively the destruction and salvation of the Empire as a result of .the adoption elf Mr. Chamberlain's policy, a considerable section of the community have refused to be roused from their normal condition of lethargy and drowsiness and have maintained an attitude of careless in- difference throughout. To them, as to the whole army of tortured •,;dyspeptics, whether food be !cheap or dear is a matter of little importance. Their one and only concern is the discovery of something to rid them of the horrible indigestion that renders life worthless and enable their stomachs to digest the morsel of food that nature compels them to eat. For that, no scheme of protection is of any avail save that which is afforded by the timely and regular use of the root and herb tonic, Mother Seigel's Syrup. This worla-famed remedy effectively prevents the human stomach being turned into a dumping ground for all manner of foreign and harmful products (of indigestion) and secures the free distribution of food nourishment within the body, thereby upbuilding and strengthening the entire system. In other words, Mother Seigel's Syrup cures indigesw don and restores tone to the stomach, liver and kidneys, en ibling them to perform the complex and difficult pro- cesses of digestion efficiently. Proof of this is afforded by the following letter from Mrs. Rogers, Fay Gate, Horsham, Sussex. Writing on May 2nd, 1903, she says : "My illness began with toss of appetite and depression. My strength failed rapidly and 1 suffered much from headaches, dizziness and nervousness. Wind was a constant trouble tome, caus- ing a feeling of suffocation that was very distressing and I was searcely ever free from pain at the stomach and between the shoulders. My sleep was very broken, and in the morning I got up quite weary and unre- freshed. I consulted Doctors and took quantities or medicine, but got no better, and was beginnire to despair when a friend advised. me to try Seigel'a Syrup. It had saved her life, she :said, and was sure it would do me good. On her recommendation 11 got 10, bottle, and after taking a few doses felt myself getting better. My appetite returned, the drowsiness. head- aches and dizziness left me,andl was soon as well'as ever before. Though it is some time now sinee my illness, I have never suffered in the same way since. Seigel's Syrup is a splendid medicine. I shallalways recommend '</p>
        <p>Cured dyspeptics all over the world greatfully ac- knowledge that no remedy is so potent for the cure of all stomach and liver disorders as this natural tonic of fruits, roots and herbs.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Barbara )1 )Ire, w!io recently died at Rolvendeg in Kent, at the age of 913, was said to have been the dart sirvivor of those who witnessed the battle'of Waterloo Born in 1811 at Gibraltar, she was the daughter of a.- colour sergeant, who took part in the battles of Badajoz and SAlanianca and in Napoleon's final defeat. With her mother she accompanied her father's regiment, travelling in a waggon. She professed to -clearly remember having crossed the battlefield covered with thad and dying., on the morrow of the battle. Hen father died in 1817. SThe herself married and became, the mother of eleven children. An interesting discovery, or rediscovery, has been made at the Garde-Menbles, or storehouse of furniture, belonging to the French nation, in the form of the whole of the carved wainscotting of a room executed for Louis XIV. This wainscot, the carving of which is described as incomparable, is divided into 41 panels, eaCh of five divisions. The subjects of the carving are the Laurel Wreath; the Lyre, the Escutcheon of France, and a Royal Trophy, surmounted by the Sun of Louis i XIV. The whole, which s painted white and retains its original ironwork, fortunately escaped the " beauti- fying"to which the Palace of Versailles was subtectediha the reign of Louis Philippe.</p>
      </div>
