        <p>HOME WRITING REQUISITES sent on receipt or is. S. Jomrson. 12 West street, Wandsworth.</p>
        <p>iItIGALN, American,4,owerftil,'suit chapel; 15 stops; £lB Ll Carriage paid.—Luc, 34. Mortimer street, London.</p>
        <p>-1 ADIES' HOSE GIVES AWAY, apply HOPEWELL. 214, I/ Essex road, London.</p>
        <p>USED STAMPS 10,000 for 2s. 3d.--ItswiaNsom, Beech kJ street, Swinton, Monctiester.</p>
        <p>(alts Rtniton.</p>
        <p>with Disraeli's famous protest against the charge that the Turks had tortured Bulgarian peasants. The Turks, he said, employed" most expeditious means " to crush resistance ! Further, we learn from the " Post "that the SULTAN has "always encouraged his Christian subjects to live in peace and quietness," and that they are no more oppressed than the subjects of Prince FERDINAND OC King PETER. It certainly needs considerable hardihood to make these asser- tions at this time of day. Everybody knows that since Bulgaria became a Principality the lot of its people has been incomparably better than that of their co-religioniss in Macedonia. It is equally within common knowledge that the Moslem peasantry in Bulgaria live at peace with their Christian neighbours. Moslem rule over Christians in Eastern Europe is a constant and grinding tyranny; but against, Christian rule over Mgaslems there has never been !a whisper of complaint. These are elementary facts which only tho most wilful ignorance can dispute. They are so widely accepted in this country and throughout Europe that to represent as visionary and mischievous any expression of public opinion which takes cognisance of them is a gross travesty which can deceive nobody. "While the " Post " assumes that the. SULTAN is an en- lightened monarch, whose benevolent intentions are frustrated by wicked bandits, the BRITISH AMBASSADOR at Constantinople is lamenting the estremely " inadequate " provision for the exe- cution of the promised reforms. That is the lament which found a more strenuous voice at St. James's Hall. Public opinion is not convinced by Mr. BALFOUR, that the Government has done everything in its power to save Macedonia from the SULTAN'S methods of restoring "order." It is manifest that Russia and Austria are unwilling to make the scheme of reforms a reality ; but are France and Italy uavvilling ? The Western Powers subscribed to the Austro-Russian pro- posals; but it was surely on the understanding that these should be carried out, and not that the SULTAN should be allowed to brush them con- temptuously aside. This is the point to which Mr. BALFOUR, did not address himself in his recent letter, although it is the crucial point of England's responsibility. W e want to know whether the Government has actually proposed that a Edropea.n Commission should control the application of the reforms, thus abrogating the SULTAN'S direct authority. Has any effort been made to obtain the adhesion of France and Italy to this measure, and to press it upon the Cabinets of Vienna and St. Petersburg ? If this pressure were employed without success, if Russia and, Austria could not be induced to join in this coercion of the SULTAN, then MC. BALFOUR could say that he had exhausted every endeavour short of war. It is idle to talk as if England could undertake coercion single-handed. MT. GLADSTONE forced the SULTAN, in 1880, to make certain territorial concessions to Greece stipulated by the Berlin Treaty; but he did this with the co-operation of the other Powers. They were unwilling, it is true, but his energy shamed them into action. The conditions to-day are more difficult. Russia makes no pretence of any zeal for the welfare of the Eastern Christians. She was eager to create a strong Bulgaria in 1878; and but for DISRAELI'S shortsighted policy the greater part of Macedonia would now be under Prince FERDINAND'S rule. But since she found it impossible to use the Bulgarians as docile instru- ments. Russia has been only too thaukful that her aims were not successful twenty-five years ago. She would rather see the Bulgarians of Macedonia ex- terminated than see them joined with their kinsfolk in the Principality which:has proved such a trouble some obstacle to her diplomacy. Austria is jeal- ous enough of Russian predominance in the East but is too much distracted 1-437 her domestic pro- blems to take a bold initiative. How, then, is the realease of the Macedonians from Turkish rule to be achieved ? No paper reforms, as Mr. BaYcE says in the first number of the "Independent Review, are of the slightest use. "All Turkish intervention, whether military or civil, must be ended, and control be placed in the hands of a European Governor, neither appointed by, nor responsible to, the Turks, who shall have com- mand of an efficient gendarmerie and of revenue sufficient to maintain it." The example of the Lebanon shews that this policy can maintain peace in a community where Christians are divided from Moslems, and Christians from Christians. If Russia, backed by Austria and Germany, absolutely vetoes such a policy for Mace- donia, nothing can be gained. But we want to be assured that the British Government has done its utmost.</p>
        <p>A step, fraught with great possibilities for the educational future of Warrinton, was taken on Wednesday morning by the ooening of the new Secondary Day School :-).t the Technical Institute Accommodation is providad forl2B students and 04 pupil teachers, so that the schnol is making a start indicative of the need there has long been for such an institution in Warrington. Hitherto the majority of elementary scholars have easily passed through their standardsand,then given up schooling in order to go to work. There was no means of de- veloping their education any further except by the aid of the Technical classes, and the proportion of those who paid their fees for this purpose was small in'comparison to the numbei. who loft the schools. With facilities for Secondary e(itiecition there is no doubt that the majority of children would have passed on from the elementary to the secondary school and thus greatly enlarged their mental equipment for the battle of life. Now that a Secondary School has been opened in Warrington, there should be an end to the profitless "marking time " which has gone on for years in the elemen- tary schools. Scholars who have passed through the ordinary standards by the time they are twelve or thirteen years old will be able to "move on" to some purpose. The young people of the next generation should be possessed of great educa- tional advantages, the utilization of which will do much to lessen the inequality said to exist between this country and others in the struggle for coin- , mercial supremacy..</p>
        <p>This is a frae country, but the freedom nf its inhabitants will soon be a negligible quantity if municipalities go on creating byelaws oi restric iva character at the rata they are doing to-day. There are lots of things a man may not do,which he regu- larly does nevertheless, and it is proposed to pro- hibit another of his pet vices. A letter to the Watch Committee from the Grocers and Provision Dealers Association complains of the damage done to shop fronts by persons striking matches on the , paint and stone work, and the Town Clerk has , been instructed to prepare a byelaw daaling with the practice. The complaint is a reasonable one, but we have very little faith in a byelaw as a means of remedying the evil. We know by personal ex- perience that stone and wood surfaces are admir- able for the purpose of rapidly igniting the hum- ble/lucifer. and it will take a lot to dissuade the ardent smoker from utilising the nearest building to hand. A prohibitive byelaw will be • of little use, for who is to enforce it ? • The police have enough to do without keeping a sharp eye on 75 per cent. of the male population in order to prevent them striking matches where</p>
        <p>they will. We would suggest the more general use of the metal "strikers" occasionally to be seen affixed to paintei wood-work, and which clearly receive considerable attention from the tobacco- cor..-suming fraternity. There is no infallible way of preventing the disfigurement of shop-fronts,except by pulling the shops down, and we do not suppose this will appeal to the shop-keepers. Some of them are great offenders, in another way, them- selves, and if it were possible we should make a point of striking matches on the sides of beef and strings of boots which encroach upon the footpath in many places. A bye-law to prevent a public nuisance of this kind would be of more service than the one already alluded to. Perhaps the Watch Committee will consider the two together.</p>
        <p>There was quite a distinguished gathering in the picturesque grounds of Appleton Hall on Thursday, when there was held the first public meeting in connection with the Warrington Branch of the National Service League. This organisation has the distinction of being the first of its kind to be formed in the United Kingdom and to judge from the efforts which are being put forth in its behalf, it bids fair to become the first in importance as well. The issue which is raised by the league is one which calls for close study before any decisive action is taken. In effect,, the league advocates a modified form of conscription. Every youth, between the ages of eighteen and twenty- two, is to be compelled to give two months' train- ing in a camp of exercise under canvas in ' the first .year, and shorter periods of training in subsequent years, making alto- gether about one hundred days of training. Furthermore, physical training on scientific prin- ciples and military drill are to be made a com- pulsory part of the educational course in public schools. The main' object of these proposals is te provide a national reserve of trained men for de- fensive purposes and thus guarantee the security ;of the British Islands by preventing the possi- bility of raid or invasion. This is a great patriotic ' ideal but some of the methods by which it is hoped to attain it, we feel confident,will not meet with the approval of the majority of Bricishers.</p>
        <p>The mere fact that it is to be carried out under compulsion is repulsive to the average free-born Briton.To be driven to do a duty,whether patriotic or commonplace, goes sadly against the grain. Then it may be very reasonably asked whether the country needs such a vast army of trained men for national defence as would be raised under this compulsory system. It is framed on Swiss linos, but the needs of Switzerland for military defence are altogether different from ours. There they are in danger of an invading force march- ing over the border—a possibility which we do not fear owing to our insular position. Some very hard words were said by Lord Newton against the existing voluntary sys tern in this country. But there are many who are not prepared to admit that it has had a fair chance, • The pay has been inadequate and the conditions of Eervice of such a character as to cause some of the best brain and sinew of the country to hold aloof. The treatment of the auxiliary forces has been something similar. Citizen soldiers have been asked to sacrifice a great deal and then been snubbed for their pains. The whole army system is bound with red tape and is out of touch with the spirit of the times. • Compulsory service is no remedy. Britishers have not lost their sense of the duties and responsibilities of citizenship and are as ready to-day, as ever, to discharge them voluntarily, if the conditions are not made intolerable.</p>
        <p>There is no denying the popularity of the healthful game of bowls in Warrington. Work— ingmen in particular find in it a pleasant recreation after their daily toil in the summer months. The spirit with which the recent works' competitions have been entered into has no doubt beep a revela- tion to matly who have hitherto been disposed to quesbionwhether, after all, Warrington possessed so manyienthusiasts as had been claimed for the sport. The team and individual merit contests have been equally well fought, and the strong muster of spectators at the handicap fihal last Satur- day afforded striking evidence of the interest taken in the doings of the local " trundltrs." Such enthusiasm appears to furnish an argument in favour of the establishment in the town of a municipal bowling green about which such a great deal has been heard. When so many workingmen can be found to patronise the greens already in existence one is naturally led to the conclusion that a Corporation undertaking of this description would here, as in numerous towns, receive ade- quate support. It is to be hoped that the members of the Council will keep the subject before them, and that next season the scheme may have a practical trial.</p>
        <p>That Sherlock Holmes whom the world as well as his biographer, Dr. Watson, believed to be dead should be alive, and in the full possession of those extra ordinary powers of what may be called "de- ductive detectiveness"is,and for a long time will 13:: a matter of supreme delight to the whole English- speaking world of contemporary literature in' which he has so long been a dominant figure. Loud were the expressions of regret at his supposed death. Indeed, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, as his biogragher in chief received many recriminatory letters when he recorded his death. One actually began with so violent an expression "You Beast!' The circumstances which led to the belief of his death, 'is well as those which explain his escape from the murderous attack of Professor Moriarty are fully told in the October number of "The Strand Magazine," which will, therefore, have a more than ordinarily absorbing interest for its readers. • Taat Sherlock should have been living cfor three years with hardly anybody aware of the fact is remarkable. Still more remarkable is his first appearance to his admirers in general, and to Dr. Watson in particular, in the story which tells of his return to London, and the pursuit of his self-imposed calling as the greatest detective of his time; for, incredible though it may seem, Dr. Watson actually sees and converses with him without being aware of his identity. "The Ad- venture of the Empty House," is ibe first of the series which chronicles "the return of Sherlock H(.4rnes," the most famous portrait in Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's long portrait gallery.</p>
        <p>While the "Pall Mall Gazette," as a good Pro- tectionist organ. tears a passion to tatters because Englishmen prefer French to home-made gloves, its Paris correspondent gives the other side of the "As for French houses," it says " nine- tenths of them are furnished by English firms,who are doing a roaring trade in France. One firm alone, which started two years ago with three workmen in their shop, have now no fewer than eighty-five. All this is not likely to please the Feench tradesmen, especially the upholsterers, as out of the four theatres than were renovated this summer three were done by English firms. And so the English craze' which commenced in Paris about five years by the Frenchman wearing clothes cut by a London tailor, and which was predicted to last but a few months, has taken such a sub- stantial footing that it is never likely to cease." Has it occurred to the "Pail Mall" that in a tariff war set up by retaliation the French Government would aim at killing this growing import trade with England ?</p>
        <p>Bradford, according to our Protectionist friends, ought to be bewailing the loss of its ruined in- dustries, whereas, according to the local market reports, quite a satisfactory view is taken of the outlook. Undoubtedly the Bradford trade was heavily hit by the McKinley tariff,and the worsted trade in particular was diverted to the United States. But the handstuff trade has been recover- ing ground year by year, and the present year bids fair to end with a value of over 22,000,000 of woollen stuffs sent into the American market, the largest amount exported since 1897, in which year the record exports under the Wilson Tariff were recorded.</p>
        <p>Even the annual Bankruptcy Returns refuse to yield material tor the Protectionist campaign. Instead of proving that we are insolvent, last year's statistics actually show that the number of failures was 106 fewer than in the previous ye tr. When it is borne in mind that the effect of the war</p>
        <p>expenditilre was undOnbtedly felt in the form of trade depression in the home trade to a greater extent than in 1901, the fact that the distributing trade should have kept up so well is a satisfactory sign. Of course we do not attach to the figures greaterirnportance,than they are worth:but as every trifling item that appears to tell against the con- tinued prosperity of the country is pressed into the service of Protectionist leafieteers,the fact that the home trade is plainly not suffering from depression is not without value in the present controversy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FOUND DEAD.</head>
        <p>FOUND DEAD.</p>
        <p>QANFORD'S POESON for Killing Rats answers well. "After one dressing I found 136 dead neat morn- ing."—J. BLTS% Helsthorpe, Leighton. " I killed 200 rats with Sanford's Polson."-0. Comr, Farmer. Bllesmere. " destroyed 145 rats with it."—O. Virtaorr. Offord.—Send Is Ed to SANFORD &amp; SONS, Sandy St.l3-34. old by Chemists</p>
