      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>EXAMINEE,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>OCUIBBIL 3rd 1903.</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR has THE VOICE FROM spoken,and recognis- SHEFFIELD. ing the fact that the</p>
        <p>country awaited a " lead " he devoted himself entirely to the subject of the hour and endeavoured to supply the deficiency. The general opinion even among his friends, is that he has failed. If they attempted to follow the "lead," there is no telling where they would arrive, but one thing is certain —they would, most of them arrive at different places. Mr. BALFOUR'S " lead " merely makes confusion worse confounded. He asks for 'freedom to negotiate," a newiy-coined phrase which may be as potent to conjure with as that favourite word among Protectionists, Retaliation. Nobody wishes to refuse Mr. BALFOUR the" free- dom to negotiate," but everybody wishes to Heaven he would produce some concrete scheme or policy which could be considered in detail, and accepted or rejected. We feel sorry for the supporters of the Government. They certainly " dunno where they , are," and we question whether even Mr. BALFOUR knows that much as regards himself. 'lf he does he succeeds remarkably well in disguising his know- ledge. He tried hard at Sheffield to be convinc- ing and to establish his position as the head orthe Government with a policy to unfold, but as he -carefully avoided going into details the country is little the wiser and no nearer a conclusion. The Free Traders must be considered an exception. They, at any rate, are convinced of two things, viz., that Mr. BALFourt's policy is really Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S policy, and that Mr. CHAMBER- LAIN'S policy is unquestionably one of retaliatory tariffs and a tax on food. This much seems abundantly clear,and sufficiently tangible to fight against, but for the supporters of the, Government there is very little to hold on to. Mr. BALFOUR is too much a philosopher and too little a business man to be of much use in such a crisis, and even his adriiirers must have despaired after reading the Sheffield speech. They are still without a "lead," a leader, and a policy, and until Mr. CHAMBERLAIN chooses to speak they are likely to remain so.</p>
        <p>IT is clear that tho. THE MACEDONIAN St. James's Hal- PROBLEM. meeting substaptil ally represents th public sentiment in this country without distinction of party. For the old idea, once precious to politicians of the Disraeli school, that the SULTAN can do.no wrong or that, if he does wrong,it is no concern oc ours, we have to read the humane columns of the " Morning Post." The SULTAN as that journal remarks with complacency, " has done his best to restore order in Macedonia." As an euphemism for extermination this may pair</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>• Coming Events. As will be seen from our advertising columns, the Rev. Herbert Dunnico will re-occupy the pulpit at Golborne-street Baptist Church to-morrow (Sunday) when the harvest festival services will be held. As Mr. Drumico has been away for a month the congre- gation will be looking forward'to seeing and hearing their pastor again in his accustomed place. On Thursday evening there will be a public welcome to Mr. and Mrs. Dunnica. Madame Rathbone will be the soloist, and other special musical items will be, rendered.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in connection with St. Peter's Church will be held in the schoolroom, John- street, on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday next. Lady Greenall will open the . sale on Thursday, and on Friday Mrs. P. Rylands will perform the ceremony. The fancy dress ball in aid of the Royal National Lifeboat Institution will be held in Parr Hall on Thursday eyening. It is hoped that the function will be the means of materially increasing Warrington's contribution to this worthy organization. A cake walk competition will be a special attraction. The Warrington Apollo Prize Male Choir will give their annual concert in Parr Hall on Monday even- ing, assisted•by the Apollo Prize Mixed Choir, Miss A. Foster (Soprano), Mr. H. Berrer (tenor), the Sh. Helens ?Opera Company, and Mr. Whit Cunliffe (humorist). An excellent programme has been arranged, and a musical treat is anticipated. The efforts of the Choir will no doubt meet with the generous public support which they undoubtedly merit.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening' a labour demonstration will be held in Parr Hall. The speakers invited are :-- Mr. J. Shackleton (I,P. for Clitheree), Mr. A. Hender- son (M.P. for , Barnard Castle), and Mr. J. Hodge (labour candidate for Gorton), Mr. E. Plinston will preside. _</p>
        <p>- The annual meeting of the Warrington and District Property Owners' Association will be held on Wed- nesday evening in Cairo-street schoolroom. Harvest thanksgiving services will be held in Bold street Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday). Par- ticulars appear in our advertising columns. At Cairo-street Chapel to morrow (Sunday) harvest thanksgiving services will be held. The Rev. Charles Harvey Cook, the newly-appointed pastor will preach. The offertories will be in aid of the District Nursing Association.</p>
        <p>Four grand stage performances of the Gilbert- Sullivan opera, "The Yeomen of the Guard," will be given in Parr Hall on the 21st, 22nd, 23rd, and 24th inst. by members of the Warrington Musical Stniety in aid of the society's funds. Personal.</p>
        <p>Inspector Print, R.S.P.C.A., who came to Warring- ton from London nearly three years ago,has this week lea the town to take up duties at• Lancaster. During his residence in Warrington Inspector Print has proved. himself a zealous and efficient officer of a worthy organization. , The presidinc• Bishop of the Reformed Church of England, will° hold an Ordination Service at Emmanuel Church, Bold-street next Friday evening, • when Mr. Joseph Callon will be ordained to the ministry. Friends of Mr. Callon. will be pleased to hear of his advancement this far in tile calling he 11• 0 chosen—that of the Mission Field. He will tat, inately proceed to the Refokmed Church Missioicri°L" in the Punjab, India. The Rev. Harvey Cook, the new pastor of Ceir°- street Chapel, will take up his duties on SundaYs November Ist. Forthcoming Mayoral " At Home." The Mayor and Mayoress will be" At home" at the Parr Had on October 24th when opportunity will he taken to unveil the bust of the King which his worshiP has presented to the town, The Mayoress will per- form the interesting ceremony. The bust will be ell exhibition at the Parr Hall on the two succeeding days- • Screen at the Parish Church. The Colonel of the Ist Battalion of the South • Lancashire Regiment has informed the. Rector that the carved oak screen which the officers are proposing ' to erect at the west end of the Boteler Chapel is approaching completion. "It is hoped" says the "Parish Magazine" "that the Earl of Derby may be . ' able to unveil the screen and the Lord Bishop of the : Diocese give the address. The day in all probability will be Saturday, November 7th, but it may be the 14th or 21st, at three o'clock in the afternoon." Chemical Fight with Foreigners. I On Thursday the report and balance sheet were ' issued of the Electrolytic Alkali Companv,Middlewich No dividend is declared on either ordinary or pre-' ference shares. The year's profit of ;£483 is carried forward. The report says the result is largely ac'-' ' counted for by the conflict between foreign and English ' manufacturers of bleaching powder, resulting in a great depression of prices. Those familiar with tile situation would be somewhat gratified that no loss of capital had been incuited. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOM—Advt, Electric Tramway Scheme for Cheshire. A huge scheme has been promotedifor the supply Of ' electricity and power gas throughout Cheshire. 1.,11 conjunction with the scheme it is intended to apPIY for powers to construct an electric tramway connect; ing Macclesfield on the one side with Hazel Grove awl Stockport to Manchester, and from Macclesfield t° Cougleton, Sandbach, Crewe, and Nantwich, with ll branch line from Macclesfield through Broken Cos°, Henbury, Chelford, Knutsford, to Warrington. The scheme will comprise tramways to oDen out inter- communication with all the towns in Cheshire. A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Adv,t, A Drowning Mystery. • Yesterday afternoon Coroner Brighouse held ail inquest at the Police Station touching the death of Albert Rotherham, captain of the barge "Laurel "a°' son of Joseph Rotherham, 80, Widnes-row, Siinres Cross, Widnes, whose body was recovered from the Mersey at Bishop's Wharf on Wednesday morning.--; It appeared from the evidence that deceased was las' seen alive at 10 45 p.m. on Saturday when Rober IRowlinson. mate on tlte barge, saw him drunk ill Mersey-street. The body was recovered from theriver by a labourer named. Clare.—The jury yeturneti a verdict of" Found rowned." Bowling Main. A very sucCessfUl main was held by the employee..s onhe Castle Rubber Co. Ltd. on Saturday th019th.,,,10:" I about 35 driving to the Plough Hotel Lvmm. . .-1-11. 'main resulted as follows : Ist T. O'Brien, 2nd ".,:" ' Cosgrove, 3rd E. Lawton, 4th D. Lancashire, sth, ..'" , - Gorst, 6th A. Field, 7th A. I.3radsliaw,Bth F. Scott. The prizes'were presented:at a smoking concert held at the Norton Arms Hotel on Friday evening 26th nit. Mr* Peter Date (manager of the firm) took the chair.aucl in the course of his remarks he assured the workniell Ihow pleased he was to be amongst them on such all occasion, and he sincerely hoped that that was the first of many gatherings which would be held in the future.Sone.s werq given by Messrs P. Bate,J. McCileti F. Scott,ll7 Rae, J. Eaton, P. Green,A. F. Burdett,o°- D. Carrolland selections on the Gramophone by Mr-1' Green. The duties of accompanist were ably under- taken by Mr. A. Smith. Municipal Elections. A meeting of the Liberal Committee of Whitecrolgs Ward Was held last evening in the new Workingmen Mission Room, Thewlis-street. The business was I' 1 selection of a candidate for the forthcoming municiPA, the election. After discussion it was unanimously decidiecle to invite Councillor Barton to again stand ag ll candidate. Councillor Barton consented to do so, tirL his decision was received with enthusiasm. .interesting address on municipal matters 1% delivered by Councillor Evans, who made specA reference to the river proposals of the Ship Car' Company.</p>
      </div>
