      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <p>-'Councillor Thornton's Incubator. Councillor Thornton has presented to the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Commttee an incubator and foster- mother, for which the committee has returned thanks Wirral and Birkenhead Agricultural Society. The Council has decided to hold the next 'annual show in the Permanent Show-yard at Bebington on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, 14th, 15th and 16th June, 1904. School Holidays. The elementary schools in the borough were closed on Thursday and yesterday owing to the completion of the school year.,, FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try a. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Accident to Mr. D. Davies. We l'egret to learn that Mr. D. Davies, one of the oldest and most highly esteemed members of the Warrington Cycling Club, met with a serious accident Whilst cycling at Acton Bridge on Sunday night., his collar bone being broken, At a meeting of the Lifeboat Saturday Committee, of which Mr. Davies is the chairman, held last evening sym- pathetic reference was made' to the occurrance, and the hope was expressed that he would speedily re- cover. The hope, we are sure, will be joined in by Mr, Davies's large circle of friends and acquaintances. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—AcIYt- Ib. H. Roberts and Occasional Licences. At the County Borough Police Court yesterday, Mr. Chalmers, landlord of the Pelican'Hotel applied for an occasional licence to sell at Parr Hall from S p.m. on Thursday next to 2 a.m. on Friday on the occasion 4cf the fancy dress ball in aid of the Royal National 'lifeboat Institution.—The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) remarked that personally he thought these applica- tions were becoming too numerous. It seemed that if there was anything in the shape of a Ance, whether the object was charitable or otherwise, there must be _ something of this kind. The Bench would grant the licence from 8 p.m. to 1 a.m.—At a meeting of the k Lifeboat Saturday Committee, held last evening, refer-, • ence was made to Mr. Roberts's remarks, and the • opinion was expressed that they were uttered ' :under a misapprehension of the facts. The decision to apply for a licence was, not arrived at without full consideration on the part •of the committee and it was with a view to pre- 'venting the introduction of intoxicating liquors into the hall from outside that the application -Was made. ASK for CONNMIHAM'S HIGH-CLAS AIINERAL WATERS.—Advt. • Lecture to Oddfellows. The first monthly meeting for he winter session in connection with the Grand Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity) was held on Wednesday evening, P.G. Thomas Healing presiding over a good muster of brethren. After the usual routine business had been transacted a lecture, illus- • trate(' with limelight views, was given by P.G. Thornby, of the Constitution Lodge. The subject' Was" A tour round the north east Coast of Ireland' and the lecture was listened to with very great interest. The lecturer was enabled to show by means of some 70 or 80 slides taken from his own photographs that the country from Dundalk to Giants causeway was an ideal place to spend' an interesting -and instructive holiday. At the close of the meeting • the president moved a hearty vote of thanks to the lecturer. P. G. Spann seconded and the vote was supported by D.G.M. H. R. Holford, P.P.G.M. T. S. P.P.G.M. S. Baldry, P.P.G. M. Massey, L.G.M. • -T. B. Bradshaw and others and heartily carried. •. - FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt.. Presentation to a Warrington Organist. Mr. J. Broadbent, who for two years has officiated as organist at the Church of the Sacred Heart was on Sunday afternoon presented With a handsome marble clock in appreciation of his services. Mr. Broadbent relinquished the position on account of 'having to leave the. town.—Mr. J. Gibbins, the -choirmaster, said he was voicing the feelings of their worthy Rector and the choir when he said he had a great regard for Mr. Broadbent. He had always done his best for the choir and the church and they were k. very sorry to lose him. (Hear, hear.)—The Rev. Father' Ryan, who presided, then made the presenta- tion, and expressed the regret all felt at Mr. Broad- bent's retirement, He could not speak too highly of his efforts for the benefit of the choir, and his genial- ity, affability, and gentleness had endeared him to all ~of them. (Applause.)—Mr. Broadbent, in reply . thanked the choir very heartily. It had been -pleasure to work with them, and he had been glad to no anything he could. He would look upon the time he had spent at the church as the happiest in his life. —Mr. G. Coggins also spoke in appreciation of Mr. Broadbent, and of the way he and the choirmaster had worked together for the benefit of the members. PLTBI IC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK BEST for BOC Sin LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Appleton Rifle Club. The last Competitions for the year were held on Saturday last, the 26th September. There was a very fair attendance of the'anembers, the attraction for the day being the competition for the A.R.C. medal. This Was wonbySergeant-Major Rawlinson,Mr. SegarOwen, being 2nd and Captain Dutton Fairclough 3rd. In the Open sweepstake Sergeant-Major Rawlinson was again -first, Mr. Sear Owen being second, and Mr. T. Daintith third-. In the members stakes Mr. Arthur 'Down took first place. With the above competitions the club closes this year's shooting. During the year 5 monthly competitions have been held, and we are 'pleased to say that those members who have attended have shown a marked improvement in their scores Over last year. It is perhaps not sufficiently known that owing to the kindness of Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon there is such a splendid miniature rifle range as that at Appleton, and within easy reach of this district. Each Thursday and each Saturday throughout the Season (May to September) markers are in attendance at the rwige:, also an'instructor, so that anyone wish- to use the most powerful rifle can fire his rounds -with perfect safety. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL. Business of the Next Meeting</head>
        <p>THE TOWN COUNCIL. Business of the Next Meeting</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening when the following, among 'Other business, will be transacted.</p>
        <p>• MORE 'QUESTIONS BY COUNCILLOR LORD. Councillor Lard has givennotice to ask the Chatrman 'Of the Water Committee the following questions, at t-41!e meeting of the Council to be held on Tuesday night, Were tenders for the water level indicators adver- used for in accordance with the Standing Orders? 4. If so, in which newsapers did the advertisements 4PDear and on what date or dates? 3. How many firms tendered, and what were the anlounts. • _ _ '</p>
        <p>4, Date of contract, and when did the' work com- ruence'' 5. What firm secured the order? 6. Is it not a fact that the poles carrying the elec- ,,ric wires were not even unbarked and creosoted or otherwise prepared prior to being fixed in the ground? 7. Did the Water Engineer certify the work as being atisfactory prior to any payments being made on C:C01111t ?</p>
        <p>8. Was each payment on account made in accordance "vith certificate given by the Water Engineer ? • lIIISIGNNTION OF MR. DEAS AS SANITARY ENGINEER. The Sanitary works committee met at the Town Hall fl September 22nd proceeded to Longford Depot, and '''",de an inspection of the works, after which a dis- ssion took place in the office, when it was resolved, at owing to the increase of the work in this depart- hl ent it is, in the opinion of this Committee, necessary y-that the manager should devote the whole of his time to the duties of the office. :stOn the 30th September, 1903, a letter was read from sr..Deas resigning his position as Manager of the a nitaryWorks Dspartmeht.. It was resolved that the l'esignation be accepted to take effect on the 24th December neat.</p>
        <p>LONDON-ROAD IMPROVEMENT. s The e Town Clerk reports that the Property Purchasing 11Q-Committee had come to terms with the owners of veral properties in Old London-road, but that negoti- tions with other owners had not been concludsd.</p>
        <p>THE NEW STREET COMING UP AGAIN. ~The General Purposes Committee has resolved that Jie proposal contained in the following resolution of ule Street Improvement Committee be approved :—A (1)0sal from Mr, R. E. Garnett to add 20 feet in idth co-extensive with his premises to the proposed it'asklgo behind the new premises in Bridge-street, and 2.give the scheduled property belonging to him, con- rininF an area of 316 square yarda, in .exchange for the siqlul(lareiny yards the. Eagle and Child-yard, containing 531</p>
        <p>TRAMWAYS EXTENSION. COllll6llol' Cntik has a specisi motion on the agenda 'or the extention of tramway No. 6 to the finger-post oPPosite the smithy in Padgate-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT RUNCORN RAILWAY STATION,</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening the lamproom at Runcorn Railway Station was discovered to be on fire. There being plenty of "fire buckets "handy the flames were quickly extinguished, but the room was considerably damaged. A smashed lamp was found on the floor, and it iksupposedthat this had exploded in some way. CHARGE OF ATTEMPTED THEFT.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Thursday, Harry Caffrey, described as a notorious thief; who has served several long sentences, was charged with attempting to steal Ms. from the till of the Fleetwood Arms, the property of Michael Con- way. The alarm was given by the barmaid, Clara Hodgett,and he was arrested by Detective Mottershead. He was remanded. SERIOUS ACCIDENT AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning a young man named Moses Cogley, residing in Midwood-street, met with severe injuries through falling from a steam crane at the Widnes Foundry. Cogley had just commenced his work when he fell a distance of about 201 t. When picked up he was found to be suffering from a fractured leg and a nasty cut on the forehead. Dr. Edwards was quickly summoned to the scene,and Cogley was removed to the Royal jnfirmary. CRUELTY TO A MARE. _</p>
        <p>At Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Harry Berrington, Overton, was summoned in two cases for illtreating a mare on September 16th by working it while in an unfit condition. He pleaded guilty.— Inspector Blake Jones, of the R.S.P.C.A., said the mare had a large suppurating wound on the near shoulder.— A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.—A charge against defendant's son, Thomas Berrington, of working the mare was withdrawn on payment of the costs. WIDNES FOREMAN'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones, deputy coroner, held an inquest on Mon- day afternoon at the Blackburn Arms, Ditton, touching the death of Tom Greenwood, who met with fatal injuries at the Calder and Mersey Extract Works, Ditton, on Saturday. William Maloney, Liverpool, said that a log of wood, weighing one ton, was being lowered from a crane on to a bogie. Greenwood appeared to slip, and the log, which had slipped away from the crane, fell upon him, killing him almost instantly. Thomas Littler said that he was in the act of steadying the loc, onto the bogie when the thin end of the log struck the bogie and loosened the hold of the chain. When that slipped the log tilted over and fell on deceased.—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned. RUNCORN WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The Urban and Rural District Councils of Runcorn not having come to terms for the supply of the town, ships of Halton and Walton, have agreed to the appoint- ment of an arbitrator to fix thl price at which the- Rural Council shall purchase the water-mains and apparatus in the townships. On Tuesday the Urban Council spent two hours discussing alternative terms from the Rural Council, the chief being that the two authorities shall combine to take 150,000 gallons each per day from the Liverpool Vyrnwy mains. The water question also aiose at the meeting of Halton Parochial Committee on Tuesday. It was alleged that the Rural Council had no mandate from Halton to involve the township in an expenditure of £5,000 to £lO,OOO in pro- viding a water supply, and a resolution was passed requesting the council to submit all information. ATTACK ON A WIDNES_ POLICEMAN. _</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Widnes Police Court, before Mr. J. S. Barrett and Alderman J. W. Wareing.—A young fellow, named William Hackett, was charged with being drunk and also with assaulting P.C. Parr, and doing damage to his uniform to the extent of 12s. 9d. He pleaded not guilty to the assault. Ellen Kelly was charged with also assaulting the constable, and pleaded not guilty.— The officer stated that on Monday evening the prisoners and several others were creating a disturbance in Well- ington-street. After being spoken to he took the male prisoner into custody, and he became very violent. They struggled on the ground, and the prisoner kicked him on the ear and ribs. While on the ground the female prisoner struck him, and others also' set upon him. With the assistance of two civilians the prisoners were locked up—Hackett was fined 20s. and costs or one month for the assault,and is. and 12s. 9d. fordamage to the uniform. The case of drunkenness was withdrawn. —Kelly was fined 2s. 6cl. and costs, or seven days for the assault.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TRADESMEN SUMMONED FOR. _OBSTRUCTION.</head>
        <p>TRADESMEN SUMMONED FOR. _OBSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>At Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday, Ernest S. Collinson, grocer,' Church-street, Frodsham, was summoned for having on September.l2th obstructed the footpath in front of his premises by placing certain boxes there contrary to Section 28 of the Towns Police Clauses Act, 1847. He pleaded not guilty. Sergeant Bratt said that on the date mentioned he saw a number of boxes in front of defendant's shop. They protruded two feet six inches on the footpath. The goods in the boxes were on exhibition. There had been several com- plaints about people having to leave the footpath on account of the obstruction. He warned the defendant a week before he took these proceedings. Defendant said there was a step 28 inches in front of the shop. When goods were delivered from the lurry they had to remain on the step until he had time to take them in. There was no other way of delivering the goods. Sergeant Bratt said the boxes were placed on the footpath for show purposes, and remained there all day. Defendant was summoned for asimilryr offence on September 19th. P. C. Fry said there were 31 boxes and barrels in front of the shop that day inspector Blake Jones corroborated.—John Gilmour Kvdd grocer, Church-street,was summoned for a similar offence. P.C. Fry said he was past the shop on Septem- ber 18th and saw several boxes arida barrel in front of the premises. Defendant had been cautioned. The boxes projected two and a half inches over the step. In answer to Mr. Linaker (Runcorn), who appeared for the defendant, the witness said the step had been used for this purpose for some years, but not so much as recently. If the goods were on the step there was no obstruction to people on the footpath. Mr. Linaker said defendant had ben in the habit of using the step, and there had been no dedication to the public of that portion of the footpath. The original land extended beyond the step, but a certain portion of the footpath had been given to the public.—The Chairman said there had been a technical breach of the law, and defendants would be fined 6d. and costs in each case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>MINEN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Sr. HELENS HOTEL INCIDENT.</head>
        <p>Sr. HELENS HOTEL INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>At Stnielens, on Saturday, a labourer ,named Gilles- pet Sherlock Wray, lodging in Cropper's-hill, was sent to prison for six months for stealing .125 from the Lord Nelson Hotel, where he had gone to sell some goods. ST. HELENS STORES BROKEN INTO. _ _</p>
        <p>The Pe;:Sley—Cross branch of the St. Helens Co- operative Stores was entered on Tuesday night by means of , a trap door at the rear being forced, the door into the shop also being broken into. About a dozen pounds of tobacco were missing. The thieves apparently had little reward for their pains. The New- town stores and the stores at Blackbrook have during the past month or two been entered precisely in the slime way. A BRUTXL Sr. HELENS SON.</p>
        <p>James Allender, an athletic young man, of Phythian- street, was on Monday charged at the St. Helens Police Court by Police-constable Duggan with a breach of the peace, and also with assaulting his mother. Allender had his arm in a sling, and the officer said he had cut it badly when he broke all the glass in the window of his sister's house. The arm had been stitched. Duggan said he locked Allender up for fear he would kill some- body. The man's mother limped into the box, and said he had nearly killed her. Allender pleaded for leniency, but the Chief Constable said the man had a bad record. Ten years ago, he was sent to the sessions for trial for Ncounding his mother, and was sent to gaol for nine months. Allender was sentenced to two months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>O.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE. The weekly meeting-was held on Monday, Sept. 28th. A capital programme was given by metnbers of P.M:C.E. and other friends each item being nicely rendered and tvell received. Mr. J. Woods presided and gave a short address. Songs were rendered by Miss J. Hill, Miss' L. Stronach and Mrs. Williams; recitations, by Mips Newton; readings, by Mr. G. Clarke ; mandoline selection, by Mr. J. Baxter and gramophone selections, by Mr. Porter. Votes of thanks were heartily accorded the visitors and• the meeting closed with the Doxology. EARt:ESTOWN AND DISTRICT P. S. A. The opening services of the above were held in the Central Co-operative Hall, on Sunday afternoon, when Mr. John Jackson presided over a good audience. The report of the past Session was given by Mr. T. Willamson,Secretary,showing that the interest of themem- bers was well-maintained. Attendances' werequite equal to previous sessions ; there had been a larger number of subscribing members, and a corresponding increase in the number of books distributed. There was a good balance in hand, and the prospects for the coming session were very favouitible indeed. After a few re- marks from the chairman, the report was adopted, and the following ofticers.a.nd Committee were elected to serve for the session now commencing. President, Mr. Thomas Ormand ; Vice president, Mr. E. J. George, and Mr. George Dring ; Hon secretary and treasurer, Mr. Thomas Williamson • Committee, Mr. John Jackson, (Chairman) Messrs Joseph Smith,James Hatton, Robert Maclin, William Wilson, James Knowles, John Eachus, and A. Lumb; Assistant secretary, Mr. Harold Roberts, Registrars, Messrs C. Wyatt, and .John F. Singleton ; Pianist, Miss Williamson. Cordial thanks were accorded the officers and Committee for their excellent services of the past year, on the motion of Mr. 'George Clarke who spoke very highly of the good tone that had been well sustained, and of the inspiring meetings held; all had worked harmoniouslyand zealously to bring about such good results, but special reference must be made to the secretary Mr. T. Williamson and Registrars Mr, C. Wyatt, and Mr. John F. Singleton. The musical arrangements of the last session had . been of a high order, excellent service being rendered by an efficient choir under the leadership and guidance of Mr. John Potter musical director, and Miss Williamson pianist, whose services could not be too highly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 3, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 3, 1903.</p>
        <p>THE FISCAL PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>THE PREMIER'S " ECONOMIC NOTES ON INSULAR FREE TRADE."</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER writes :</p>
        <p>I have been much interested in 'reading Mr. Balfour's pamphlet manifesto with the above title. To my mind the value of the !paper is enhanced by . this, although marred by errors inlfact,that the writer goes much deeper into the heart of thesubject than ordinary. Protection- ists. The practical value of the fiessay is however weakened by • the fact that it largely deals with .hypo- thetical conditions,the imaginary position,of an imagin- ary island.</p>
        <p>take the following sentence, "If the Free Trade country is large enough, and varied enough, the open markets within its own territory may, as we have just seen, ba ufficient. They will not be sufficient if the character and capibilities of the country are limited in such a way that, while large imports are a vital neces- sity, the exports required to pay for them are not of a kind which other nations—all by hypithesis protection- ist-7-are obliged .to take." . .</p>
        <p>This sentence like many others is dominated by its "ifs "and its hypotheses. But my object in quoting this sentence is to show that the writer realizes, what very few Protectionists do, (least of all those who have written to the Warringbon papers) that the vital thing in national trading is the fact that " large imports are a vital necessity," and that the exports are the things that pay for them. What is•.true in individual dealings is just as true in nationalaffairs. A workman's labour is his export, the wage, the return for his labour, is his import. If a workman by working less, could get the same remuneration, he saves the amount of labour, he exports less and gains thereby, or to put it the other way, if by the same labour, or export, he can increase his wage or import, surely he ought not to grumble, but rejoice in his increasing import. The labour is not an end itself, but a means to an end. In like manner, the imports a nation receives in payment for goods exchanged, or services rendered, are the all important consideration, and exports ase only valuable in so far as they secure abundant imports. And yet, strange to say, most Protectionist writers, instead of looking at increasing imports as healthy signs, appear to view themas a growing calamity, and • would rejoice to see us sending more out of the country than we receive into it. Of course I am well aware that if excessive imports were the result of our running into debt, and mortgaging our assets to foreign countries, then such would be a bad sign, but there is no evidence of this, indeed the evidence of increasing national wealth is all the other way. Mr. Balfour says himself "I can find no evidence, that we are living on our capital." The blue book just published by the Board of Trade confirms this.</p>
        <p>We have to thank Mr. Balfour for emphasizing the factthat the danger, if it exists at mil, is in the curtail- ment of imports resulting from lessened exports. Mr. Balfour himself bears witness to the continued pros- perity of England when he says :—"We imagined a Free Trade country completely environed by a wall of pro- tection, a wall high enough to make exports first difficult and then unattainable. We inferred that it would find imports first costly and then unattainable. In actual fact we see Britain hampered indeed by Foreign tariffs, yet able in spite of these to carry on an export trade which, if it does not inerease as we might wish, yet increases rather than diminishes, and an im- port trade of une,rampled magnitude." Mr. Balfour might have added that per head of the population we export nearly as much as the two largest exporting countries, and import considerably more, and further that our imports show a healthy increase.</p>
        <p>.. But Mr. Balfour in a closer examination of our ex- ports is not satisfied and says "If we exclude coal from the sum of our exports—still more if we alsO exclude I machinery, there are signs not only of a diminution' relative to population, but of a diminution absolute." i This is not accurate because it only takes cognisance of visible exports. But the invisible exports such as i freights show a larger increase than any other nation Why exclude either coals or machinery? If we exclude, such things from our exports,it will be necessary toex- elude like things from theexportsoft countries with which we make comparisons. Thus America's largest export to Canada is coal, amounting annually to about £2,000,000. One of America's largest exports to this country of manufactured goods is in the form of machinery, and yet I have not noticed any protectionist writer in comparing the two countries eliminating these things from any ', comparison, although frequently they deduct coal from English exports. Mr. Balfour's reason for eliminating coal is not the ordinary one that it is a " raw material." He truly says " much labour and capital has to be expended before what is stored in the seam becomes fit for the furnace" but he objects to this export because it fosters the manufacturing interests of other countries. If there is ground for dissatisfaction in regard to this, how great should be our satisfaction in our vast imports to aid our manufacturers which come in freely from other countries and how chary we should be of putting on 1 protective tariffs to impede these imports, be they ' sugar, iron, leather or anything else. Mr. Balfour says 'large imports are a vital necessity" and it seems a curious method of maintaining these to threaten to huild up tariff walls to keep them out. But it may be replied, that the intention is to put tariffs on certain goods with the object of afterwards taking them off when theyhave accomplished their purpose of forcing a competing country to lower their duty on what we want to export. This then is the fiscal war Mr. Balfour seeks to inaugurate. I call it a fiscal Nvar,some may prefer to call it" negotiations," but negotiations will not carry ' us far, any more than they did in the case of the Boers, We must not try the game of bluff as we did in the case of President Kruger, but be prepared to strike, to retaliate, to hit harder than the other fellow, and must count the full cost before we enter into a commercial war policy which may strain the country more than did the Boer war. The Government entered the South African struggle with a light heart, counted not the i cost, engaged n it with no appreciation of its serious- ness, and no adequate means to carry it through. As we know they muddled through somehow. Let us count well the cost before we enter into a tariff war, in which, while we may be able to hurt other nations we may hurt ourselves much more. The general history of retaliating tariffs has been to leave permanent tariffs between the two conflicting countries, and this is not to be wondered, at. .If you give a vested interest to industries by protective tariffs, if you give to an arbitrarily selected number of trades the luxury of taxing their own fellow countrymen—for this is what ithey seek to make themselves rich—as soon as there is a I prospect of the monoply being withdrawn, these ' favoured industries will fight for all they are worth to maintain their privileges. The country will be snowed up with pamphlets emanating from subsidized fighting trade associations; lecturers will cover the country, the motto of these trades will be borrowed from I another trade that is dependant on legisla- tive action, and will be "Our trade, our ' politics." Statesmen and governments will in this as in other countries have to bend or break before the vested interests they themselves have created, and the sufferers, the consumers, the people, as a whole, sleepy, divided about many things, scarcely conscious of the wrong that is being done will, as in all protected countries, be beaten by'the disciplined forces of the various vested:interests, united by the cry " Our trade in danger." And the Governments in both countries who may in good faith put on the tariffs with the idea that retaliation would bring about Free Trade, find themselves powerless to take the off again. The residuary legatee of retaliation is always permanent Pro- tection. It is well therefore that we should understand where we are. I have no wish to disparage the amiable Free Trade sentiments of Mr. Balfour, bat under him the Government is surely tending towards Protection. Mr. Arthur Elliott who has just resigned his position , of Financial Secretary, says he cannot remain longer in a G overnment "which is steadily tending to Protec- tion." .</p>
        <p>We d 0 not know how much faith to put in the ro- bustness of the Free Trade sentiments of Mr. Balfour, but of this we may rest assured, that once he starts es- tablishing Protection and thus endowing particular industries he will, and the country will, find their dis- establishment and disendowment an almost impos- sible task. Like Sinbad the Sailor whose kindness prompted him to Carr the little old man over the stream, they will find that it is afterwards impossible to shake off the incubus. The only hope is that the people who will have to pay for the enrichment of the few mayrealize the danger of this Class endowment here and now,and show at the ballot box that this thing shall not be.</p>
        <p>T do not wish anyone to infer from above that I am against all governmental action in the development and defence of British industry—in this direction very much can be done. We will wait with interest to see what definite plan Mr. Balfour has(to put before the country. But no scheme which impoverishes the many for the sake of the few ought to teceive the nation's su ppo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
