      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS AND BOWLING. Distribution of Prizes.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS AND BOWLING. Distribution of Prizes.</p>
        <p>MR. A, H. CR3SFIELD AND HIS CRITICS.</p>
        <p>The distribution-of the cup,medal,and prizes to the suc- cessful competitors in connection with the first annual bowling handicap of the "Prince of Wales" Lodge of Oddfellows took place at the " Pelican " Hotel on , Thursday evening. The chairman of the evening was Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, and the presentation was made by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, the donor of the cup and medal. Needless to say Mr. Crosfield (who is an Oddfellow) was received with loud cheering on entering. The room had been tastefully decorated for the oestasion by the proprietor,Mr. W. Chalmers. Therommittee had spared no pains to make the event a memorable one in connec- tion with their lodge. The musical pragramme was splendidly arranged, the artistes being Messrs. W. H. Ireland, J. Atherton, J. Willett, T. Healing, and others, and . every song was well sung and received the applause., it richly deserved. In addttion to prizes given by members of the com- mittee, awards were presented by the following:— Messrs J. Earle, Caldw.ll's Ltd., W. Chalmers, C. Moundeld, J. Hall, J. Wrench, T. Davies, H. Singleton, J. Banks, and J. Stanway. The result of the competi- tion which took place on the 22nd August has already appeared in our columns,and the 16 winners attended to receive their prizes, which were as follows:- 1, W. Jones, the "Crosfield" Cup and medal 2, J. Atkinson, barometer 3, .1. W. Elliott, copper kettle 4. F. Wisedale, copper kettle</p>
        <p>5, G. P; Freeman, box of cigars 6, E. Haigh, silver-mounted stick 7, J. Wrench, bat 9, A. Cooper, rug 9, T. Boardman, silver match box 10 J. Wilkins silver cigar holder</p>
        <p>11, J. Willett, value 55.- 12, R. W. Cook, value ss. 13, T. Rigby. leg of mutton 14, H. Powell, hat 15, S. Wych, pipe and case</p>
        <p>16, H. Heys, pipe and case The usual loyal toasts having been drunk and honoured</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN extended a cordial welcome to Mr. Crosfield and pointed out that Mr. Crosfield had treated the lodge handsomely in providing a cup and medal for the bowling competition. The competition was a dis- tinct success and he wished to take that opportunity of thanking the committee for the services rendered. The Chairman also paid a tribute to the services of Mr. Freeman who proved himself the right man in the right place. (Hear, hear.) Mr. A. H. CHOSFIELD, who on rising to distribute the prizes was received with the singing of "For he's a jolly good fellow," thanked the members for their hearty and kind welcome. He was extremely glad that this bowline, competition had been such a success, and he might add, to sea the progress which the game was making all round the town. They had got a very ancient prece dent for their proceedings that evening. Amongst the ancient Greeks it was customary to make music and athletics a prominent part of the education of every child in the state. (Hear, hear.) He imagined that bowls had this in common with golf that the game was rather an exacting mistress. To the losers in the competition he would commend the • remark that was made many years ago at Walton Lea by RidhardCobden to his (Mr. Crosfleld's) uncle George, who was able to give Cobden some assist- ance in the early days of his campaign. Perhaps it might interest some of them to know that many years ago his uncle and father were able to be of some assistance to Richard Cobden and in close touch with him and John Bright in those days. One evening at Walton Lea when Cobden and his (Mr. Crosfleld's) uncle had thrashed out their political business they had a game Of billiards together. His uncle—Ur. George Crosfield was rather useful at the game. (Laughter). It. ran a little in the family—(laughter)—and his uncle gave Richard Cobden a severe hammering. (Laughter,) When the game Was over. Cobden turned to him and said "Ah Crosfleld what a deal of time you must have wasted on this game." (Loud laughter). If he (Mr. Crosfield) was not much of a hand at bowls it was not because he had not been acquainted with the game for a good many years. He might say his acquaintance with bowls I dated back 30 years because when he lived at Lymm he had to go with his father to a neighbouring friend's house and he was employed to carry the footer. (Laughter.) They would therefore see that he was initiated at quite an early age into the mysteries of those recondite questions of lengtir and land. It was from that time that he dated the special and pecular interest which in another field of life it had,since been his duty to take in what was called the land question. (Laughter and applause.) He supposed it was 20 years ago since he first tried to encourage howling amongst the workmen at Bank Quay and since that time—he supposed about a dozen years ago—he!hadtried to extend bowling amongst other work men, outside. (Hear, bear.) Although it was not much that he had been able to do, still it had always been an intense satisfaotiou to him to be able to encour- age this game of bowls, than whichhe ventured to say,no other kind of recreation could be healthier more pleasur- able or better suited to fill up those long summer evenings or in3n who had the n tra daily wark to do in this busy work-a-day world. Although some good people had been kind enough te offer some littlAcriticisna about the encouragement that he had given to bowls for quite a number of years past, he could only tell them that he did not care one brass farthing for those criti- cisms—(applause)—and he hoped to be able to assist in smallway to maintain the prominent position which Warrington held in the bowling world bras many years to come, and he hoped more. (Applause). They had not yet reached the time when as John Burns hoped there would be"leisure,pleasu re and treasure" for every- body. But by degrees as sbill and science increased man's power over the natural forces of the world, by degrees we should make still greater progress in that direction. It was an evidence of a form of advanoing civilization, in which in their own proper place—not as an end in themselves but as a means to an end—emines had an important, a useful and a pleasing part. Hear, hear.) They helped one, if he might so express it (to quote the words of a great American—Emerson) to keep himself in what he called"the top of his condition,"and they must not tell him (Mr. Crosfield) that anyone of them, in whatever field of work their lot was cast, would do that work any the worse for a certain amount of time given to open-air games of this sort. (Hear' hear.) On the contrary they would do that work all the better for keeping themselves in good trim and they would be able in all ranks of life to fulfil the all-round duties of citi zensh ( Applause.) Mr.lW ‘fiswowrx proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield for the cun and medal he had presented, and also a similar acknawledgment of the gifts 'of other donors. He pointed out that the lodge was paying £3OO a year in sick benefits, and had an income of £BOO, , It was utterly impossible for the lodge, to use any ' portion of the funds for such a competition as that which had just been held. The winner of the cup was the representative of the third generation of a family who had become members of the lodge. (Hear, hear.) Mr. REnutsff seconded the vote of thanks and announced that Mr. Crosfield had offered to give a gold medal with the•cup next year. (Applause). The vote was carried with acclamation. _</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSPIELD, in reponding, referred to the National Service question, a report of which will be found in another eelumn.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks to the o'ficials for the carrying out of the arrang.ements and also to the pianist and artistes concluded a verT enjoyable,evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PAROCHTAL CommrTTEE.—At a meeting of this Com- mittee held on Thursday evening the tender of Mr. Miller to carry out the nightsoil contract was accepted.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. INTERESTING BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Parish Council washeld on Thursday evening when there were present :—Messrs. Clarke (presiding), Whitley, Bouchier, INicTntyre, Fairhurst, and Bradshaw (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>TIIE TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>TIIE TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Cheshire County Council makingcertain objections to the proposed War- rington Corporation Tramway to Stockton Heath. After sornediscussion it waS decided that the whole question was settled so far as the Parish Council and the Corporation were concerned and the Parish Council had nothing to do with the objections. A report was presented by the sub-committee con- sistin of Mr. Clarke, Mr. Bouchier and the Clerk, which was appointed to wait upon the Corporation respecting the proposed tramway. The committee, it was stated, was met by the Corporation in a very reasonable manner, and the suggestions which they made were likely to be entertained.</p>
        <p>MORE _COLTS( ILLORS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Cheshire City Council wrote stating that the authority had decided to grant the Parish Council's application for an increase in the number Of Parish Councillors from seven to twelve.</p>
        <p>EDITCATIONAL MATTERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke referred to the subject of education and especially to secondary education. He thought the children of Stockton Heath should have the advantage of the Secondary schools which mig.llt be erected in Warrington in the future and moved that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Director of Educa- tion to the Cheshire County Council suggesting that he should make arrangements with the Warrington Comore: tion to pay for any children going from Stockton Heath to Warrington for secondary education.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bouchier seconded the motion which was carried. Mr. Rouchier' as the representative of the Council on the Stockton Heath School Management Committee reported that the question of providing better school accommodation was being considered by the proper authorities and steps were being taken to furnish the requisite accommodation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>MEETING AT APPLETON HALL.</p>
        <p>Speeches by Lord Newton, Earl Egerton of Tatton, Mr. A. H. Crosfield</p>
        <p>and Others.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Warrington Branch of the National Service League, a meeting and garden fete were held at Appleton Hall (by the kindness of Mr. T. H. Lyon) on Thursday evening. The weather was very unfavourable' but fortunately marque 4 had been erected and this gave shelter to the large company that responded to the invitation of the President (Mr. Lyon). The motto of the league was emblazoned on the balcony of the ancestral hall and read "The path of duty is the path of safety." The league seeks to promote the legislative adoption of compulsory naval and military training for national defence. The committee of the Warrington branch is as follows :—Mr. T.H. Lyon, IJ.P., (president), Major F. Mc I. Root&amp; (vice-president). Rev. Canon Willis, Rev. Father Wilson, Dr. K. E. Markel, Ph D., M.A., Dr. R. Smith, M.R.C.S. Mr. C. Carson with Mr. C. Haslam and Captain G,R. CCosfield (lion. secretaries.) The pro- posals of the National Service League are broadly as follows :—(l.)—Physical training on scientific prin- ciples and military drill should be made compulsory as part of the educational course in all schools. (11.)--. Between the ages of eighteen and twenty-two every youth not enlisted in the Regular Army or Navy, or serving in the Mercantile Marine, or joining some branch of the Auxiliary Forces in which the standard of training shall be higher than that proposed, should be liable for training as below:- 1. For those electing to be trained in the land forces: —Two months' training in a camp of exercise under canvas in theS first year. Fourteen days' training in the second, third and fourth year. All who have been through this training to be offered a retaining fee for an extra term, oncondition that they completean annual course of musketrf. 2. For those electing to be trained at sea :—Two months' training in the first year. Fourteen days' train- ing in the second, third and fouth year. 3. Every man in the land forces to receivea shilling a day and rations during the period of training. Every man in the naval forces to receive one shilling and six- pence a day and rations during the period of training. The forces to be organised and trained locally. Those undergoing training to be trained as far as possible with reference to their occupation in civil life.- The exemp- tions from military service in force in other countries to be recognised, and areduction in the period of train- ing only to be allowed in respect of educational qualifi- cations.</p>
        <p>Mr. T.H. Lyon presided, and was suppor fed, by Earl Egerton of Tatton and the Duchess of Buckinghamand Chandos, Lord Newton, General Sir Thomas Fraser, K.C.8., and Lady Fraser, Colonel and Mrs. Cornwall Legh, Colonel Ridgway, Colonel Brooke (Royal Engin- eers), Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr. Shee (secretary of the League), Major F. Mc L Roome, Major Lyon, Canon Willis (Rector of \Warrington). Rev. the Hon. C. F. Cross (Vicar of Stretton), Miss Shrine, Mr. Ussher, Mrs. Kenneth Foster, Mr. and -Miss Langford Brooke, Mr. George Egerton Warburton, the Hon. Mr. John Cross, (Captain G. R. Crosfteld, (hon. sec.), and others. A letter waslread from the Duke of Wellington, K.G. President of the League, regretting his inability to he present, and wishing the meeting every success. Apologies for absence were also received from Lord Lilford, Mr. Egert9n, Warburton, Sir John Brunner, Mr. Leo Manse, Colonel Pilkington, M.P., Colonel G. Kemp, M.P., Sir Gilbert Greenall,Bart., and Sir Ernest Forbes Adam.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in a few introductory remarks , expressed the satisfaction of the committee in securing the presence or Earl Egerton of Tatton, who wasone of the principal promoters of the League —(applause), and Mr. Shoe, the secretary, to address them on so important a topic. He recommended every- one to read Mr. Shoe's book—a book that taught true patriots hoWto do their duty. Mr. Shoe afterwards spoke at great length on the aims of the League. These aims were (1) the vital import- ance to the Empire of having a reserve of trained men both for the Army and for the Navy; (2) the physical, moral, and industrial benefits resulting from general, naval or military training, and the special need for such training in the case of our large town population; and (3) the costliness and the inadequacy of any measure, short of general .% training, which may be put forward with the object of creating reserve forces. rheir desire was to place the national defence on a sure basis, and to do this they proposed to make it incumbent upon every able-bodied youth to go through a minimum training which would render him able to defend the country in the day of need. They did not propose conscription, but a system of universal military training upon the Swiss system. Lord Newton said be had arrived at the conclusion that some form of compulsory military service was absolutely necessary for this country, and the-report of the War Commission had strengthened that belief. He contended that our military weakness was, due, directly and indirectly, to what he ventured to call a blind adherence to the so-called voluntary system. Before the issue of the War Commission report, he was well aware of the weak state of our defence, and was not in , the least surprised when war broke out that we had to supplicate the Colonies to come to our assistance' and had to pay five shillings a day to men who barely knew one end .of a horse from the other, and dis- played ignorance to the same eatent in respect of the rifle with which they were armed. (Laughter.) He was no admirer of the so-called voluntary system. He admitted, however, that the regular army of this country must be a voluntary army, but forhome defence the nation should be trained to arms. (Applause.) Continuing, his Lordship spoke of the pour pay the pro- fessional soldier received, which was in itself a great harrier to men of intelligence adopting the army as a profession. (Hear, hear.) in the words of Lord Rose- bery-, they muddled through the Soutii African war, and the Government was now improving the Volunteers out , of existence. (Laughter and applause.) The Volunteer took upon himself the patriotic, duty of training himself for home defence. What the Government said to this patriotic body of men now was this: "Hitherto you have not done enough. You will have to do a great deal more, or we shall punish you." (Laughter.) That was, he maintained, a gross act of injustice. (Hear, hear.) He did not hesitate to say that had we been prepared for war the fight in South Africa would not have taken place. (Hear, hear.) Our present hand to mouth system could net go on indefinitely. The late war Ought to have taught us, if we were not absolutely blind, that do what we would, we could not take citizens from their employment and turn them into trained soldiers at once. Did anyone suppose that if we were at war with one of the European powers we should be allowed to take people from the plough and the factory and 'convert them into soldiers at our leisure? Even this elementary truth must be apparent to everybody at the present moment and he thought it was becoming apparent to the Government. He could not see any reason whatever why home defence should be a voluntary question at all. Surely if there was one thing more incumbent upon a man than anything else it was that he should learn to defend his own goods and his own country. He would not give any option at all. He would say: "You are a citizen; you have duties like anyone else; your duty is to fit yourself for the &amp;fence of the country and if you don't do . it I shall make it uncomfortable for you." In carrying this absurd and absblute voluntary i-ystem we were trying to do what had been abandoned by every other civilised nation. We had made , it into a sort of fetish or idol. We put it in the same catagory as cricket and Free Trade. (Laughter). He confessed it was a very, encouraging sign to find that the Free Trade idol was showing signs of wear and ' tear. (Laughter). It was likely that the idol and fetish of the voluntary military system would disappear too. He would implore theni not to be misled into supposing thet army reform and thequestion of national defence were nothing more than questions of war office re, organisation. He was not an admirer of the war office. (Laughter). The war office was perhaps not a more efficient department than any other, bat it was grossly -unjust to place the blame of our military inefficiency on our War Office. The War Office had not got the proper material to work with, and if we had not the proper material how could we have a sound system. What we should work for was not the reorganisation of trumpery details but to put our military edifice upon a sound foundation. (Hear, bear.) The only sound foundation was the principle of universal personal service. (Hear, hear.) Hitherto we had not availed ourselves of it and the result of our carelessness might be disastrous in the future. Every sensible and every patrotic person ought to see by this time that some radical change in the nature of what was now advocated was necessary. if they had the courage to advocate it and if they could find a GovOrnment to carry it ,out they would have the pleasure of conferring incalculable be,...efits upon the rising and future generations of Englishmen. (Ap- plause. Earl Egerton of Patton said Englishmen were as a rule a conservative people, and when they made chang6s they made them gradually. Therefore, in order to make any change in the constitution of the Army the whole question had to be thoroughly discussed. One of the bugbears of Governments had been the fear of con- scription.. He thought it had been shown of late that that was a bugbea,r which ought to be removed. During the last war we had suffered enormously because we had rested entirely noon what was called the voluntary system. We had not in the Army the class of men we ought - to have. The majority of the recruits to-day were drawn front our large cities, and lacked the stamina which enabled them to go successfully through the trying ordeals of a soldier's life. in the South African war large numbers fell easy victims to disease. With universal military training we should have a better chance of filling the ranks with men of good physique and possessing those ilualities which were essential to success. His Lordship contended that the voluntary system had broken down, and that the defence of the</p>
        <p>country would not be placed on a satisfactory basis until we had a nation trained to arms. (Applause.) add General Sir Thomaf Fraser</p>
        <p>General Sir Thomas Fraser, K. C. B. said he was con- vinced of the great necessity of improving the defences of the country. If they wanted to introduce any sub- ject in England and carry it, they must carry with them the mass of the people who governed the country. Therefore the league ought to set to work and submit its proposals to the public generally. It was said that every country had the government it deserved. We had been very well content with our system of government and he thought we deserved an excellent thing and had got it. He believed it was also true that every country got the army and navy it deserved, but as regarded the army he did not think they could felicitate themselves (Hear hear) General Fraser proceeded to point out that personal service was as old as the English constitution. In an interestinr, way he . contrasted the ppsition of England one hundred years ago, whenshe was absolutely supreme on. the sea;with the condition of things today when we had many rival powers with large fleets on the sea. During that period our empire had grown enor- mously and at the present moment the king ruled over a quarter of the inhabitants of this world, his subjects numbering over 400,000,000. If the people of this country were shown that the empire on which we ex- isted wasat stake and was in real danger in case of attack, he thought the league would be able to carry their proposals through with perfect success. (Hear hear).</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Willis thought the league deserved the highest praise for its candour and courage. In issuing a most excellent pamphlet explaining the objects of the league, the committee had not hesitated to put the pros and cons of all the great experts and it had not shrunk from making public its principles and inten- tions. It was such a (rood object that it could stand on its own merits. He advocated the aims of this univer- sal service on three grounds: The first was on the ground of peace. Did anybody think that the Boers would have rushed into war if they had thought we were prepared!? We had reason to know that the Boers were aware that we were unprepared and so they rushed into war. Suppose we were at war with a great con- tinent•al power such as 'France, Germany or Russia, what • would be our trosition ? France and Germany had each 4,000,000 trained men and Russia had 8,000,0 05 It was because he was a man of peace and because he felt the importance of peace that he advocated universal service. Secondly, he supported it on the ground of patriotism. Anyone who read history must feel a thrill of pride when he thought of the position of this country with what it was, say, 400 years ago. We had got possessions in each\ of the great continents 9f the world. The population of the British Empire was 400,000,000, and Our commerce 10,000,000 tons, whereas the commerce of France was one million tons, and Germany two millions. We needed to be prepared to defend such a rich heritage. Then thirdly, he supported universal service on the ground of national physique. He knew there were many causes at work tending to the physical degeneracy of the people. Scanty food,impure air, and early marriages might account for much of this degeneracy, but he thought a want of physical training was also a cause. He was immensely struck with what : he saw in Germany. He sa•w the order which came from universal service, the most implicit obedience and the sobriety of the people. Whilst there he never saw a case of drunkenness. (Hear, hear.) He was quite : sure that if we paid more attention to drill and universal service as well as to other things, our nation might still be greater and better that it is. (Applause): Colonel Ridgway, in preposing a vote of thanks to the speakers, said he had had !large experience of the auxiliary forces, and he was of the same opinion as Mr. Shea and the other speakers that the present system could not go on. Patriotism was very good,but patriotism without. drill was not good. It would not win battles. We had in this country a great number of men who seemed to think that they could attain their drill by attending football matches. (Laughter.) It was neces- sary that they should be trained to arms and constitute a great reserve, whether they called it universal service or conscription. It would have to be done, and until it was done they could not expect to get the defences of the country on a proper footing. • (Hear, hear.) Mr. A. H. Crosfteld said he had very great pleasure in supporting cordially and earnestly this vote of thanks. He thought they had listened to speeches that were very instructive, very important, extremely interesting and very suggestive. He'hoped they would have the effect in Warrington of leading men of all shades of opinion'to devote to this question of national defence far more attention than he was afraid it had yet received. (Hear, hear.) He came to that meeting to be instructed and he was afraid he must plead guilty to being one of those who had not so far given the subject as close a study as it deserved. There were sty+ a variety of topics nowadays claiming one's attention. Lord New- ton alluded very humorously to the sort of questions one had to deal with. He was told there were 15,000,000 leaflets in stock at Highbury—(laughter)----and another 15,000,000 leaflets in preparation. (Laughter.) They would soon see what was in store for them, the country in fact was getting snowed with leaflets. (Renewed laughter.) In regard to this question he (Mr. Crosileld) yielded to nobody in recog- nizing that IV3 owed it to - our country to see that it was thoroughly and powerfully defended. (Cheers). That was a necessity which appealed equally to patriotism, to prudence, and to common sense. Canon Willis had said that he approacned this question as a man of peaee and with the traditions of his (Mr. Crostield's)family that was the point of view from which he approached it. (Hear, hear.) He thought people of all shades of opinion had got to recognise that an adequate Imperial -force to defend our shores and Empire was the best possible guarantee of peace. (Hear, hear.) The sabject they had !discussed that afternoon appeared to divide itself into two branches. First, the great object which the gentlemen who had come 'here had in view was the important question as to the best methods of achieving it. He (Mr. Orosfleld) was not an expert on this question and it would be out of place for him on that occasion to offer any criticism on this question of methods. This would he thought have to be further considered but as regards the main object in view he would do his best to promote it in IlWarrington. (Applause.) He mostheartily thanked the distinguished speakers who had Come to Warrington that day to inform and educate the public and he had very great pleasure there- fore in seconding the vote and putting it to the meet- ing. The vote of thanks was carried with enthusiasm. Captain Crosfteld proposed a very hearty vote of ' thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Lyon. He said both. Mr. and Mrs. Lyon had been unwearying in their endeavours to make the arrangements for that meeting ad what was more they had taken the greatest enjoyment in doing it. (Hear, hear.) At the suggestion of .Captain Crosfleld three cheers were raised for Mr. and Mrs. Lyon. Mr. Lyon acknowledged the compliment and the meeting terminated. • -</p>
        <p>Tea a..-nd coffee were afterwards served and a troupe of pierrots gave an entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S VIEWS ON THE</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S VIEWS ON THE</p>
        <p>MATTER. "NOT YET A CONVERT TO COMPULSORV SERVICE.' Speech to the Oddfellows.</p>
        <p>, Addressing the members of the Prince of Wales Lodge of Oddfellows on Thursday evening, at the presentation of prizes in connection with the annual bowling handicap, Mr. A. H. Crosaeld alluded to the question of national defence. It was a question, he said, which he had discussed - a .great deal lately. They owed the important meeting which had. been held at Appleton Hall to the public Spirit of Mr. and Mrs. Lyon and he thought. they had done a real and valuable service to the town. He spoke to them as fellow-townsmen on a question which he was very glad to say in strik- ing contrast to many other public questions, was not a party matter, but a matter of 'real and vital national importance (Hear hear). It had been pointedly brought horn') to us lately by the amazing revelations of the Commission of inquiry into the conduct of the war. The report of that Commission hid shown that in plain English the defences even of great Britain itself were imperfect and inadequate and that we actually hod not in these islands having regard to the safe conduct of the great food traffic on which we depended, sufficient defences to save us from foreign attack. He did not approach this question—as he thought a good many would realise— from the point of view of that interesting individual who had been described as a jingo. He approached this matter from the standpoint of what was called sane imperialism. (Hear, hear.) There was a kind of imperialism—or he would ratherecall it jingoism—with which he, fpr one,. would never be identified. As regarded that aggressive and provocative kind of foreign policy which was adopted in the summer of 1899 he retracted not one word or one syllable of what he had , written in the local press. On account of the position .he (Mr. Crosfield) then took up, a certain distinguished Ipolitician came down not very long since an,4 called him a "Little Englander" mid various other things (Laughter.) He (Mr. Crosfleld's) impression was that that gentleman would not retarn and do that again because their friend got just as good as he gave, and perhaps a little into the bargain. (Hear, hea.r,and laugh- ter). He did not look at this question of our national defences from the point of view of a little Englander. (Hear,lear.) Our national defences came under three catagories. They knew it was essential for us to have an army well paid, both as regarded officers and men— ' an army that would be a thoroughly efficient striking force, when ever in time of need it was wanted for foreign' service. But, beyond this they required some- thing more. . (Hear, hear.) They could not have the fleet confined to the work of guarding these shores. The fleet was wanted incase of need for carrying the war 1 into the enemy's camp, for guarding our commerce and food supplies from all parts of the world. Therefore it was absolutely essential to have an adequate defence for the whole country at home. This could be</p>
        <p>LC')</p>
        <p>obtained by having a east standing army, as on the Con- tinent, but that was exactly what we did not want. (Hear, . hear.) We had to maintain an enormous navy, and we did not want to have the incubus of maintaining a vast standing army in addition. Thee wile only one other way out of the difficulty and that was by a powerful, corn- patent, well-equipped and highly efficient Volunteer service. (Applause.) How to get it was an important question and there would be differences of _opinion as to the methods in which this efficient Volunteer force could best be produced. Many military authorities who had given close study to the question had come to the conclusion that the best way to bring about the desired end was to make it compulsory for every man to give utAbout 100 days of his life in acquiring the rudiments- of military science. He was bound to tell them, speaking for himself who was not an expert on this question of home defence, that so fares he had ' one in the matter he was not yet a convert to com- pulsory service. (Hear, hear.) He still believed that if the question was rightly approached we should be able to get all the fightina force on Volunteer lines which was necessary for the protection of our trade,of our homes, and of old England herself. (Applause.) He did not believe that the right spirit—the true spirit— of patriotism was so defunct or deficient that our citizens would fail to respond if it was made in the right way. (Applause.) A VOICE: They have not done so in the past. Mr. CROSFIELD ": So far they have never failed us. (Applause.) Continuing he said he had heard a great deal about the matter as his brother was deeply inter- ested in it. He could not say that anything like proper encoumgementlead beerigiven to our count rymenito serve as Volunteers. (Hear, hear.) If it was necessary that large body of men in the country, say half-a-million I men, should always be ready as competent Volunteers, and if in order to attain that object it was necessary to I get men to go into camp for two months' training during 'the first year and afterwards do a fortnight's drill for the next five, six or seven years, he (Mr. Crosfield) believed it could be got on Volunteer lines. (Applaute.) I The whole system would have to be radically different ' from what it had been. He knew perfectly well the I difficulty which had been experienced with employers; ' how employes had been cold-shouldered when they had wished to go away for a fort- night's drill and in some cases a week's drill, and how in some cases men had been penalised; how men had been out of pocket and how they had been dis- couraged in every yvay. His point was this that if Englishmen were approached in the right way they would respond to the call. (Hear, hear.) He did not think a single man ought to be one penny-piece out of pocket if he gave his time to this patriotic duty, nor did he think it was necessary to offer the slightest monetary bribe in order to get men to serve in the defence of their country, but they ought not to be Penalised (Hear, hear.) If necessary Parliament ought to step in and wjthout delay too—for it is a pressing matter—and lay it down that every man was to have the opportunity of giving the necessary service. (Hear, hear), In the event of any person being disposed to handicap such a man or punish or penalise him for entering that service, he thought Parliament ought to attach a very heavy penalty on the person who did so. Then the pay given Ito men while in camp ought to be good pay just as it ought to be good pay to I men of the line. Everything should be done that was necessary, not to discourage, but to encourage those who were anxious to become proficient in the work of National defence. (Hear, hear.) This meant money, perhaps a large expenditure and he did not think it. practicable unless they tapped fresh source of revenue. He had told friends of his and he repeated it now that when this reform was carried out he thought it would be high time to carry reform of taxation and include some method of taxation of site values and land along with it. Some of his friends had been arguing that we should go in for compulsory service all round and bring in everybody. But after all there were so many sides to English life and English character that he did not himself see the necessity or even the advantage of carrying the thing so far. What- ever method was adopted to achieve the great end which he had briefly described, let altenative methods .be thorougly considered and well thrashed out, and then let the best plan win. (Applause). Personally he did not wish to see every man trained as a soldier,any more than he should be trained as a sailor. Nor for his part would he care to See the military spirit developed in our coun- try beyond the point which would give us ample and abundant national security. (Hear, hear.) But after all that was not the first question we had before its. The grave and serious fact which stared us in the face was the utter inadequacy of our present force. And it. was the bonnden duty of every Englishman to help to repair the deficiency in one way or another, and to do so without loss of time. We must do this for the sake of the hearths, and homes of Old England; we must do it for the peace of the world for what greater menace to that peace could they imagine than a weak and unprotected British Empire? He was sure every one of them must feel that this was a question of supreme national importance and urgency. (Hear. hear.) How to solve it he frankly admitted was 'a grave problem. Personally he believed it could be solved without compulsion. But if they thought it was behind the times, well any little deficiency or short- comings of his in that respect would hr more than made up for by another member of the family—(laughter)--- because he emild assure them that his brother George as well as his friend Major Rooms, were working tooth and nail to get this question" forrarder,', and he hoped they would say with him "more power to their elbow." He might tell them that he promised that? afternoon, that with the permission of the Chairman and their own kind indulgence, he would venture to bring this question under their notice that evening, and] perhaps they would accept that as an excuse if he hadtdetained them a little too long upon it. lie spoke as a sane imperialist. He had travelled a bit and seen something of the Empire. He had seen the noble work done by England in Egypt. He had seen that mighty territory of Canada which offered such a wonderful field and glorious home for millions of our people in years to come. When heaves there he could not help being struck by the kinship that one found there and the warm welcome which was extended by all classes of the people. One could not hurt or wound a. Canadian more than by forgetting that he was a mem- ber with yourself, on an equal footing of our great empire. (Hear, hear.) He said that without reflecting in the slightest degree upon the great republic by which, for the present, Canada was a little overshadowed. He had spent some of his time in America, and he remembered after his trip to Catiada, spending some weeks at Newport, which so many American commercial magnates made their summer home. There was a dinner party there at which many of them were present. in referring to the competition between Great BNtain and the States he (MaCrosfield) expressed the opinion that it seemed to him a danger that, 'the two greatest forces in the world were to be brought into sharp political rivalry in the future. He remembered very well telling his friends that night that while there had been this enormous blunder made by Lord North in the years between 1775 to 1780, which had cost us the greatest possession of the Crown, yet it VMS not a blunder beyond reparation. Well what followed; did they start to twist the lion's tail?—(laughter)—or did the eagle flap his wings and look dangerous? No. He would tell them what happened. Bishop Potter rose and made a speech in which he gave cordial consideration to his (Mr. Cros- field's) remarks, and wound up by proposing the Queen's health. He (Mr. Crosfield) responded by pro- posing the President's. Was that not a little indication of the fellow feeling which after all underlies all other feelings in the Anglo-Saxon race—(hear, hear)—that in fact" Bood is thicker than water. (Hear, hear.) And then just think how many dis- tinguished men on this side of the water were united by tender ties to the daughters of the west on the other side. (Laughter.) Well, this was but a possibility of some future time. Meanwhile we had to face the cir- cumstances and the problems of the present day. There had been bequeathed to its a great trust and a great heritage. Let them dominate 'the councils of the world, not to produce or provoke bloody warfare with all its squalid horror and unspeakable waste and misery, but in the interests of a farhigher and better civilization than we saw around us to-day. Then indeed might come a day "When the common sense of most shall hold the fretful realm in awe." (Cheers.) But while this was but a hopeful possibility of the distant future, we had to reckon with the frets of to-day, and with the primary fact that we were responsible for the government and defence of some font- hundred millions of people, about one quarter of the inhabitants of the globe. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>HELSBY PORTER'S TERRIBLE DEATH</head>
        <p>HELSBY PORTER'S TERRIBLE DEATH</p>
        <p>Killed While on a Carriage Footboard.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Coroner Bate held an inquest at Prodshain touching the death of %alter Clays, Porter at Helsby Station, who on Saturday night was killed on the rail- way between Helsby and Frodsham. He was seen on the footboard passing from one carriage to another. He disappeared while the train was passing over Chester-road bridge. His body bore marks indicating that the bridge must have struck him off the footboard with terrible force. While going to Chester deceased walked along the footboard, • and had remarked to his friend Brooks that there were a couple in another compartment. Brooks, who admitted having enough of drink, said he slept on the return journey, and awakened to find himself alone in the compartment and Clays oa the footboard. Other evidence showed that the window of the com- partment was broken. The Corbner thought it possible that Clays was try- 'ing to get into another compartment. I Brooks's evidence was described-as unsatisfactory. A verdict of "Death from misadventure" wit% eturned.</p>
        <p>A RECORD FOR ST. HELENS. Fifteen weeks had elapsed up to Saturday night with- out the St. Helens Fire Brigade having received a cal/. This constitutes a record for many years. On Saturday night they were summoned to a fire at the slaughter house connected with the Co-operative Stores in Henry- street, but no serious damage was done.</p>
