      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SAD HOME LIFE AT WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>,te-Ni</p>
        <p>Jam es H wgreave3, residing at Morris Court, Church- street, was summoned at the Warrington Police Court on Monday for neglecting his two children—Alfred (aged 10) anl James (aged 3).—Mr. Browne prose- cuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. and said the defend- ant was formerly in the employ of the Corporation and earned 255.- per week. On the day after Good Friday he gave up his situation and since then he had not been so regularly e.nployed. Inspector Hackett's attention was called to the case on May 2nd and he warned defendant that he mast stop drinking and attend to his children. Defendant promised to be better. The house was fairly well furnished and very clean, and the Inspector said the wife evidently did her duty. For some time there was an improvement and defendant worked regularly. On September 10th, however, his wife wrote to the inspector asking him to come to the house as she had nothing at all to eat and there was no fire in the grate. On visiting the house the Inspector found " the woman's statement to be true. Defendant had beea drinking for f)iar consecutive days and had given his wife only 84. or some ridiculous sum like that, during a considerable period. He had told his wife he would never be better until he went to gaol.—The defendant's wife was called and said that on many occasions the only food she and the children had received had been two meals of dry bread per day. The children had been starved.—The magistrates decided to adjourn the case for a month to give defendant an opportunity of amendment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court. Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court. Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (in the chair), T. Birchall, J. W. Hayward and S. Wright, Alderman</p>
        <p>Smethurst, and Councillor Thornton. A POLICEMAN ASSAULTED.—WiIIiarn Smallman, 36, Old-road, was charged with having been drunk and dis- orderly in Bridge-street. at 1.30 on Sunday morning, and also with assaulting Police-constable Gibbs in Museum- street. Prisoner denied the assault.—Gibbs said that he saw the prisoner in Bridge-street at 1.30 on Sunday morning in a elrunken condition, shouting, swearing,and behaving in a very disorderly manner. Witness took him into custody because he refused to go away, and when in Museum-street prisoner stooped down and attemptedlto throw him over his shoulder. Prisoner said, "I have done silemonths and Twill do another six months forthee."They fell to the ground,when the prisoner kicked him several times about the legs.ln the struggle witness's right foot was injured.—Prisoner, who had previously been convicted, was committed to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Adolphus Frederick Wilkin- son, Seven Oaks, Northwich, was summoned for cruelty -t) a horse by working it whilst in an unfit state.—P.C. Phillips stated that on September 9 he was on duty at B:idge Foot, and saw the defendant in charge of a grey mire attached to a loaded cart. The horse was very lame on the near fore foot.He called defendant's attention to it, and he said he kaew the animal was lame and that it was suffering from ringbone.—P.C. Gibbs corroborated. —Tnspector Print, R.S.P.C.A. said he examined the horse and found it to be exceedingly lame on the near fore limb from ringbone. It was suffering consider- able pain and was quite unfit for work.—The defendant stated that the horse" went worse after he left home." A fine of 10s. was imposed. FicammG.—The following were bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs for committing a breach of the peace by fighting in Winwick road : Andrew Leonard, 5, Melville street, John Birton, 84. Winwick street, and William Richardson, 70, Watkin st:eet. A case against John Marriott, 20, Dolman's lane, was dismissed on payment of costs (3s 6d). DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT AT A SALVATION ARMY MEET ENG.—Mary Broderick, 4, Dallam lane, was sum- moned for having been drunk and disorderly in Bewsey street on September 19th.—The evidence showed that defendant went to a Salvation Army open air meeting and made US3 of filthy language. A woman took her home and a police officer who followed to obtain her name and address was threatened by the defendant— Defendant,who made her 30th appearance, was fined 10s and costs with the option of a month's imprisonment. Y.—Before Aldermen Sutton (presiding), and Hutchinson, and Dr. Fox. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A MoN-rif FOR BEGGING.—Thor-as Collins, last from Liverpool, was charged with begging in Bridge-street on Monday morning.—The prisoner went to the shop of Mr. Oliver, Bridge-street, in an intoxicated condition, and asked for coppers. P.C. lla.mblett was informed of tue circumtance, and he afterwards saw the prisoner stop' a man in the street. With the assistance of P.C. "folly prisoner was taken into custody, and ln the way to the Police Station he used very bad language. This was his tenth appearance, and he was sentenced to a month's imprisonment. SERtuus AssNut.T.--John M'Cormick, 112. Forster street, was charged with assaulting Thomas Bracegirdle Jockey-street, at 11.30 on Saturday night. Prisoner, who was represented by Mr. Steel, pleaded not guilty.— The charge was ofiginally one of unlawful wounding but with the consent of complainant it was reduced.— Complainant stated that on the night in question he was in his back kitchen when prisoner came, under the influence of drink, into the back yard. Witness went and told him to ge out, as he had no right there. While gfArvi out, prisoner struck witness with something on the had and wrist, causing wounds which had to be dressed at the•lnfirmary.—l reply to Mr. Steel, Brace- girdle, denied having struck the prisoner. Several witnesses were called for the prosecution,and Mr. Steel said that his client ' had had far too much drink, and had gone into the yard by mistake Someone came out of the house and prisoner was treated very roughly. He was knocked against the wall and struck on the face. There ;was no truth in the sug- gestion that he had anything in his hand, and he did not remember strikiug complainant. It was probable that Braeegirdle sustained his injuries by falling against the wall.—The Bench inflicted a fine of 10s. and costs.</p>
        <p>.WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. T. Brchall (presiding), and</p>
        <p>other justices. SHOP-DOOR THEFT.—Sarah Maloney, who is well- known to the police, was charged with stealing a pair of boots value 2s. lid, from the door of Messrs. Baker and Co s, establishment in Horsemarket-street.—The Chief Constable said that at 6.30 on Tuesday evening Mr. Albert Pritchard, the manager for the firm, sew the boots hanging outside the six:), but when the police made a certain inquiry he missed them. At seven pr •3oner went to Mr. 'Pyrer's pawnshop and offered ti: 3 boots in pledge to the assistant, Walter Bracegird: :3. Noticing Messrs. Baker's stamp on the boots and seeing that they were new, he gave informa- tion to the police and the woman was arrested.—She admitted committing, the theft and was sent to prison , for three months.</p>
      </div>
