      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>REV. J. S. HUGHES'S VIEWS.</head>
        <p>THE REV. J. S. HUGHES'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. S. Hughes preached on the Macedonian crisis on Sunday evening at Hill Cliffe Chapel. He took as the basis of his discourse, "The appearance of ' the man of Macedonia to the apostle Paul,' and dwelt upon the contrast between the ancient and modern cry of Macedonia. He also reviewed briefly the history of the Turkish rule, as .expressed in the Bulgarian atrocrities of twenty-five years ago, and the Armenian massacres of seven and eight years ago, and now culminating in the Macedonian herrors. He showed that this was the result of Turkish misrule, and the apparent hatred of Christianity which prevailed in Turkey and the desire to exterminate the Christians. That tyranny, hatred and cruelty were characteristic of the Sultan,who had become variously known in history as "Tae great assassin" and "The monster." Whether these titles could be fully justified or not, these recurring outbursts of massacres were a acandal to the nation and to Europe and called for strong syntpathy for their suffering fellow Christians and energetic measures from the .European Powers. They must remember that the bitter cry was not, as in the days of the Apostle, the cry of the heathen, but the cry of Christians, whose chief offence was their Chris- tianity. The cry of to-day was not the indefinite cry of a child, half conscious, just waking from its sleep. It was not a midnight dream,but,the strong cry of a whole nation suffering from unspeakable i etrocities n the open day, the slaughtered victims of passion and revenge. He gave several instances of the enormities that were being daily cemmitted, and said thacry they heard from Macedonia was not an appeal to one man, as in Paul's case, but to the nations, and should they cry in vain? Germany and Russia were turning deaf ears to the piteous appeal. Italy and France, more recently delivered from despotic pviesteraft, were inclined to respond. What should they do, they who had always been rag trde'l as the friend of the oppressed and down- trodden? Was it not a reflection on Christianity that they hs,l been s:.) norespansive, so deaf and so inactive for they were under speoial obligations to these oppressed people? Under the treaty of Berlin they were in a large measure responsible for the guardian- ship of these peopleland yet so far they had refused to do that, and had looked with undimmed eyes upon dastardly outrages on helpless victims. Why their sense of humanity called for action ; otherwise they involved themselves in woeful guilt. What could they. do? They could pray tolhigh heaven and join in a con- cert of prayer. They could protest, and raise their voices in indignation,against such injustice and cruelty. They could rouse the consciences of the people,ancl stir the They by thundering at its doors. It was to him anawful thing that for the sake of the greed for gold, they en.ta:ed madly into the late disastrous war in South Africa, and it was the same love of money interest that prevents them from flying to the rescue of outraged humanity. If they did not gird against that Sordid spirit anl selfish greed it would undermine their power and hasten their ruin, "for the love of money is the root of all evil." They appealed for immediate action. So then as Nonconformists,there was-a special force in that appeal, for it was in reality a cry foe religious freedom. The Macedonians were fighting for the same right of religious equality which Englishmen claimed: there they were fighting against thelintolerance )10 Mohammedanism,here they were en- gaged in fighting the intolerance of priestcraft. There they saw the ultimate effect of intolerance and tyranny on the throne—lad it was to prevent the enthrOnibg and crowning of priestcraft and intolerance in the rule of this cdantry that they were waging this war for freedom in this country. The sad object lesson of en- throned tyranny in Turkey ought to nerve them with greater energy for the conflict. At the close of the sermon Mr. E. Lloyd Jones spontaneously moved "That a resolution be passed ex- pressive of abhorrence of the M icedonian atrocities and calling upon the Government to take immediate action in the interests of our Macedonian fellow Christians." This was seconded by Mr.E.England and unanimously passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PROPOSED MUSEUM FOR WIDNES.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED MUSEUM FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, at the Monthly meeting of the Widnes Free Library Committee, the question of the establish- ment of a museum and art gallery at Appleton-house was gone into. A sub-commtttee appointed to consider the project submitted a report, in which it was stated that Appleton-house, except that portion used by the head gardener, was unoccupied, and was gradeldlY get- ting into dis-repair. It was found that the rooms on the ground floor of the modern portion of the house, as well-as the entrance-hall, were admirably adapted for the display of pictures and other objects. The com- mittee recommended thqt the rooms should be appro- priated as follows:—No. I, art room; No. 2, reading room; No. 3, industrial museum; entrance-hall, art purposes; end fernery, ornameetal horticultural pur- poses. A deputation of the sub-committee visited the Victoria-park at St. Helens and inspected a smali :museum there. The circumstances in St. Helens were i*most identical with those at Widnes. The sub-com- 'raittee felt that there was no difficulty whatever in opening such an institution at Appleton-house. The estimated cost of putting the place in order was .£BO, and the committee submitted that of that amount £53 should be defrayed by the Park and Cemetery Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Councillor Laws proposed the adoption of the report and Mr. Calvert secondel.</p>
        <p>Alderman Wareing thought £BO altogether inadequate to put the place in proper repair.- Mr. F. Neil expressed a similar opinion, whereupon it was pointed out that it was only proposed to utilise a portion of the building. After some further discussion the report of th? com- mittee was adopted, and the matter will now have to come before the Council.</p>
      </div>
