        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A CONSTANT desire to vindicate themselves keeps lots of people in trouble. THE man who wants the earth probably never stopped to think what the taxes would be. IF the kitten had been provided with a slightly longer tail it would have been deprived of a great deal of innocent amusement. __ _</p>
        <p>ORGANISED charity is the skimmed milk of human kindness. _ _</p>
        <p>ELASTICITY is a good test for suspenders, but a poor one for consciences. HE is a wise man who never lets his wife know that he can put up shelves as well as a carpenter. THE "still small voice" would suit some of us better if it would keep still. IT is the things we are going to do that makes life worth living. "You are my sun," he breathed passionately. "Have you noticed that lately the sun has a ring?" She inquired, with a delicate blush. Summon : "I wondah what makes mah eyes so weak?" Miss Mabel: "They're in a weak place." GRIGGS : "Why is it that young women with fifty thousand or more a year seldom marry?" Marie: "Gracious I They don't have to." "I DON'T think Stuffer likes to sit at the dinner with such a pretty girl." • "Why not?" "It dis- tracts his attention." _ _</p>
        <p>----- " MR. JOHNSIN, does you know whar de sailors got dot name of 'tars' applied to them ? " Sure, in some pitched battle, of course, sat." "WHEN a man asserts that he is just as good as anyone else; do you think he really believes is ? " "Certainly not. He believes he is better." SOMETIMES NOT THEN, EVEN.—" I wonder," said the garrulous boarder, " why they speak of Truth being at the bottom of a well ?" "Because," said the Cheerful Idiot, "It often can be got at only by long pumping." IF SOME ot the people who think they are ahead of the times would pause and look back they might see that the times are not going in their direction at all. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"IT IS said," said one girl, "that many men nowadays have a great deal more money than .! brains.' "Yes," sighed another; "and so little money at that." SWATS—" How long did you know your wife before you married her, Grims ? " " Grims—" Not a minute. Don't know her yet. Never shall know her." MAY: "Do you think George is serious in his attentions to you ? " Maud: "Well, he has reached the stage where he can't sit still for more than half a minute at a time!" SAFE SECRETS.—He—"It wouldn't be the correct thing to tell what happened at the lodge." She —" No; and it would require some recollection of what did happen." CAUSE AND EFFECT.—" Inkwell, the playwright, is as happy as a king." "What is the cause of his joy ?", "The royalties on his new play." DECIDEDLY.—Briggs: "That was a great dance. I hope I made an impression on that girl." Griggs: "I guess you did. She has been limping</p>
        <p>ever since." "You ought to have been at our house last night," said little jimmy Summerlea to Freddy Tillinghast. " Why ?" "We had an eclipse of the moon." " Yah ! we had one too." A CLINGING DErausioN.—" What is force of habit, ma ? " "Force of habit? Well, it is the way your father keeps on thinking he is not fat, after he has had to have a special porch-chair made for him!" A HARD -PUSH, TOO. • "It's love that makes the world go round," So runs the famous song; But you will find that money is What pushes it along.</p>
        <p>ESCAPED HIS MEMORY.—First Statesman: "I see that you were interviewed at length yester- day." Second Statesman (surprised) : Is that so? But now I think of it, I did find a reporter's card when I got home." MISS iSCOTT: " Yes ; she has been saying all manner of wicked things about me." Friend: " You should not heed her, dear. She merely re- peats what other people say." THE BEST REASON.—Little Clarence (who reads and ponders) : "Pa, I have just been reading a paragraph which says there are various reasons why a man wha talks in his sleep should not marry. What are some of those reasons pa?" Mr. Cal- lipers: "The best reason, my son, is because he talks in his sleep." ALL THE WORLD'S—A COURT•HOUSE.—LittIe Clarence (who reads and ponders) : "Pa, is it true, as Shakespeare says, that • all the world's a stage ? " Mr. Callipers: "It may have been so in Snakespeare's time, but as far back as I can remember it has been more like a court-house; it has been so full of trials." .</p>
        <p>A POINT OF INFORMATION.—LittIe Reuben "Pop, equar' ' means 'honest' don't it?" His Father: "Dies right, chile." Little Reuben: " Wal, den, kin a pusson hab a squar' meal off'n a chicken what ain't his'n ?" Tire FAIR THlNG.—Seldum Fedd (at the door) : " Maddirn, have you got a nice plate o' cold meat flat you kin spare for a poor blind man?' Mrs. Flint: "Huh I You are only blind in one eye." Seldum Fedd : "All right, den. Gimme half a plate." WORTH TRYING.—Diok Dashington : "I wish I knew something about law." His Friend: "Want to break a will ? " Dick Dashington : "Not that; but I would like to know it I could get an injunc- tion preventing old Bondclips from interfering with my attentions to his daughter. A NEW RULE.—Patent Medicine Proprietor: "Hereafter, all testimonials must be accompanied by orders for at least half-a-dozen bottles of medi- cine." Clerk: "Yes, sir." Patent Medicine Proprietor: "If these people want to see their names in print, they ought to pay for it." MOTHER: "Where have you been, Lizzie ? " DAugh ter : "Only for a walk in the park." "Who with ? " "No one." "Then explain how you came home with a walking stick instead of an um- brella."</p>
        <p>WIFE: "John, don't you think you had better give up trying to shave yourself, and go back to the barber?" Husband: "Why, of course not. See how much I save every month." Wife : " Yes, I know that; but then Willie is always around when you shave, and he is learning so many bad words.</p>
        <p>„ SUCCESS IN ARGUMENT.—” I understand she is very successful in argument." "Well, I should say so. Why, at our debating club last week she was still talking when everyone of those opposing her was completely tired out." SOMETHING STRONG.—Customer: I want some- thing that will kill the smell of onions on the breath." Druggist: "Give me your name and address, please. Customer: What for " Drug- gist : "We never sell poison without first obtain- ing that information."</p>
        <p>COULD APPRECIATE THE SITUATION. First Tramp: "It wuz an hour an' a half dis mornie before I cud find anybody dat 'd give me some breakfast." Second Tramp (sympathetically: "It's awful to have yer leisure time broke up like dat."</p>
        <p>NOT VELL.—A marine recruit of German extrac- tion, who was walking his post and calling the hours as required, called, "Seven bells and all's yell." The next call, however, was a variation. It was: "Eight bells and all is not veil; I have droppit my musket oferboard."</p>
        <p>THE ONLY ONE OF THE KlND.—Grimahaw : "I hardly know what to make of Poorchap. I never knew another fellow just like him." Askins : "What is there peculiar about him?" Grim- shaw : "Why, although he is in very straitened circumstances he never brags about having squan- dered a fortune 111 his younger days !" LITTLE Boy: "Mamma, may I give what's in my savings bank to that beggar man?" Mamma: "You dear, sweet little cherub! Do you want to give away all of that money your uncle gave you? There was over five shillings." "I spent some of it, mamma." "Did you? How much is there ?" "There's •a shilling left, but the e&amp;udiz</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>MACEDONIAN CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE MACEDONIAN CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Sermon by the Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>THE PRIME MINISTER'S REPLY TO THE</p>
        <p>PRIMATE.</p>
        <p>"The Piercing Cry for help from Macedonia" was the subject of a special sermon by the Rev. John Yonge at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening. There was a large congregation. Thepreacher observed that the book of the Acts of the Apostles recorded how St. Paul when preaching the Christian gospel in Asia, one night saw a visioa. ,He saw a man from Macedonia, who said to him, "Come over into Macedonia, and help us." Consequent upon that striking call, Paul ani his companions crossed the sea and landed on the continent of Europe. Then; in Philippi; a city in Macedonia: for the very first time the gospel was preached and planted upon European soil. That fact in the earlieso history of Christianity must always invest Macedonia with peculiar interest for Christian people, and should make them more than ready to respond, in such terrible times as were now in that province—to the renewed and piercing cry for help and rescue. The fearful sufferings of that people at the hands of their oppressors, who now seemed bent upon their literal extermination.— appealed to the best instincts of common humanity, But they also appealed to our sympathies as fellow Christians, and their cry also appealed powerfully to us . as citizens of the British Empire, inasmuch as their present condition was the direct result of a long established British policy. We could not therefore turn either a deaf or an indifferent ear to their appeal. Vpon every ground of humanity, of Christianity, and of national responsibility, we were bound to , give it sympathetic and practical attention. No attempt at justification could be necessary for deal- ing with the matter on the Lord's Day and in the Lord's house. Indeed, to be silent about it would be absolutely , wrong. The Christian Church, and the Christian pulpit, alike, mistook and exceeded their mission when they displayed the spirit and temper of political partizan- ship. But they ignored and failed in their mission when they did not protest against the wrongs and oppressions of humanity. Against all forms of cruelty and tyramiy and of social, national and international wrong, the Church of Christ was bound to array itself. The Mac- edonian people was a Christian race with an assassin at its throat. Hundreds and thousands of its men, women, and children were being daily done to death, and in the case of its women done to what was infinitely worse than death. Nearly one hundred thousand, it was believed, had already suffered fearful extremities in this way, or had, at least, become homeless, helpless, fugi- tives hiding in forests which their enemies were busy , burning so that the people might perish in the flames or be driven out to yet more cruel death. Netrly 200 ' villages had been burned to the • ground, so that if such as might escape death, should return, ' their homes their farms, their properties, their liveli- ' hood, would all be gone! What seemed in a sense most ; horrible of all, was the cynical tone of statesmen who would say as a distinguished Russian was reported to • have lately said, /that "the slaughter of 10,000 Mace- donians is not of very grave import!"We must not for.:et, however, that similar sentiments were expressad in 1876 by the then Prime Minister of England, had , been repeated in yet more recent days by another con- spicuous English statesman concerning the persecutions and assassinations of the people of Armenia and were ' being renewed to-day by Mr. Balfour himself. When • politicians could forget or sneer at the claims of ' common humanity it was time for Christian Churches to raise their indignant protest.. Proceeding to refer to the attitude of the Turk towards Christians, the rev. gentleman said the Mussulman believed that all Christians were the outcasts of the human race. They could never hope to obtain even the semblance of liberty so long as they were subject to the Sultan. On the • contrary, dreadful and horrible as it appeared. the Turk • persecuted; tortured, dishonoured, robbed and killed * Christians in the belief that he was thereby doing God service. Mr. Yonee then gave a graphic account of the condition of Macedonia tp-day.erhe was strik- ing, he said, for freedom, such as Bulgaria gained 2.3 years ago—for the right to live in peace and quietness, and with prospects of , progress and prosperity. Christian Europe and Chris- ! tian England was letting the Sultan, amid infamous , cruelties, dishonours and sacrifices, stamp out a Chris- tian race, which had been guilty of no crime except the desire to be free from the most infamous tyranny that had ever cursed the world. If Christians stood by, saying nothing and doing nothing, the responsibility of the Turk's iniquitylwas ours even more than his. As a , nation the British people had far greater and more direct and terrible responsibilities. By the treaty of San Stefano, obtained by Russia rrom Turkey as the result of her victorious arms, Macedonia would have , become part and parcel of inaancipated Bulgaria. But • in the congress of Berlin, our Government through their representatives, altered that treaty arrangement • and thrust back the liberated Macedonia into the empire of the tyrant. Marcedonia was again en- slaved by the act of our Government, backed by the , popular sentiments of the misguided day. There would be no possible doubt that if Macedonia had been left as thettreaty with Russia had designed, these troubles and horrors would never have been possible. That was the decisive act which had eventuated as we saw to-day. Let it be understood, however; that while thus disen- franchising Macedonia and throwing her back again into tbe clutches Of Turkey, the treaty of Berlin eontained certain provisions fo r:the better goverament of the Christian provinces by the Turkish authorities. It stipulated certain reforms which had been per- sistently ignored. None of the powers, not even our statesmen, had insisted on these reforms being achieved. He had laid before them the terrible facts of the case of Macedonia. It was simply one more, and perhaps the worst, exhibition of Turkish cruelty and oppression. They had seen, moreover, in what way we, as a nation, were even directly responsible for the whole disaster. The case was making its appeal to our nation with more manifest effect than did the case of Armenia. England's supineness then was inexplicable and shame- ful. A heavy burden of responsibility lay on her in that matter. But it would seem as if now our people were being moved. Very notable had been the protest and appeals 'emanating from Bishops of the Church of England. The Bishop of Durham held that England had an opportunity now as well as a responsibility. The Bishop of Hereford wanted to know what had come of the Foreign Secretary's promise six months he() to insist upon reforms for Macedonia. The Bishop of Worcester thought silence had become at last unbearable, and asked what England was doing to bring about eoncerted action. The Archbishop of Canterbury had been addressing a letter to the Prime Minister urg- in„ the necessities of the case. All that was hopeful and encouraging. But what, so far was the upshot? In! the paper of Saturday they had the Premiers reply to Primate, and grievously disappointing was its whble tone. Its gist was this—that as Austria and Russia were apparently moving in the matter, with the consent of the other Powers of Europe, we, as a nation, could safely leave the matter in their hands. Further Mr. Balfour suggested that the Macedonians were as blame- worthy as this Turks for the steps they had. taken to create and ferment the insurrection. Evidently in his Opinion Macedonia was to blame in seeking her libertie</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A Foundling. On Sunday morning a newly-born child was found in Bank-street. It was taken to the Workhouse and is doing well. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. A Warrington Soldier in Trouble. Mail advices from India state that Private Earn- shaw, a Lancashire soldier connected with the Bth King's, recruited at Warrington, had been arrested and handed over to the military authorities at Rangoon some time ago for personating a detective and encouraging young lads to hold secret meetings. Some doubts were entertained as to his sanity, and he has been for-some time in the military hospital under observation. It is now announced that he has been pronounced sane, and has been handed over to the I police.</p>
        <p>BASSINETTES AND • MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J., S. G ARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Canal Company and the River. • At a special meeting of the Chamber of Commerce held at the Chambers, Market-gate, on Monday morning, usder the presidency of Mr. John Fairclough,, the proposals of the Ship Canal Company with refer- ence to the condition of the river were carefully considered. Whilst the Council approved of the general principle of the weir and sluices they decided to wait for further information before passing any definite resolution, and refrained from expressing any opinion as to the details of the scheme. Asti for CDNNINGIIAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERA. WATERS.—Advt. The Photographic Society. On Saturday the members of this society held the last of 'the series of summer outings, the rendezvous being Delamere. A punctual start was made from the headquarters, the Old Academy. The afternoon was perfect for photographic operations, but the drive and tea occupied most of the time before darkness set in. • From the social point of view the outing was an un- qualified success, and being under the direction of the newly-appointed secretary, Mr. G. Nicol's, it augurs well for a very prosperous winter season, the syllabus of which will be shortly issued. GIVE THEM A TRIAL.—ALF. Ta.v.Lort's wonder- ful boots for everybody. The November Elections. On Monday evening a meeting of the Conservative committee of the Howley Ward was held at the Con- servative Club, • Sankey-street, Mr. John Fairclough presiding. The attendance included Aldermen Bolton and Bennett, Councillors Davies and John Evans, and Mr. Thomas Mason (hon sec.). .A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Evans for his services daring the past three years, and he was asked to seek re-election in November. He replied that at present he could not see his way clear to do so. A discussion ensued,and ultimately Mr Evans consented to reconsider the matter and report his decision a fortnight hence.At a meeting of the Conservatives of the Town Hall Ward, a vote of condolence was passed with the widow of the late Mr. T. C. Brooks, who was for many !years vice-chairman of the ward. It was decided to offer no opposition to the candida- ture of Councillor Lord, whose term of office expires i nNovember. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Choir at New Brighton Eisteddfod. The third annual musical eisteddfod promoted by the management of New Brighton Tower, which took place on Saturday, proved quite as successful as its predecessors. The spacious Tower Theatre was crowded. The chief competition in the afternoon was the one for male voice choirs (30 to 35 voices). The first prize was £25 and silver challenge cup, value 30 guineas; second, £5 and silver medal to the conductor. The test piece was "rho Britons" (Dan Protheroe), and the following choirs competed :--Manchester Orpheus Prize Glee Society. conductor, Mr. W. S. Nesbitt; Southport Vocal 'Union, Mr. J. C. Clarke, F.R.C.O. ; St. Helens Male Voice Choir, Mr. Harry Berrey ; Talke and District Male Voice Choir, Mr. J. Whewall ; Warrington Male Voice Choir, Mr. S. Hassan ; and Wigan Harmonic Male Voice Choir, Mr. A. Knight, A.R.0,0. , The adjudicators awarded the first prize to Southport, and the second to Manchester. The chief choral competition was reserved for the evening programme. This was the one for mixed voiced choirs (60 to 75 voices), and it attracted a great amount of interest, the theatre being again crowded. The pieces selected were—(a) " OFather whose Almighty Power," from Handers " Judas (b) " Gently comes the breath of evening" (Isalaw). The three competing choirs were St. Helens United (Mr: Harry Berny), Talke and District (Mr. J. Whe- wall), and Waenfawr(Mr. H. J. Roberts). First prize (£35 and silver challenge shield, value 35 guineas), Talke and District ; second prize (£lO and silver medal to conductor), St. Helens. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Conservative Club Bowling Main. A successful bowling season in connection with the Conservative Club was brought to a close on Saturday evening, when Councillor John Roberts, in the pre- sence Of a large company, presented the prizes to the successful competitors in the bowling main as follows: —l, W. Millington, marble timepiece ; 2, H. Booth, walnutwood clock; 3, J. Genders, case of cutlery ; 4, T. Cooper, cruet ; 5, W. W. Westwell, teapot ; .6, H. Barnes,copper kettle ; 7, W. Kerfoot, handbag ; 8, T. Turner, set of carvers; 9, D. Rawlings, set of -brushes on stand. The winners of the consolation prizes were: —l, T. Coombs, dinner service; 2, T. Sutton, set of bowls ; 3, J. H. Gittens, umbrella ; 4, J. Graham, 1113 of tobacco and pipe ; 5, T. Horrabin, two bottles of wine; 6, W. Strettle,one bottle of wine and pipe ;7, P. Bradburn,one bottle of whisky.Votes of thanks were accorded to the donors and subscribers to the prize fund, and also to the Bowling Green Committee and secretary for their services during the past season.— On the motion or Mr. J. P. Taylor, seconded by Mr. H. Booth-, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor Roberts for presenting the prizes.—ln replying, Mr. Roberts took the opportunity of moving a vote of thanks to Mr. George Watkins, the manager, to whom great credit was due for the excellent way in which he had worked up the green to such a high standard of perfection. While regretting the fact that Mr. and Mrs. Watkins were severing their connection , with the club to take up the position of host and hostess of the White Hart Hotel, Sankey-street, he wished them every happiness and prosperity in their new undertaking.(Applanse.)—Mr. Westwell seconded, and the motion was unanimously carried.—Mr. Watkins replied, and thanked the members for their good wishes.</p>
        <p>You CANNOT DO BETTER ! Buy your boots and shoes at ALF. TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and best town.</p>
        <p>Presentation to an Honoured Stationmaster. , A public presentation was made at Moore .on Satur- day to Mr. Thomas Johnson, the late highly respected stationinaster of Moore. Mr. White presided, and Miss , Dutton, as secretary to the testimonials fund, read letters of apology from subscribers for non-attend- ance.- Before asking Miss Dutton to make the pre- sentation the Chairman remarked that they had met to Show some outward mark of respect to one whose unfailing courtesy had been the admiration of all for very many years. He could assure Mr. Johnson that he might look upon the testimonial as a spontaneous offering from the inhabitants of the district, all being unanimous in the desire to show him some -token of respect. (Applause). Miss Dutton, in a very graceful manner, then handed to Mr. Johnson an illmninated address, together with a purse containing ~22 Os. 6d., which she asked him to accept as a small moaranto of the kindly feeling the inhabitants of the district entertained towards him at the completion of 30 years' service with the railway company. She had known Mr. Johnson through times of sorrow and times of joy, and she felt that the testi- monial was not only honour to Mr. Johnson, but to his children Who would' succeed him. (Applause.) Mr. Thomas Warburton, of Daresbur2,-, spoke in terms of the highest praise of the .civility and kindness with Which Mr. Johnson treated farmers who had business at Moore Station. He was sure all regretted his retirement. In a very emotional manner Mr. Johnson tendered his heartfelt thanks for the present, and for the Many kind remarks which had be en made about his connection with the railway company. He assured those present that he had only striveti to do his duty, and felt he scarcely deserved so much praise at their hands. Mr. Congreve proposed a vote of thanks to Miss Dutton and the Rev. F. Clifton-Smith seconded, both speaking in eulogistic terms of Mr. Johnson. In reply, Miss Dutton con- sidered that all the Credit was not due to her, as Mr. J. L. Jones had very kindly assisted in soliciting sub- scriptions. A. hearty vote of thanks, proposed by the Vicar, was accorded to the chairman, awl the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The services of thanksgiving for the higathering of the harvest were commenced at the Warrington Parish Church on Thursday. A willing band of workers had decorated the church lavishly and artistically and the effect was most pleasing. There was marked attention to detail, and where everything was so well done, it would be almost impossible to single out any feature for special mention. Perhaps the most 'attention had been devoted to the chancel decorations, the altar, altar rails, clergy and choir stalls being richly embel- lishedlwith a wealth of flowers, fruit and corn. The pulpit, font, and window ledges were very tastefully adorned. The following were the decor- ators :—Altars, Miss Ethel White and Mr. F. M. Darby ; altar rails, Miss Willis and Miss Willcocks; chancel rails, Mrs. William Fairclough, Miss F. Price and Miss E. Brookes ; choir stalls, Misses Rogers, Middleton and Williams; clergy stalls, Mrs. Farrell, Mrs. Lowe and Miss Brown; litany desk, Mrs. Range; pulpit, Rev. G. W. Dickenson, Mrs. John Davies, Miss Davies ; windows in the south aisle, Miss Boyle and Mrs. Evans ; font, Mesdames R. Collin, Hall, and Mawson ; reading desk in the Boteler chapel, Mrs. Willis and Miss Lowe; gasaliers, Miss Nield and Mrs. Range. There was an early celebration of the Holy Communion on Thursday and evensong at 8 p.m. was well attended. The musical portions of the service were admirably rendered and reflected the greatest oredit upon the organist (Mr. F. M. Darby,; Mus. Bac.) The ..Magniflcat and Nunc. Dimmitis were sung to Bunnett's setting in F and the anthem "The heavens are telling" (Hay in) was given with a taste and expression which showed careful training. The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) intoned the prayers and the lessons were read by the Rev. E. J. Willcooks. The preacher was the Rev. F.*Wainwright, M. A. vicar of St. John's, Altrincham, who discoursed eloquently on the words "And all the congregation that were come again out of cap- tivity made themselves booths and dwelt in the booths." He referred to the resemblance between harvest thanksgiving services and the Jewish feast of tabernacles or booths. To the Jews as to us harvest always had at least two effects—it prompted to joy and suggested deep thought. The preacher showed that in erecting the :booths on the tops of their houses the Jews had a desire to get back to nature. It had always beena strong plea with the philosopher and historian that the people should more or less try to go back to nature especially in these luxurious and extravagant days which undoubtedly they did live in. For his part he thought that extravagance and luxury were to-day a more serious danger than any which arose from fiscal inquiry—take one side or the other. In .a luxurious and extravagant age it was well to be reminded of the simplicity of nature, and they liked to be reminded of it in a way. They were teaching people to build garden 1 cities, and why ?—to go back to natureand let nature in the form of gardens have its humanising effectlupon them The rev.gentleman applied the same argument in the case of open-air treatment for diseases. He then proceeded to expound the text and pointed out that they were all tabernacles of the living God for God dwelt in each man's heart 4.nd conscience. In conclusion he asked his hearers to think of the lessons of the harvest and said that every fruit and flower had some special meaning.— The offertory was in aid of the clergy fund. St. Barnabas' Church.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with this Church was continued on Sunday, all the services being largely attended. The preacher at the choral celebration of the Holy Communion in the morning was the Rev. J. R. Jones, vi2ar of Great Sankey, who based an interesting and instructive sermon on the words "When ye come into the land which I give unto you, and shall reap the harvest thereof, then ye shall bring a sheaf of the first fruits of your harvest unto the priest; and he shall wave the sheaf before the Lord, to be accepted for you." (Leviticus xxiii. 10 and 11.) In the afternoon a children's service Wfl6 held and the Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth), delivered an address. The ohurch was filled in the evening when the Rev. J. F. Nuthall, Vicar of St. Anne's, Liverpool, preached.lHe took for his text the words "We are labourers together with God" and urged the duty of co-operation with God by making use of the means of grace wiiich He had provided, and serving Him faith- fully.—The collections were in aid of the choir fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Legh-Street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Legh-Street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were held in this church on Sunday when the Rev. J. F. Matthews was- the preacher. Good oongregations assembled, the build ing beinefllled at the evening service. The test for I the service was "Nothing but leaves." The , organ voluntaries for the day were—morning, Finale, I 6th Organ Sonata (Mendelssohn) ; Harvest Festival March, introducing the tune " Grafenburg " (James) ; afternoon, Adagio, Ist Organ Sonata (Mendelssohn); Triumphal March (Silas) ; evening, "Lied ohne Worte;" Festal March (Elvey). Mr. Fred. Podmore presided at the organ throughout the day. In the afternoon a can- tata was rendered by the choir entitled" Two Seasons," the following being the principals: Sopranos, Misses A. Bridge, Eva Millar, A. Woodland; contraltos, Misses A. Bissel and E. Woodland; tenors Messrs. J. Corrin and W. R. Roberts; bass, Mr. W. Perry. The solos and choruses were given with precision. Mr. Alfred Spruce conducted, and after the services, votes of thanks were given to him, the choir,and all who had helped to make the ;service:is° successful. At the evening service thellachoir rendered "Sing to the Lord of Harvest," an intricate anthem, with great success. Miss Bridge and Mr. Corriu sang a duet by special request entitled "Sing to the Lord." On Monday evening members of the congregation and choir, attended a social when an interesting programme I was gone through. The Rev. J. Gardham Reed occu- pied the chair, and thanked all who had helped to make the harvest festival services so successful. Miss Elsie Spruce sang "When the children are asleep," Miss Lavinia Hewitt "Violets and Butterflies," Miss Amy Maurell " Sowing the seed," Mr. Walter Perry" Van Tromp "and a selectedsolo. Mr. Fred Podmore accom- panied on the piano. Speeches were given by Mrs. Wright (New Zealand) and Mr. Ben Parkes. A sale of fruit and vegetables concluded the meeting. The col- lections realized over £l3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Spiritualist Church, Warrington.</head>
        <p>Spiritualist Church, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were held in the Spiritualist Church, Bewsey-street, on Sunday. A liberal supply of flowers, fruitsivegetables,corn,bread,plants in bloom*. was forthcommg,and the room was tastefully decorated, At the front of the platform were arches of evergreens and flowers, in which were suspended large bunches of grapes, and at one side was suspended an ingeniously devised motto, " 0.P.5." A large table was covered with the various gifts, and the sills of the windows were also treated with evergreens, flowers, and vege- tables. On the wall at the rear of the platform were hung three oil paintings, kindly lent for the purpose, and altogether the displayreflected great credit on those who had undertaken the task.- The services on Sunday were taken by Mr. C. Llewellyn, a local preacher, and at night to a crowded congregation he spoke well on "After sowing, what shall the harvest be ?" At the evening service, Miss Carrie Hastings, a promising 3*oung . . . . „—.</p>
        <p>local .rocalist,.salig with great Cas.te "The chil.dres</p>
        <p>home' and " Angel; ever bright and fair," Mr. A. Long accompanying on the organ. On Monday evening a tea and social took place, when various items were contri- buted by several friends: Miss Carriage sang with taste</p>
        <p>and expression the well-known song uDaddy," and Mr. Jackson played with considerable ability "The Broken Melody" and the intermezzo from "Cavalberia Rusticana." The accompanist was Mr. C. Knox. Mr. Summers created considerable amusement by his render- ing of a Lancashire sketch, "The Stuffed Monkey," and Mr. J. Collin gave a number of phonographic "records." A few dances were indulged in, and at the close, after the President had thanked all Who had contributed in making the festival asuccess, the fruit, vegetables, &amp;c., were sold for the benefit of the funds of the society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey</head>
        <p>St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey</p>
        <p>On Sunday, special harvest thanksgiving services were held at St. Mary's, Great Sankey. The interior of the church had been beautifully decorated with choice fruit, flowers and produce, provided by the parishioners. It is noteworthy that this little church for years past had been to the front for Its display at the harvest festival and the artistic blending of the various colours of flowers, Eicc., but this year it ; surpassed all previous occasions. The ladies who had the work in hand were—Miss and Miss M. I Tinsley, the *Misses Jeans (3), Miss and Miss lE. Tunstall, Mrs. H. P. C. White and daughters, ! Miss Hodgkinson' Miss Houghton, Miss Perks, Miss F. Fairclough, Miss Gilbert, Miss Jones and Miss Daunt. ;Holy Communion was celebrated at 8.15 a.m. by the i Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A., vicar. The Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth, M.A., vicar of St. Barnabas', Warrington, preached at the usual morning service. A children's ; flower service in the afternoon was conducted by the ! Vicar, when the church was crowded. In the everting, the Rev. F. S. Crane, M.A., vicar of Sutton, ! conducted the service. Every available seat was I occupied by five minutes past six, and forms anq additional seats had to be provided. A great num , ber of persons were unable to gain admission. The ; offertories during the day were in aid of church expen- ses and amounted to £.22 12s 7d. The flowers brought ' by the children at the afternoon service were equally divided between the Warrington Infirmary and the Union. Anthems and appropriate hymns were sung at each service. In the morning the choir rendered the I anthem " Let us thank the Lord our God." and in the evening "While the earth remaineth." At the after- noon service the Sankey Choral Society gave" The earth is the Lord's." Miss Mary Standish presided at the 1 I organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>REVISION IN NEWTON DIVISION</head>
        <p>REVISION IN NEWTON DIVISION</p>
        <p>The recent revision in the Newton Parliamentary • Division has resulted favourably to the Unionist party, who have a net gain of 58 votes. According to the return of Mr. W. Holland Owen, hon. solicitor for the Newton Division Conservative Association, the Conser- vatives and Unionists had 58 claims allowed, compared with 39 brought forward by the Liberals. On objection the Liberals had 99 struck oft and the Conservatives O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>Tbe proof of the purity of</p>
        <p>'Provost' Oats</p>
        <p>is not far to seek. You have'nt to travel to America in search of it, but just to look at the lovely fertile fields of bonnie Scotland, where in a perfectly pure atmosphere the oats are grown, and; then to see the</p>
        <p>country mill where the oats are ground and rolled and packed, never being touched by hands.. It is impossible for anything to be more pure, and that explains why those who eat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>'Provost' Oats</head>
        <p>'Provost' Oats</p>
        <p>porridge are among the healthiest and strongest in the land.</p>
        <p>It is almost as healthy as living among the Scotch hillk, to make a daily meal of porridge made from</p>
        <p>'Provost' Oats.</p>
        <p>R. ROBINSON &amp; SON,</p>
        <p>ANNAN, N. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>OPENING OF THE SECONDARY</head>
        <p>OPENING OF THE SECONDARY</p>
        <p>DAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The secondary day school which has been instituted by the Warrington Education Committee was opeeel on Wednesday morning with a full complement al scholars and teaching staff. The class-rooms at the Technical Institute are being used for the purpose sect the number of students for whom accommodation bes been provided is 128, in addition to 94 pupil teachers - The secondary day school students are divided jet(' four sections, and two other sections are devoted tn the pupil teachers. Mr. Alexander Goy, B.A. is te head master and his staff includes : Mr. C. 3. Shepperson, physics master • Mr. R. H. Alber/r chemistry master; Mr. .I.A. Buckley. assistant masteri Miss L. Frost and Miss M. B. Morton, assists" mistresses, and Mr. H. 0. Armstrong, manual instructor. In addition to providing accommodation for On Secondary Day School, the Technical Institute is si,scr used in the day time for the instruction of elemellt"" school children in cookery. No fewer than 216 children receive lessons in cookery each week, and the number is shortly to be increased by 120 more. It will there- fore be seen that full use is being made of the Institut° by the Education Committee. From 9 o'clock. the morning until a quarter to ten in the even- ing it is now need as a centre of instruction. 13eforeproceeding with the lessons in the Secondari Day School on the opening day, Mr. F. Monks (elantr; man of the Committee) addressed a few words to students. Amongst those present were Mr. J. It (a member of the Education Committee); Mr. Murray (director of eduoation), Mr. A. Gow, B. (headmaster), and Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Woodcock. f Addressing the students, Mr. MONKS said on behalf,,_o the Education Committee, he had to extend to tol students a hearty welcome. The Committee hopedthila the students would profit by the instruction they vven receive in that school. This was an opportunity thn had never before been given to the youth of Warringtouf and he ventured to say that the Committee had appoie; ted a staff for their instruction second to none in Win rington certainly, and, he believed himself, second very few in the country. They had now the opportueu ,t_tir and it rested entirely with themselves what use t:jla made of it. He hoped the girls would workas bard° _- heartily and efficiently petition be as keen as they liked. The girls 115c1 br°slll 'as well as the boys, and he himself had not reason why girls should not take as good a Ptigeorld boys. Of course, boys ',went more into the %ties than girls, but still there were plenty of opportuel...q in these days for girls to make their ---ta in the world. He could not say more tint'ttor- urge them to take full advantage of the °I" that tunities they would be provided with 111 .dept school. He would conclude by relating a little ea a that occurred to him more than 50 years ago. 'ft" ha gentleman was addressing scholars like themselvesteg mentioned that to be in good time was to be five ruilinhol before time. In his (Mr. Monks's) business life he `,"ha found the advantage of punctuality, and one of things he would urge upon them was to be punctea'llite school and in all their relations of life. He was,(ll,lr, certain they would never regret it. He would Murray to say something on such an occasion " was historic so far as Warrington was concerned. do Mr. 31crutiv said he Ilad looked forward to WI for some time. He felt that here. as Mr. Monks said, they were making history in Warrington. would not do more, than give them a motto to ca'rici through their school life, and that was: "What is w„nbc,i doing at all is worth doing well." He was sure it carried that through their school life they would he the happier for doing so. The maricing of the registers then commenced. .00 Half-an-hour later other members of the Educeqop Committee appeared on t 4 scene under the impresta, that the opening ceremony was to be at 9.3 o—the t"?., stated on the circulars which they had receluver Amongst those present were the Rev. Morley StePP so son, Rev. John Yonge' Rev. Father Wilson, Aiderele, Bennett, Alderman Smethurst, Councillors tjP,.500, Brophy, Ellison, Craik, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, E. Pliue" T. May, and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). li They made an inspection of the roonis guidance of Mr. Gow and were pleased wit the es ts lent arrangements which had been made for the stud° and staff.</p>
