      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>"Dante"</p>
        <p>There was a very encouraging attendance at the first ef the series of University Extension Lectures on "Dante "by Mr. Philip H. Wicksteed, M.A. The lec- taires are being held under the auspices of the Warrington -Kducation Committee, and the Co-operative Hall was 'ell filled with an appreciative audience on Wednesday evening. "Dante" is a subject which has brought fame to Mr. Wicksteed as a lecturer. Almost exactly a quarter af a century agog a writer to the "Liverpool Daily rest," says, he attended one or more of a similar series 121' the same lecturer in Little Portland-street Chapel, In London. Mr. IVicksteed had then a reputation for learning; he had succeeded Dr. Martineau in the minis- try of the chapel about four years previously; but few save his friends were aware that'Dante"was among his fa.vourite studies. A copy of these lectures, published in 1879, is before me, as is also the syllabus of a course given in 1890. A comparison of these with the above indicates the ' advance of the lecturer to the fore- front of Dante scholarship. Other studies have made Progress; He is a Hehraist, a keen" higher critic," and translator of Kuenen's " Hexateuch." He speaks as Well as reads Dutch. He can chat 'pleasantly With the farmers and boatmen in Norway, is at home in Germany. reads Beowulf and Aelfred's • Boethius " as -if he were born before ,the Conquest, and, of course reads Fr2nch and Greek and Latin. On a recent visit to hs quie-t retreat at Childrey, the writer found him deep in a lecture he was to give at Oxford on the influence of Aristotle on medieval Thought, and noticed on his desk a rare volume of Albertus Magnus. He is also an authority on economics. ixteen years since the writer was a privileged mem- -ber of a small circle, including Professors Edgeworth and Foxwell, Bernard Shaw,. and others, who met at liampstead practically to listen to Mr. Wicksteed's 'brilliant exposition of the theory of values. He has pub- lished a small work on the subject that ought to be, .3_2(.1 we hope will be, the prelude to a much ‘a, rger volume. But hitherto " Dante " has chiefly brought him fame. He is simply steeped in his hero. -As he stands before his audience, generally without a note, gradually and then swiftly the age, environment. Philosophy, religion, symbolism, humour, and passion of the great Florentine are described and conveye d with en 'infectious enthusiasm; for Mr. Wicksteed has more °f the prophet iniaim than the pedant. He feels keenly the'bearing of the master's teaching on the present age. -tt is something of a mission titf him to open the, minds Ipf the public to the spiritual greatness p of medievalism. In introducing the Lecturer, Mr. Alderman MONKS %id when the Committee came to consider, the subject for that course of lectures it was no easy matter to come to a decision, especially when a request had been made for a course of lectures on" Dante." The Committee were informed that it was a subject that could not 4PPeal to a large number, and that they were not justi- lied in fixing upon such a subject. He was glad to see that the prophets were once 4tain ,mistaken, and he willingly confessed that they had a larger audience than theY ventured to hope for. This was a great encourage tent to the Committee, and he hoped as the lectures °eeanie known the attendance would be still larger. (Ifear, hear.) a, The LECTURER, who had a cordial reception, said that aninte's date might betaken as 1;300. This was the date assigned to Dante's vision. Dante wrote his book in the form of a record of actual experience in which he represented himself as travelling through hell, purgatory Rad 'heaven. He seemed to stand between what we Thought of as the Middle Ages and what we thought of „As modern history. He essentially belonged to the age nehind him. The things Dante cared about, the things he believed, all his inspiration, and all his passion be- longed to the period that lay behind him—a period nhoue which we knew nothing—but he spoke in a way as to be intelligible to make an appeal to modern times. lietvveen the time of Augustine (about 400) and haute (in 1,300) there %as practically not a eLgle voice that had carried down to us. Therefore these had been called the nine • dumb "centuries. An enormous mass of literature Was produced but it was dumb to us in the sense that rio voice carried and was felt by us. The consequence was that all these ages wete practically lost to our ken. Dante could bring us into touch with this lost period If we were not only. ignorant but indifferent with re- spect to a great period of history and a set of subjects -which interested the heart and brain of generation after generation of great and earnest men,if we thought the things they discussed and thought about were only silly we might be sure that meant that there was some per- manent aspect of life and thought which we had come to be indifferent to, and there was some part of our life which we had not developed and the significance of which we did not understand. We must have become dead and insensitive to things of real and permanent interest. Consequently if Dante brought us into touch and made us understand and respect Medieval ideas, aspirations, thought and religion, he Would inevitably and necessarily be at the same time teaching us more fully to appreciate and under. Stand our own life in giving us a larger insight and a larger sense of life. If Dante became the interpreter ef the dumb centuries he would become the interpreter , Of our own lives, and as we enlarged our sympathies we should find we had deepened our experience of life. Roughly speaking, the ivine Comedy, although it was not original in its choice of subject, embraced all the ,!..!Pects of thd higher life and experiences of the period in which it IN:ftB written. It reflected the learning, politics, passions, aspirations, poetry, and religion of his time. Proceeding to analyse the poem, the lecturer pointed out that it gave one a quickened • of the unity of life. The pure beauty of Dante's 'Work was next touched upon and he thought there was source of beauty more pure and more perennial than ▪ ante. To enjoy that beauty they must make up their blinds to study Dante in his own tongue and not be r (Intent with translations. In referring to the influences NOaich were reflected in Dante's work, the lecturer *owed how he imbibed • the spirit of the ,reach and Provencal literatures, dwelling especi- „lY on that relating to the Troubadours. concluding portion of his lecture was devoted 7-11 an eloquent disquisition of the "Vita Nueva" , -41° an referring to Death's love for Beatrice, he pointed °Ut that there was no trace of the element of exclusive re3aession in his love. He never asked for any recip- -tr4tY• When she recognised him with a passing word „ ;I- greeting it seemed to 136 an of such ineffable con- ai,meloension to him and tobo glorify andinplift his life rat he said there was reward in eternal life for this 'neila.ble courtesy. class was afterwards. held at which the subject `1"(ler discussion was more fully explained.</p>
        <p>g 1</p>
        <p>\ *me big vures.</p>
        <p>The population of the British Empire is 396 millions, 26 per cent. of the total population of the world. There is no corner of the King's</p>
        <p>vast dominions where Colman's Mustard is not</p>
        <p>in constant daily use.</p>
