      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ST. HELENS,</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS,</p>
        <p>Second Division.</p>
        <p>Points. For Agst. Sept. 2. Leigh „ 5. Dewsbury „ 12. Birkenhead ~ 19. Barrow home 18 6 , 26. Holbeck away 3 10 Oct, 3. Normanton „ 10. Stockport away „ 17. York home 21. Morecambe away „ 31. Brighouse 11 home Nov. 7. South Shields 14. Mißom home ; 21. Bramley ; 28. Wakefield home Dec. 5. Pontefract „ 12. Castleford away „ 19. Rochdale H away , 26. Lancaster away Jan. 2. Dewsbury away „ 9. Birkenhead ••• , 16. Barrow ;, 23. Holbeck „ 30. Normanton Feb. 6. Stockport home „ 13. York „ 20. Morecambe home ~ 27. Brighouse R away Mar. 12. Minoru away „ 19. Bramley „ 26. Wakefield Apr. 2. Pontefract home „ 9. Castleford home „ 16. Rochdale H „ 23. Lancaster home „ 30. South Shields</p>
        <p>ST. HELF.NS CHEMICAL WORKER HEAVILY FINED. John Fay, of Park-road, was charged at St. Helens on Monday with assaulting Thomas M'Caffrey. Mr. H. L. Riley prosecuted, and said this was really a serious matter. The men worked together at Baxter's Chemical Works, belonging to the United Alkali Company. On Tuesday morning Fay went into the works and challenged the man to fight. M'Caffrey declined, 15'ut Fay waited outside the. works, and some hours afterwards, when M'Caffrey left work, Fay went up to him and said, "1- have you now. I will kill you and throw you in the pit." He then took M'Caffrey by the throat and battered him severely.--Defendant made a statement which caused considerable amusement. He said that he certainly had an "ambition" to thrash complainant,and tried to restrain it, but when he got some drink and met the man he gave him what he promised.—The magistrates said they would make Fay an example for other work- men. He would be fined £3 Os. 6d. costs, and £1 for the advocate's fee-24 is. 6d. in all, or twenty-eight days.</p>
      </div>
