      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <p>THE ARTFUL DODGER.</p>
        <p>WILL MR. CHAMBERLAIN CAPTURE</p>
        <p>THE FREE • TRADERS?</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 3, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WHY HE RESIGNED,</head>
        <p>WHY HE RESIGNED,</p>
        <p>A Desperate Remedy. IT is difficult to understand on what grounds Mr. Chamberlain can be called a statesman; but we must all eve him the first place in the kingdom as an electioneering hand.. His political progress has been strewn with the wreck of promises, principles, and parties. When he came home from South Africa the political situation was black enough. He left •behind him a ruined and desolated continent, the only result of his Colonial policy. He returned to a country burdened with debt, the price of the "feather in his cap." His Government was under sentence for its untolht mismanagement of every Department of State and the crime of the Education Act. The only possible way of saving himself and his Government from eternal disgrace—to prevent defeat was impossible—was to plunge the nation into some other and even more absorbing controversy.</p>
        <p>A Political Brigand. MR. CHAMBERLAIN evidently means to press forward his Zollverein scheme until, like his numerous other proposals, it has served its turn in reviving his political fortunes. Zhen it can be dropped. Meanwhile, there is siime- thing attractive in the very audacity of his proposals, the skill with which he has handled the members of his party, and his complete sub- jugation of the Prime Minister. He has taken up a defunct policy, rescued a discredited principle from oblivion, saddled his party with a programme they had long repented of in dust and ashes. He compelled his political associates to assume and confess complete ignorance upon a question which, a few weeks before, they would have answered without a moment's hesitation. For four months Mr. Chamberlain held up the Prime Minister of England, and forced him to hold up his party. Meanwhile Mr. Chamberlain matured his plans and completed his organisation. There is not another man living who could force his party to adopt a policy which very few, if any of them, entirely agree with, and which has long since been proved to be detrimental to British people and British trade.</p>
        <p>The Fall of Mr. Balfour. ONLY a mere accident prevented Mr. Chamberlain forcing the Unionist party to accept his Zollverein as their policy. That accident was the timely, or untimely, by- elections and the presence of a few inde- pendent and powerful Free Traders in the Unionist ranks. These gave Mr. Balfour a warning that if he allowed Mr. Chamberlain to lead the party into Protection there would soon be no party to lead. But for these warnings it is clear Mr. Balfour would have carried the Unionist party,with a few brilliant exceptions, bag and baggage over to the Pro- tectionist camp. Just mark the course of events. Immediately after the Government had announced their intention of withdrawing their shilling duty upon corn Mr. Chamberlain denounced their action, and declared a tax upon food was absolutely necessary in order to carry out his proposals for giving preferential trading to the Colonies. Had Mr. Balfour opposed such a policy, he would have called upon Mr. Chamberlain either to renounce his proposals or resign. Mr. Balfour did neither. Had Mr. Balfour not been in favour of Mr. Chamberlain's policy, he would have provided every opportunity ot letting tne world see how hollow that policy was. Instead of that, Mr. Balfour strained every order of the House of Commons to prevent either friend or foe discussing Mr: Chamber- lain's policy. To pacify his followers he originated an inquiry by the Board of Trade. But in the last days of Parliament, when the House of Commons was completely silenced, Mr. Balfour issued to his colleagues a pamphlet in which he supported Mr. Chamberlain's proposals, except in so far as they referred to a food tax. That vital question he ignored. He did not oppose the food tax. But the by-elections had made it impossible td support such a proposal. Meanwhile, there had been certain developments in the Unionist Government and party. The House of Commons could not be entirely gagged, and the House of Lords could not be gagged at all. Conse- quently three or four members of the Govern- ment declared against Mr. Chamberlain's scheme. A band of Free Trade Unionists had formed themselves into the Free Food League. A split seemed inevitable, and Mr. Chamber- lain had to set to work to evolve fresh plans for holding the Unionist party together.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Meaningless Resignations.</head>
        <p>Meaningless Resignations.</p>
        <p>THE first step was to disarm suspicion by resigning his place in the Cabinet. This act was intended to delude the Free Food Unionists, and the country generally, into the belief that the food tax had been abandoned. Its real purpose was to. give Mr. Chamberlain (neater freedom to carry out his campaign. This is what was clearly Stated by Mr. Balfour in his letter -V) Mr. Chamberlain: - If the second involves„ as it most certainly does, taxation, however light, upon foodstuffs, I an convinced with you that public opinion is not yet ripe for such an arrangement. . . . feel, however, deeply concerned that you • should regard this conclusion, however well founded, as one which makes it difficult for you, in your VCr: special circumstances, to remain a member of the Government. . . If you think you can best serve the interests of Imperial unity . . . by pressing your views on Colonial preference with the freedom which is possible in an independent position, but is hardly compatible with office, how can I criticise your determi- nation? The loss to the Government is, great indeed, but the gain to the cause you have at heart may be greater still.</p>
        <p>There you have the whole case. Both Mr. Chamberlain and Mr. Balfour agree that a food tax is unpopular. Both agree that Mr. Chamberlain cannot push forward his policy while in office. Both agree that Mr. Chamber- lain can help the cause better by giving it his whole time and attentioh. Had Mr. Chamber-</p>
        <p>lain persisted in his attack upon food. as a member of the Government, a split in the Unionist party would have been inevitable. But now he is nominally outside the Govern- inent Free Trade Unionists have an excuse for abandoning their independent action, and making their peace with the Government. Mr. Chamberlain's resignation was simply a trick. It is perfectly clear that tho Prime Minister is in full agreement with Mr. Chamberlain's tax on food. Let us quote once more from the Prime Minister's farewell lqtter to Mr. Chamberlain—a letter apparently whtten in consultation with the late Colonial Secretary. Said Mr. Balfour: I believe with you that closer Fiscal union between the Mother Country and her Colonies would be good for the trade of both. . Mr. Chamberlain has left the Cabinet because, he says, "I can best promote the cause I have at heart from outside." He has gone, know- ing that he has the full sympathy of the Prime Minister, with the more practical know- ledge that while the Free Trade members of the Cabinet have had to go too, his son and his satelJius have been promoted. The Government will be more completely dominated by Mr. Chamberlain than over.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
