      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>SOCIAL REFORM.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL REFORM.</p>
        <p>By HERBERT SAMUEL, M.P.</p>
        <p>It is beyond dispute that the condition of the English people ha; vastly improved in the last half- century. It is equally beyond dispute that the standard of living in Free Trade Britain is far higher than among the-Protectionist countries of Europe. But the fact 'remains—proved by the most careful investigations— that a third of the population of our great towns are so poor that their means are barely sufficient to provide them with the necessities of life; multitudes are frequently under-fed and always over-crowded; a month's sickness or lack of employment will plunge them into destitution. There arestill 1,000,000 paupers. Still 300,000 cases of open drunkenness are annually tried in the police-courts. Still 250,000 people have to be sentenced to prison every year. Ignorance, over- work, unhealthiness, bad surroundings, hopelessness of improvement, still blight the lives—not of some insig- nificant percentage—but of a great host of the popula- tion, in the richest country that the world has ever known. We speak of our national prosperity, our teaming wealth, the greatness of our Empire: there are these miseries at the base. The figure with the head of gold and the feet Of clay—it is the image of modern England. Social Reform is the policy which tries to remedy these evils. It works through the Churches. through the Trade Unions, through the Co-operative and Friendly Societies, through the charitable agencies, through the helpfulness of one man to his neighbour. It works also through the State. Good laws have done much, and should do more, to make it easier to live well and harder to live ill. The statesman may be the most effective of philanthropists. IBut eight years of Conservative government has meant to the social reformer almost eight years of arrears. The long domination of Toryism, now so near its close, has been marked by only one large measure of progressive legislation closely touching the lives of the people— the extension of Mr. Gladstone's law of Employers' Liability by the Workmen's Compensation for Accident Acts of 1897 ; and even that measurecxcludes a third of the working classes from its benefits, and several kinds of accidents from its scope. For the rest, wehave had legislation by driblets and reform by homoeopathic doses. Some meagre Housing Acts which have touched the fringe of one great - problem, and a Licensing 'Act which has touched the fringe of another; Mr. Robson's Bill, raising the minimum age of school-exemption from eleven to twelve, per- mitted to pass into law; the Act of this year regulating ' the employment of certain classes of children—these, and a very small number of other Measures of even less limportance, are the contributions of the Government to thp solution of the social problem, the barren yield of eight years of unquestioned political power. There I were some well-meaning men who—remembering the ' early history of Factory Legislation, in which Tory Governments played a worthy part, and recalling the career of Lord Shaftesbury, a Conservative, but one of the greatest reformers—hoped that the traditions of those now distant times might be revived, and that the famous Social Programme of the election of 1895 might have been meant in earnest. Experience has ex- tinguished those hopes. It is clear to everyone that Ministers have not had their hearts in the work of 4bcial reform. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>At least twelve measures of large importance may be , mentioned which, ignored or postponed hitherto, will I demand the earnest attention of future Parliaments. One is the extension and simplification of the Com- pensation for Accidents Act. A second is a stringent , law to abolish bad conditions of housing both in town land country, and to facilitate the provision of better. I A third is the much-needed reduction in the number of public-houses and the more vigorous suppression of drunkenness. A fourth is the regulation of home work- shops, the soil in which the "sweating system" still flourishes unchecked. A large reform in the methods of ! relieving the poor is recognised to he necessary, par- ticularly with reference to the needs, on the one hand of the children, on the other hand of the aged. Those who have studied with unprejudiced minds the problem of the unemployed hold the opinion that in several , directions it is possible for the State to aid, without pauperising, the deserving among that class. To facili- tate this the provision of agricultural Small Holdings, I in order to retain a larger prOportion of the population I in the healthier surroundings of rural life, was strongly I advocated by a Committee of the House of Commons I in 1889, and by the Royal Commission on Agriculture lin 1895; the measure passed with this object by the ' previous Conservative Government in 1892 has failed , in its purpose, only 650 acres having been obtained in ten years wider its provisions. A new Small Holdings Act has long been desirable. There is the question of the Early Closing of Shops, a reform recommended by a unanimous resolution of the House of Commons hr 1893, and by the unanimous report of a Committee of the House of Lords in 1901. There is the Miners' Eight Hours Bill, delayed ' only by the opposition of Northumberland and Durham. There 'is the improvement in the conditions of employ- ment of certain classes of workmen in Govern- ,' 'Dent establishments. In order to provide the local I authorities with the means with which to carry out those portions of the work of social progress entrusted ! to their hands,the Rating of Land Values is urgently necessary. And, by no means least of the twelve, an amendment of the law relating to Trade Unions is I needed, in order to restore to the unions, as nearly as I may be, that liberty to promote or defend the interests I of their members which until recently they enjoyed un- challenged. In addition to all these, there is work to be done in the improvement, in many points of detail, of the national system of education, and of the various Acts that regulate the conditions of labour on the railways, and in the mines, the ships, the factories and workshops. These are no vague, revolutionary, or idealist pro- posals. They are all definite, and essentially practical. Every one of them has been adopted, so far as neces- sary, and is in successful operation in the colony of New Zealand—that Mecca of social reformers ; most of them in other parts of the Empire and in other countries as well. The State, indeed, cannot do every- thing to cure the diseases that afflict the body politic ; other agencies must cc-operate, and apply the medicines of voluntary effort. But it is for the next Liberal Ministry to shew that the State in this matter can do much.</p>
        <p>[This article forms one of a series which we have arranged with leading members of the Eighty Club to contribute to our columns. Book copyright strictly reserved. The next article will be by Mr. E. S. Montague, Liberal Candidate for West Cambridge- shire, and will be entitled "Some Notes on the Political Situation."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>MOTHER'S FRIEND. Earlestown Child Poisoning Case.</head>
        <p>THE MOTHER'S FRIEND. Earlestown Child Poisoning Case.</p>
        <p>The adjourned inquest on the body of William Mac- donald, the seven-weeks-old son of James Macdonald, of Haydock Street,lEarlestoxvn, who died from the effeCts of narcotic poisoning following the administration of a mixture known as "Mother's Friend, "was held on Tuesday at the Earlestown Town Hall, before Mr. S. Brighouse. Mrs. Mee a registered chemist under the old Act, and her husband were present, and were repre- sented by Mr. W. Robson, of Manchester. The evidence of the child's mother, grandmother and aunt, was to the effect that on September 10th the child was suffering from "wind," and that about half- past ten Harriet Ellis.the aunt went,ito Mrs. Mee 's shop in Bridge sitreet, for two pennyworth of "Mother's Friend," with which Harriet Ellis said she was supplied by Mr. Mee who is not a qualified chemist. The bottle was also given to her without a label attached. Half a teaspoonful was given to the child, but half an hour afterwards it was noticed that the child was groaning, and it then went to sleep. Dr. r Solomon, locum tenens for Dr. Dange, was called in, and from the symptoms suspected narcotic poisoning. He treated the child accordingly, but it died ten hours later. The child's grandmother stated that for 2.8 years she had used the "Mother's Friend" for her children, treating fourteen of them, but she had previously obtained it from another chemist. That which she obtained from Mrs. Mee was of a different colour from that obtained from Mr. Peake, the originator of the mixture. Mrs. Mee's was a very dark brown, and Mr. Peake's of a light watery colour. She did not suspect anything from the different colour. The contents of the stomach Of the child,together with the bottle of mixture, were sent to the public analyst, who stated that a one-and-a-half ounce bottle of the mixture contained about 3 drachms of syrup of poppies' flavoured with aniseed, and containing 1-50 grain oimorphine to the fluid drachm. He came to the conclusion that the syrup of poppies was a weak pre7 paration, but infants were peculiarly susceptible to the action of opiates, and deaths from smaller doses were on record. The drug contained a small quantity of salicylic acid, presumably as a preservative, but the quantitywould be too small to have any material effect. The presence of salicylic acid in drugs was very objectionable, and more so as there was no necessity for it in the case of strong syrup. . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. Solomon's. evidenCe as to the effect that death was due to narcotic poisoning and that the congestion of the lung from which the child was suffering would have been Accelerated by the drug.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Robson said he did not I).i_ipose to call Mr. or Mrs. Mee.</p>
        <p>The Coroner briefly put the case befor? the jury, and they returned a verdict of death from misadventure, and Considered that Mr. Mee was censurable.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that in view of the probable pro- ceedin‘gs by the PharmAceutical Society he would offer no comments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>RECORD FOR ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>A RECORD FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Fifteen weeks had elapsed up to Saturday night with- out the St. Helens Fire Brigade having received a call. This constitutes a record for many years. On Saturday night they were summoned to a fireat the slaughter house connected with the Co-operative Stores in Henry- street, but no serious damage was done.</p>
      </div>
