      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>8</p>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. Matter intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper and sent in, if possible,not later than Thursday. - - • -</p>
        <p>Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly A short letter has a much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>We cannot under ordinary circumstances undertake to return themanuscripts which are not used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Wanted—a Stockton Heath Cricket Club</head>
        <p>Wanted—a Stockton Heath Cricket Club</p>
        <p>Sir,—As it is a recognised fact that, of our great National games cricket is the healthiest,most strenthen- ing and safest, I should like to convince the people of our large suburb of their mistake in not encouraging it. If Stockton Heath wants a crioket club it should move at once, for it is not possible to have a pitch ready for next season, if a move is not made this month. An attempt to raise members and discuss the possibility and uses of a club will be made immediately and a meeting will be held in the National School rooms on Friday the 9th,—Yours &amp;c,</p>
        <p>"A -1E1IBE1t."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Selection of Mayor.</head>
        <p>Selection of Mayor.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am not surprised that Mr. Parr has refused to accept the office of Mayor for the third year. I think it would have been unwise for him to have done so, and thus have pushed other gentlemen out who are well qualified to fill the office. As was expected, one of the gentlemen I named in my letter in last Saturday's "Examiner" (Councillor William Pierpoint) was pro- posed; but I regret to say in such a manner and with ;such party spirit: that it was enough to have made every Conservative member of the Council hold aloof, however much they may have liked to support Mr. Pierpoint, and I mid other Conservatives were prepared to do so in order,after the November elections, to have made his nomination as Mayor unanimous, as they were prepared to have done in the case of the other two gentlemen if either had been proposed. Their one desire is that the selection should be unanimous. Now, unfor- tunately the question is adjourned, and unanimity I am -afraid spoiled. As I have stated, it is no use to attempt to make the selection until after the November elec- tions. There appears to be a confusion of parties in the Council at present, one distrusting the other, and if our town is to prosper, this feeling must be stamped out.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Passive Resistance.</head>
        <p>Passive Resistance.</p>
        <p>Sir.—ln reference to the sale of Passive Resisters' goods on Tuesday last. I should like to remark that while the members of the League were conscious of some well pronounced sympathy in various quarters, the public expression evoked on that occasion far ex- ceeded their most sanguine expectations, an.l has affordz)d them a large amount of encouragement. It is I believe, almost proverbial that Warrington is " hard to move "but lam convinced that once stirred it does not readily let go an expousedcause,and surely Tuesday's demonstmtion shows that its heart is touched in this very serious matter. SRI venture, therefore, through the medium of your columns, to further appeal to all, in view of the future to recognise in this movement the persistant force of an intense conscientious conviction,which aims at no party, sectarian, or favoured class advantage, but a just and equal right and liberty for all initial realm of religion; where no Than has the right to assume lordship over another, and that those whose sympathy is already en- listed will fan that sympathy into a zeal thatshall burn in earnest co-operation with us so long as this bitter struggle shall last, and so strengthen our hands by standing by us as "Associates" or if in the pesition to do so, factually keep in the contact by direct passive resistance.</p>
        <p>To give this practical form I desire to renew the offer made at Tuesday's meeting which many availed themselves of then,to supply copies of the League mani- festoes and pledge forms for Passive Resistance, or associate membership for those who do net pay thei rates separately, gratis, to any who may desire to have them. To all such we would tay—We don't urge you to join the League, but we do urge your earnest considera- tion, after which, if the call of duty is clear, we shall gladly welcome yours names on the roll. The mani- festoes and forms may be had on application at 21, Lovely-lane, or I shall be happy to supply them to representatives of any of our religious bodies in the town, for judicious distrigution amongst their respec- congregations. Faithfully yours, ' WILLIAM CLATWORTHY. Sec. and Treas. Warrington Citizens' League. 21, Lovely-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS. Holy Trinity Church.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS. Holy Trinity Church.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with this church were commenced on Thursday evening when a large congregation assembled. The church had been very prettily decorated by ladies of the congregation. The preacher was the Rev. H. Alden Nash, vicar of St. Mark's Liverpool, who delivered an appropriate and earnest discourse. The choir rendered the anthem "The harvest is the end of the world," Mr. Percy Randall presiding at the organ. The offertory was on behalf of the curacy fund.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>St. George's Mission.</head>
        <p>St. George's Mission.</p>
        <p>This mission had been beautifully decorated for the harvest festival services which commenced. on Thursday evening. The work had been undertaken by the follow- ing :—Mesdames Barratt, Smith, Woods, Buckley, Knight, Messrs. Kenwright and Turton. The Rev. G. B. Thurston Rector of Lynun and formerly curate-in- charge of the mission, was the preacher and took for his text the words "She went, and came, and gleaned in the field after the reapers." The choir gave an effective rendering of the anthem aI will feed my flock" (Dr. Bridge), Mr. Turton presiding at the organ.</p>
        <p>In proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Hickman for his services, Mr. bA. SC RIVEN said the auctioneer had under- taken a very unpleasant duty in selling the goods which had been distrained to pay not the poor rate but the priests' rate. (Applause.) The Government had intro- duced a measure which would not allow them con- scientionsly to follow their Lord and Saviour Jesus i Christ (a voice: Nonsense). They were there to pro- I test against that diabolical Act from a Christian stand- . point ; never mind politics. Mr. Hickman had kindly undertaken the duty free of charge andhe (the speaker) could not do. less than propose a hearty vote of thanks to him. The Rev. J. F. Merrxews seconded and said it was at his suggestion in the first instance that Mr. Hickman undertook that most unpleasant duty. He had done it expressly on their behalf and he was not taking a single copper for his services. Might he suggest, that out of respect for constituted authority they should hold their meeting on the Fair Ground? The resolution was heartily carried and Mr. Hickman briefly returned thanks. Amongst those who witnessed the sale were Mr. W. , H. Woodcock (borough collector) and Mr. F. Timperley i who attended in their official capacity. THE PUBLIC MEETING. • MESSAGES FROM DR. CLIFFORD AND , DR. LEACH. . . DENUNCIATION OF THE ACT. . After the sale the spectators moved in a body to the Grappenhall Church. . Fair Ground where a public meeting was held. A The harvest services commenced on Friday evening, market stall was brought into requisition as a platform, September 25th, and were continued onpunday last. and round it the crowd pressed eagerly. Mr. N. Balm- The church was,, as usual, prettily decorated w thpresident of the Werrineto Cid' s' f9rd, e , n zen League, pre- flowers, plants, &amp;c., and large congregations attended side, and was supported by Mr. W. Clatworthy, secre- the different services. Special preathers had been pro- • tary and treasurer;t the Rev. J. F. Matthews, and the cured, the sermons being appropriate and edifying. The , Rev. HOll i nnico. Rev. H. Macnaughton Jones, Northwich, occupied the ! THE SECRETARY .. pulpit on Fitiday evening, the Rev, P. A. Screeton, • them the Rev. James Travis, president of the National M.A., Vicar of Christ Church, Latchford; Sunday morn- Free Church Council, but a he was net able to be present. ing; the Rev. H. V. Pigot. M.A. Vicar of Stockton He wrote : ' 24, Bouverie-street, ester. 26th emeri Sep- Heath, Sunday evening. The offertories are to be t b 1903. Dear Sir' —I very much regret that an devoted to church expenses. i. • es - engagement n Lancashire on Tuesday afternoon and i evening renders it impossible for me to be with you on St. Thomas's, Stockton Heath. i that day. With best wishes." He then tried to get Dr. Leach, of Manchester, who wrote: There was a consensus of opinion that the harvest "62, Cecil-street, Manchester, September 28th, 1903. decorations at this Church surpassed all previousefforts, i Dear Friend,—l would gladly come if I could. and they were much admired by the parishioners who :At three o'clock to-morrow, Tuesday afternoons the attended the opening service of the festival last even- bailiff is coming to take my goods, and I must inn. The decorators were:—Mrs. Greenall, Mrs. be at home then. I have given the authori- Eclersley and Mrs. Stewart, Misses Westbrook, Willett, rities due notice that I shall become an active resister Forcett, Wilson, Hedgecock, Braide, M. Richardson if any attempt is made to persecute me by taking more Seward, Bainbridge, Francomb, the day school teachers, goods than may appear to be necessary to cover my and Messrs. Spencer and Lawie. The preacher last the choir default and expenses. I wish you all good success. evening was the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton and y presiding at Keep the flag flying." He had also received a telegram Tendered special music, Mr. F. Rose ably. from Mr. Andrews, Wesleyan. missioner, Bolton, who the organ. i was unable to attend because he had to take the place Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel. lof another speaker who was ill. They would see that the passive resistance leaders were so busy that they Harvest festival services were held at this place of were falling ill. The Secretary, in conclusion,appealed worship on Sunday. The preacher at the morning to the public for support. He said they had done their service was the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees, and in the even- level best to organize this matter so that it could pass eng Councillor Upson occupied the pulpit. The choir off in a peaceable manner. They did notwant to add to rendered the anthems "Give ear, ()Lord" and "0 Lord, any agitation in the town. but they were not prepared how manifold are Thy works" at the latter service. Mr. to guarantee anything in the future. That was to say E. G. Rickers presided at the organ and Mr. D. B. Dunn they would stand on their principles and for their conducted. The Chapel had been prettily decorated, consciences. They were willing to let things take their special attention being devoted to the rostrum. On natural course without any effort whatever to make Monday evening a fruit banquet was held and was peace or mar it. They would simply leave the matter largely attended. A capital musical programme was to the judgment of the public as to whether they were submitted, the vocalists being :—Misses C. Jackson, L. right or not. (Applause). Johnson, L. Hardy, M. Dunn, Messrs. D. Dunn, G. ; Mr. BALM FORD said it was very difficult to stand Sankey, E. Jones and W. Pacey. The proceedings were there and not lay themselves open to the charge of most enjoyable. , seeking to become martyrs and gain a cheap popularity. I They thought that the gentlemen who made such state- . ; ments in the press or from the pulpit would 'ere long be enlightened. They had tried to conduct the NOTTINGHAM RACES.—The Great Central Railway campaign in a dignified manner and charitably, but Company announce cheap excursions to Nottingham (Victoria and Racecourse Stations) on Monday and when charity lapsed into indifference it was no longer' charity, but callousness. They might have hit hard Tuesday next. Passengers can obtain either single or sometimes in their speeches but they believed they had y two days' tickets and the local stations from which the spoken the truth, and they had not yet stooped to will be available are :—St. Helens (Central) Haydock, Nlakerfleld, Golborne, Lowton St. Maseesoind ridicule and insult, nor when hit hard had they with- Ashton-in- drawn behind a doubtful pride and refused to fight. Culcheth. Tickets and bills Cilll be obtained from the They were told that they had very little back bone and company's booking offices and stations and from the very little heart and their statements were !usual agents. said to be untrue. If they did not resen e</p>
