      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>St. James's, Latchford.</head>
        <p>St. James's, Latchford.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services at this Church com- menced on Thursday evening when there was a large congregation. The building had been very prettily decorated with flowers, fruit, &amp;c., by lady members of the congregation. The service was conducted by the Rev. A. F. Moxon. the special preacher being the Rev. W. Hollowell. of West Kirby Grammar School. The musiolwas of a festal character and included a harvest anthem taken from the 23rd psalm. Mr. Davies pre- sided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>St. Clement's Mission.</head>
        <p>St. Clement's Mission.</p>
        <p>There was a large congregation at this mission on Thursday evening when the harvest thanksgiving ser- vices commenced. The decorations were beautiful and artistic and reflected credit upon the ladiesl of the congregation to whom the work had been entrusted. The preacher was the Rev. E. P. Wethdrhed, ViOar of Hollinfare, who preached from the words "Consider the lilies." The musical portion of the service included the anthem "The Lord is loving unto every man,"which was admirably rendered.Mr.gruith presided at the organ The collection was in aid of the Parish Church clergy fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</head>
        <p>PASSIVE RESISTANCE.</p>
        <p>SALE OF RESISTERS' GOODS AT WARRINGTON. 17Insage from Dr. Clifford,</p>
        <p>In. the presence of a large but .good-hurnoured crowd, who .evinced the greatest interest in the pro- ceedings, goods of seven Nonconformists who refuse to pay the education rate were disposed of by public auction in the Market Place on Tuesday afternoon. -Rarely has an auction sale in Warrington aroused such interest. A. considerable time before the sale commenced spectators began to assemble, and quickly the attendance sivelled to about 1,500. 'Contrary to the experience in many towns, the proceedings were carried through most peaceably. For one thing the auctioneer, Mr. William Hickman, had little difficulty in placing himself on good terms with the audience. It was at the request of the resisters that he had undertaken the unpleasant duty, mid he did-so gratuitously. His customary geniality ,also stood him in good stead and although the crowd seemed to be largely composedof sympathisers with the resisters there was not the slightest approach to hostile denaonstration. Before the sale commenced, posters bearing the portrait of Dr. Clifford and the words in large red letters "For conscience sake" were nailed upon the wall amid some cheering. Promptly at half past two the auctioneer mounted one of the market stalls, and hastened to "put himself right." with the crowd. He said he wanted them to clearly understand that he took that sale only at the requegtnf the passive resisters— (hear, hear, and applauseouid a voice:- "Three cheers foe Hickman")—whose goods were being distrained upon for the rate. Had it been at the request of the overseers or the rate collector alone he would not have undertaken the duty. (Hear, hear.) But he was surprised when the Secretary of the Citizens' League appealed to him to undertake to sell the goods. He would not say anything about the Education-Act. He would leave that in abler , hands. He was not there to preach to them but to sell the goods: The conditions were as usual. There was no doubt they had all come for bargains, or they would not have been there. (Laughter, and a Voice: "No, no.") What he was surprised at was this: Hew soon they could get there on an occasion of that sort.and'yet at othertimes they always came late, whenthe bargains were gone. (Laughter.) They meant to be in. time that day. He could tell them what they were after. (Laughter). The firstlot he had to sell was a hand- some carriage clock. .He would not tell them whose it was, but it belonged to a lady, and he was sure they wonld bid all the better for that. He wanted to explain that whatever was paid for the goods that day the bal- ance over the amount required would be returned to the person who owned them. Theauctioneer wouldnot get a farthing out of it. (Hear. hea”.) "What shall I say for this clock?" asked Mr. Hickman. Immediately the Rev. H. Dunnico bid 17s 10d. "What 17s 10d," said the auctioneer,withshow of sur- prise. "Why, that is exactly the amount wanted for the rate "and the clock (belonging to Miss Fieldhouse) was at once knocked down. Exactly the same routine was followed in the case of the other articles. A chippendale table belonging to the Rev. J. F. Matthews, was purchased by Mr. Dunnico for 12s 3d. When a pair of silver candlesticks was knocked down to'Mr. Dunnico for 13s 11d, Mr. Hickman remarked that the audience was not giving him much work. A silver watch and chain, of which Mr. Gallop had been relieved, were next put up and someone in the crowd ventured to offer is for them. Mr. Dunnico again bid the exact amount required (17s 3d). Mr. Hickman : I should think your uncle will lend you more than that on them. (Laughter). There was no other bid and the articles Were handed over to Mr. Dunnico. The spectators were next invited to bid for a watch and suit'of clothes belonging to Mr. Balmford. The auctioneer remarked that if the clothes were too big a tuck could be placed in them, and if they were too small they could be stretched. (Laughter.) Again Mr. Dunnico was the only bidder, and the , goods *ere sold to him for £1 us. A marble clock, owned by Mr. E. Howard, was sold for 10s. 9d. to Mr. Dunnico, no one else bidding. The last lot was the only case in which the amount required for the rate and expenses was exceeded. A pair of bronze ornaments, taken from Mr. Meyer, was put up by ' the auctioneer. "I hops this will be thellast , passive resistance sale that will over be held in War- rington," said Mr. Hickman. "There is no joy in it,and no competition. Never mind, you are all good temper- ed." to-day (Laughter). Mr. Dunnico offered 15s. 9d. (the sum required) for the articles. Someone on the fringe of the crowd bid 1.65., but Mr. Dunnico went one better and offered 16s. 3d. There was no more bidding and the ornaments were knocked down to Mr. Dunnico. , Mr. Hickman: The sale is all over. lam much obliged to you for your attendance here to-day, and I shall have great pleasure, under proper conditions, in selling all of you up on some future occasion. (Laugh- ter.) * At this stage the resisters were informed that Mr. Place, the chief market inspector, wished the public meeting to be held on the Fair Ground. The Rev. J. F. Matthews explained that it would not be a political meeting. No politics would be talked. Mr. Place said he was not allowed to let the meeting be held there. The Rev. J. F. Matthews: Mr. Place has no instruc- tions to allow the meeting to be held on the present site. (Cries of "Shame.") The Rev. .T. F. Matthews: No, it is all right. ' A Voice: Goon with-the meeting. - THANKING THE .AUCTIONEER. A "DIABOLICAL ACT."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 3 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 3 1903</p>
        <p>such re.ear:ss th y would ha lass than lams p issive resister because he had not been married long If there was anything an Englishman prided enough—(laughter)—but he hoped that on the next himself upon it was his heart, his pluck, his backbone, occasion the distraints were made he would be able to and weracity. Wailst they refused to stop to use the stand by his friends who had had their goods sold up weapons of their opponents, they said that such state- that afternoon. (Applause.) As he had to speak at a ments as he had referred to were either deliberate mis- passive resistance meeting at Earlestownthat week he representation or the outcome of wilful ignorance. The would not make a speech onthat occeision,but be wished campaign would. go on. They could not look back, to move: "That we the citizens of Warrington of all The men who had put 'their hands to that plough would creeds, strongly condemn any Act which cc seriously never look back. The movement would grow. It was outrages the consciences.of the people as the Act now growing in Warrington and already they knew of many upon the Statute Book of our realm." (Applause.) Was 'others who, at the next time for paying the rateswould is notan astounding thing, he continued, that in this,2oth join their ranks. They had had very much sympathy in century of enlightenment their'bost and most peaceable the matter, not only from officials but from the towns citizens should be taken into a court of law and get people. For that they were very grateful. They had treated in the way they were treated by a Roman got odds to fight and great difficalties to overcome, but Catholic magistrate because they. believed themselves they believed they would be.overcome and they intended to be following the teachings of Jesus Christ? to resist the unjust and cowardly Education Act. (Als- (Shame). If the Roman Catholic Church became pre- ; plause.) dominant in this land, they would have the same treat- The Rev. J. F. MA.TTHMS, who had a hearty recep- ment meted out to them as Dr. Fox meted out to their tion, then ad grossed the meeting. He said he thought friends at the Police Court. But their said they would he was the last man in Warrington to court anything not submit—(hear, hear)—they would not stand it. No; like public applause. He had had so much experience if it meant dying at the stake they were prepared to die of standing before all classes and conditions of his before they would pay for such pernicious teaching. fellow-countrymen that it mattered very little to him (Applause). If the priest got control and power he whether they manifested hostility or sympathy:; yet one would do to-day what he had done before. He would naturally looked for sympathy in a campaign of that coerce not only the spirits of men but the brains and kind. And so far they had not been disappointed. All bodies of all into a foul and loathsome bondage. They round in Warrington they had been met, except, in his would not submit to that and to-day they made their judgment, in the case of the presiding magistrate, with solemn protest, and asked for sympathy. The passive the greatest possible amount of courtesy and considers- i resisters were fighting the battle of religions freedom tion, and they were present first and foremost that ' for all, and whether theyagreed with them or not, let afternoon to acknowledge with all their hearts that, them be treated as men. (Hear, hear.) They strongly kindnesS and consideration. (Hear, hear.) In the condemned the present Education Act which seriously second place he wished to remind his hearers that they outraged the consciences of so many of their best. were present not as politicians, and not as educationists, citizens, and earnestly longed for the day to come though he was second to no man in Warrington in his when it would be repealed, and England would once. love for education, and if he were to tell them what he more be the land of liberty and truth. (Applause.) was doing personally in education:matters he might put Mr. CLA.TWORTILY seconded, and remarked that he to shame many of his confreres, even in Warrington. had copies of the manifesto and forms of member- (Hear, hear.). No, they were present first and foremost ship of the League which he should be prepared to die- as Christian citizens, and they did not intend that any tribute to those who wished to have them. other citizens belonging to any particular section of The resolution was then put and carried without a the community should impose upon them a yoke that dissentient, and the large audience quietly dispersed. neither they nor their fathers were able to bear. He A FRODSHAM PASSIVE RESISTER AND HIS had received a letter from his dear old friend and SUMMONS. ' pastor, Dr. Clifford, that morning, in which he said : 'My dear Friend,—l am glad of the bold stand you At Frodsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday, before and your friends are taking for the primary liberties of Messrs. C. Taylor, J. J. Crosfield and S. Woodward the human mind. If we suffer the loss of one liberty, William Thornton, Deynecourt, Frodsham, was sum- we imperil all. For the sake of our fellow citizens and moned for the non-payment of a portion of the poor to protect their rights as well as our own, we must go rate.—Mr. G. Jones,assistant overseer, said the balance of rate owing wasills. 7d.—Mr. Thornton drew atten- is a menace to our independence and our progress. Par- forward with this fight. Rome is on the rates. That tion to an irregularity in the summons. It was served liament has no right to control any of our religious eight days before it was dated, and he was summoned organizations, least of all has it the right to appear five weeks ago.—The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. to compel us to pay for their supports—With F. A.Lake) : You were away from home at the time, kindest remembrances, I am very sincerely yours and the dates have been altered.—Mr. Thornton said J. CLIFFORD.. Rev. J. F. Matthews." (Applause.) the date of attendance had not been altered. Perhaps, continued Mr. Matthews, they might be in- —The Chairman observed that it could be dined to ask "Why are you here? What reason can amended.—Mr. Thornton preferred that it should you assign for your activity as passive resisters ?" if not be amended. It was, he said, a legal he might be allowed to put the question that way. Mr. curiosity. He did not object to appear to it.—The Balmford had referred them to the remark of the Rev. Magistrates' Clerk said the summons =lSt be Morley Stevenson in a sermon delivered two or three amended. He then altered the date.—Mr. Thornton Sundays ago in a local church to the effect that Non- said he objected to pay the rate voluntarily because the conformists had very little backbone in them with re- Act compelled him to pay a rate for the teaching of gard to this matter and that passive resistance would sectarian doctrines in which he did not believe. It soon die down. He should, however, like to tell Mr. seemed to him to be an Act byNvhich the priests of the Stevenson that if he thought Nonconformity was rooted Roman Catholic-and Anglican Churches had the liberty either in political prejudice or vapid sentiment he was to proselytize the Free Church children without let or, making a great mistake. (Hear, hear.) The country hindrance, and Nonconformists had to support there in in. days to come would have to reckon with Noncon- doing so.—The Clerk: That is, of course, not a legal , formity as it had never reckoned with it in days gone objection.—Mr. Thornton: I do not object to pay for by, and any Government that attempted to introduce education, but Ido not believe in the sectarian part of 'an educational measure as the one being administered the Act.—The Chairman : You pay under protest ? at the present time, that violated their religious liber- Mr. Thornton : Yes.—The magistrates made the usual ties and insulted the religious conviction of one-half (sailer. his 'Majesty's subjects would have to pay the penalty and reckon with them. (Applause.) 1 The Iparticular church to which he belonged, the 'Baptist Church, had a large measure of honour in this passive resistance movement, and he ventured to think that alone they could compel any Government to face the situation seriously and to ask themselves what they were going to clO with regard to the education of the boys and girls in their schools in the future, and make it impossible !for any church, however dignified or old, or whatever its prestige might be, to attempt to rule the education of the country,so that the children might be made not Christian citizens but little Catholics. (Cries of " Shame " and applause). The Rev, gentleman proceeded to cnote from an article which he stated appeared in the High Church Journal "The Guardian." The writer said " We must all recognize the possibility of having to resist passively. There is a higher law than that of earthly government. If they conflict we must obey God rather than man,. This disobedience to man would probably involve suffer- ing but it is to a great extent by such disobedience that moral and spiritual progress has been made." For once, continued Mr. Matthews, he found himself in complete accord with that leading High Church journal, and he took his stand on the platform which that paper had so ably provided for him. The dow Education Act was, he contended, a menace alike to the civil and religious liberties of the nation. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Stevenson hadventured•to say' in the particular sermon to which reference had been made that Nonconformists had not studied the Act. Why they wereThresent at that meeting because they had stuaied it, and because they knew what it was going to do with respect to the education of the boys and girls of the country. There were only two things about the Act that anyone could praise. The first was that it placed education where it ought to be,and where it would have .to be if it was to be efficient, namely, in the hail&amp; of the people—on the rates, he shou/d say. Secondly not only was the staff increased, but it was made infinitely better than it had been. They must please remember that they were not present in opposi- tion to the payment of the rates as such. He should be prepared when the Act was repealed, or modified at any rate, though it would reduce the margin of his living wage, to joyfully pay ten times the rate for which his goods had been sold that afternoon in order that the education of the children might be in- creased in efficiency. They were. not present because they objected to pay an education rate, but because they objected to pay a rate Which placed the training of the citizens of this country under the direct control of the Roman Catholics and the Anglican Church'. (Hear, hear.) He would not pay a rate for the support of a Baptist school. That was his principle. They objected to State interference in matters of religion and con- science ; if he would not pay for the support of a Baptist school, he certainly would not do so ,for the support of Roman Catholic and Anglican schools. He left the Anglican Church on conscientious grounds and he was not going to pay for the support of her teaching. Another injustice which he thought ought to appeal to , the citizens of this boroughwas that most of the School ' Management Committees were "packed" bodies. The Rev. Morley Stevenson generously ignored this in the sermon referred_ to. He wimt so far as to say that while two-thirds of the committee were provided for by the Act, two outside members might he co-opteci,and that was giving a representative element. They, how- ever, knew that even in Warrington in the case of all the Roman Catholic schools the managers were Roman Catholics and in the case of several Anglican schools,in addition to those who formed the foundation manage- ment, many others were Churchmen and not Noncon- formists. It was a palpable injustice when the schools were atmosphered by Anglicanism and Romanism when they were managed by Roman Catholics and Anglicans, and when they (the Nonconformists) had not a chance of looking in to say what the religious teach- ing of their boys and girls must be. He gobjected to pay for any atmosphere, and rather than have his goods distrainedupon in the future he would be prepared to accept the other alternative which the law imposed upon him. He would not compulsorily pay for religious teaching in which he profoundly disbelieved. They were told by diocesan inspectcirs and the clergy that the aim of schools under their control was not so much to pro- vide efficient secular education for the children as to make the schools a medium of training children for the Church to which the schools belonged. Could they conscientiously support a system which made such a rooted injustice as that ? . Could " they conscientiously say to the authorities "we will help you in this act. We will support it to the best of our ability "when on all hands an atmosphere they did not like was created, teaching which they disbelieved was given and their boys and girls were made to suffer in other ways. He would tell the Nonconformist parents present that there were something like 16,410 Anglican schools in the country whose head teacherships were absolutely closed to their sons and daughters("Shame") In most of the schools their boys and girls could not even obtain junior teachershipe. Under the Act about two dozen Nonconformist girls had obtained teacherships but so far a.s he knew at present no Nonconformist boy teacher 'under the Act had been appointed afteacher in a Church school. Was it. fair as a matter Of justice? Was it right that he shoald be called upon to pay for an Act under which, their boys and girls who wanted to enter the teaching profession had not all the head teacher- ships open to them? ("No" and applause). When the Universities of Oxford and !Cambridge were opened to Nonconformists, it was soon seen that the advantage conferred upon Nonconformists was not only not abused but was nsed to the very best advantage, and their Non- conformist Churches numbered among them many young fellows who had won senior wranglerships and occupied distinguished positions in the universities. Not until the schools of the country were placed absolutely under the control of the people and sectarian education altogether banished, might they, expect to see their boys and girls trained as they hoped to see them trained and the educational advantages of the country made the most of by the boys and girls. There were something like 45 training colleges in the country: 32- were in Anglican hands, 3 were Roman Catholic, 2 Wesleyan Methodist, and 8 nndenominational. Every year some- thing like 12,000 boys and girls sat for the King's scholarship examination and 6,000 of them passed. Of those 6,008. however, 3,000 were rejected, partly because they could not , subscribe to Anglicanism and therefore there was no place for them, aml partly because there was no room for them at all. The ;speaker contended that this was not fair and urged his hearers as soon as an opportunity occurred, to assist in making it possible for a better Government to mete out justice to all citizens of the country.alike, and so increase the value of education and make it impossible for any Church, however fine her services to the court- try might have been, to dominate the education of the land. (Applause.) The Rev. HERBERT Duassico said he was not yet</p>
      </div>
