      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>SOCIAL REFORM.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL REFORM.</p>
        <p>By HERBERT SillvlUEL, M.P.</p>
        <p>It is beyond dispute that the condition of the English people has vastly improved in the last half- century. It is equally beyond dispute that the standard of Hying in Free Trade Britain is far higher than among the Protectionist countries of Europe. But the fact remains—proved by the most careful investigations— that a third of the population of our great towns are so poor that their means are barely sufficient to provide them with the necessities of life; multitudes are • frequently under-fed and always over-croWded; a month's sickness or lack of employment will plunge them into destitution. There are still 1,000,000 paupers. Still 300,000 cases of open drunkenness are annually tried in the police-courts. Still 250,000 people have to Ibe sentenced to prison every year. Ignorance, over- work, unhealthiness, bad surroundings, hopelessness of improvement, still blight the lives—not of some insig- nificant percentage—but of a great host of the popula- tion, in the richest country that the woild has ever known. We speak of our national ,prosperity, our teaming wealth, tho greatness of our Empire: there are these miseries at the base. The figure with the head of gold and the feet 'of clay—it is the image of modern England. Sdcial Reform is the policy which tries to remedy these evils. It works through the Churches, through the Trade Unions, through the Co-operative and Friendly hocieties, through the charitable agencies, through the Selpfulness of one man to his neighbour. It works also through the State. Good laws have done much, and should do more, to make it easier to live well and harder to live ill. The statesman may be the most , effective of philanthropists. But eight years of Conservative government has meant to the social reformer almost eight years of arrears. The long domination of Toryism, now so near its close, has been marked by only one large measure of progressive legislation closely touching the lives of the people— the extension of Mr. Gladstone's law of Employers' Liability by the Workmen's Compensation for Accident Acts of 1897 ; and even that measure excludes a third of the working classes from its benefits, and several kinds of accidents from its scope. For the rest, we have had legislation by driblets and reform by homceopathic doses. Some meagre Housing Acts which have touched the fringe of one great problem, and a Licensing Act which has touched the fringe of another; Mr. Robson's Bill, raising the minimum age of school-exemption from eleven to twelve, per- mitted to pass into law; the Act of this year regulating the employment of certain classes of children—these, and a very small number of other measures of even less importance, are the contributions of the Government to the solution of the social problem, the barren yield of eight years of unquestioned political power. There were some well-meaning men who—remembering the early history of Factory Legislation, in which Tory Governments played a worthy part, and recalling the career of Lord Shaftesbury, a Conservative, but one of the greatest reformers—hoped that the traditions of those now distant times might be revived, and that the famous Social Programme of the election of 1895 , might have been meant in earnest Experience has ex- tinguished those hopes. It is clear to everyone that Ministers have not hacl, their hearts in the work of social reform.</p>
        <p>At least twelve measures of large importance may be mentioned which, ignored or postponed hitherto, will demand the earnest attention of future Parliaments. One is the extension and simplification of the Com- pensation for Accidents Act. A second is a stringent law to abolish bad conditions of housing both in town and country, and to facilitate the provision of better. A third is the much-needed reduction in the number of public-houses and the mere vigorous suppression of drunkenness. A fourth is the regulation of home work- shops, the soil in which the "sweating system" still flourishes unchecked. A large reform in the methods of relieving the poor is recognised to be necessary, par- ' ticularly with reference to the needs, on the one hand of the children, on the other hand of the aged. Thosel who have studied with unprejudiced minds the problem of the unemployed hold the opinion that in several directions it is possible for the State to aid, without ' pauperising, the deserving among that class. To facili- ' tate this the provision of agricultural Small Holdings, in order to retain a larger proportion of the population in the healthier surroundings of rural life, was strongly advocated by a Committee of the House of Commons in 1889, and by the Royal Commission on Agriculture in 1895 ; the measure passed with this object by the previous Conservative Government in 1892 has failed in its purpose, only 650 acres having been obtained in ten years under its provisions. A new Small Holdings Act has long been desirable. There is the question of the Early Closing of Shops, a reform recommended by a unanimous resolution of the House of Commons hi 1893, and by the unanimous report of a Committee of the House of Lords in 1901. There is the Miners' Eight Houk Bill, delayed only by the opposition of Northumberland and Durham. There is the improvement in the conditions of employ- ment of certain classes of workmen in Govern- ment establishments. In order to provide the local authorities with the means with which to carry out those portions of the work of social progress entrusted to their hands, the Rating of Land Values is urgently necessary. And, by no means least of the twelve, an amendment of the law relating to Trade Unions is needed, in order to restore to the unibns, as nearly as may be, that liberty to promote or defend the interests of their members which until recently they enjoyed un- challenged. In addition to all these, there is work to be done in the improvement, in many points of detail, of the national system of education, and of the various Acts that regulate the conditions of labour on the railways, and in the mines, the ships, the factories and workshops. These are no vague, revolutionary, or idealist pro- posals. They are all definite, and essentially practical. Every one of them has been adopted, so far as neces- sary, and is in successful operation in the colony of New Zealand—that Mecca of social reformers ; most of them in other parts of the Empire and in other countries as well. The State, indeed, cannot do every- thing to cure the diseases that afflict the body politic ; other agencies must cc-operate, and apply the medicines of voluntary effort. But it is for the next Liberal Ministry to shew that the State in this matter can do much.</p>
        <p>[This article forms one of a series which we have arranged with leading members of the Eighty Club to contribute to our columns. Book copyright strictly reserved. The next article will be by. Mr. E. S. Montague, Liberal Candidate for West Cambridge- shire, and will be entitled "Some Notes on the Political Situation."]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>RAVEN INN (GLAZEBURY) HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY,</head>
        <p>RAVEN INN (GLAZEBURY) HORTICULTURAL SOCIETY,</p>
        <p>The 27th annual show of vegetables, fruit,ancl flowers was hold on Saturday, Sunday, and Monday last, there being a very good attendance. The following is alist of prize winners : .</p>
        <p>Vegetables: 1, James Taylor; 2 John Maddock, sen.; 3, John Maddock, jun. : 4, Jan Boardman; 5. Joseph Warhurst; 6, Thomas Girlington; 7, Peter Hill ; 8, John Clough; 9, Thomas Peacock. White plate onions: 1, James Taylor; 2, Thomas Peacock; 3, John Clough. Red onions: 1, Joseph Warhurst: 2, James Taylor; 3, John Maddock sen. Carrots: 1, Joseph Warhurst ' • 2, Thomas Peacock. Turnips: 1, John Maddock; 2, John E. Boardman, Leaks.l, John Maddock, men.; 2, John E. Boardman; 3. John Maddock, jun, White celery L .Tames Taylor; 2. Peter Hill. Red celery: 1, Peter Hill; 2, Jos. War- hurst. Savoy Cabbage :• 1., John Clough: 2. Thomas Girlington. White cabbage: I. James Taylor; 2, Thos. Girlington. Red cabbage: 1, James Taylor; 2, Thomas Peacock. Flat potatoes: 1, Thomas Peacock; 2. Joseph Warhurst. Round potatoes: 1. Peter Hill; 2, Joseph Warhurst. Broad beans: 1, John E. Board- Mal; 2, John Maddock, son. 'Kidney beans: 1, John Maddock,sen.; 2, John E. 'Boardman. Peas: I, John Maddock, sen.; 2, Thomas Peacock. Pickling onions: I, John Clough; 2, Thomas Girlington.VApples : 1, Joseph Warhurst; 2, James Taylor. Plums: 1, Josonh Warhurst •, 2, ,Tames Taylor. Tomatoes: I, Peter Hill; 2, Joseph Warhurst; 3, John Maddock. sen. Pet Plants.-Geraninm: 1, Joseph Warhurst; 2, John Maddock, sen. Pot liliums 1, J. Warhurst; 2, J. E. Boardman. Fuschia: 1, J. E. Boardman; 2, J. Maddock, -jun. Variety: I, .T. Maddock, sen.; 1 .1. E. Boardman. Stocks (bloom): 1, J. Warhurst ; 2, T. Girlington, •Asters: c, T. Peacock; 2, .1. Warhurst. Manqolds : 1 J. Taylor: 1, J. E. Boardman. Dahlias: 1, J. Warlmrst; 2, J. Taylor; 3, J. Maddock, jun. After the distribution of prizes, which took place on Monday evening, the old and new members were enter- tained by Mr. and Mrs. John Clough to a supper, after which a very enjoyable evening was spent, Mr. John Maddock, sen., in the chair. Messrs. Boardman and Warhurst deserve praise for songs rendered. Mrs. Clough was specially thanked for the special prizes in tomatoes and vegetable marrows for the, last two • sea- -1 sons: Votes Of thanks were passed to the officers for the past year, and Kr. John Maddock, sen., was again elected chairman, Mr. John Clough treasurer, and Mr. . John E. Boardman, hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYA.NCHAPFL—The usual monthly or,an mlet ing promoted by the members of the Men's Bible Class was held in the Chapel on Sundny afternoon last. Mr .Tames Dodds presided, and there was a good ,!ongrega tion. The speaker NV:IS Mr. W. Travers, of Widne9, and he gave a thoughtful and eloquent address on "Socialism." Solos were ably delivered by Miss Florrie Woods and Mr. Broadhurst, Mr. George Weaver officiating at the organ. LIBER CLI7II.—A billiard handicap just concluded at the above club, h resulted in H.Cheetham securina the premier prize, and J. Bowyer 2nd. Much keenness has been displayed in the preliminary rounds, and the play all round has .beea of a good quality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>PRETTY WEDDING AT DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>PRETTY WEDDING AT DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>Sacker —Litton.</p>
        <p>All Saints' Church, Daresbury, was the scene of a very pretty wedding which aroused great interest on Wednesday afternoon,when Mr. John Eustace Seeker, son of the late Rev. J. T. Seeker, of St. Peter's Bishops Wearmouth (formerly of Bindey-with-Wyker, Coven- try), and nephew of Mr. John White, Moore Hall, was married to Miss Gertrude Litton, daughter of the late Mr. Thomas Litton, of Farrington, and Mrs. )Litton, of Uplands, Moore. . ! The bride and bridegroom are both well-known and are held in the highest esteem in the district. As sub-agent to Sir Gilbert Greenall Bart., under his uncle, Mr. John. White, Mr. Seeker has been 'brought into close touch with the tenantry of the estates with whom his relations have been of the happiest descrip- tion. On Wednesday afternoon the little village was all astir, a flag floated proudly from the Church Tower, and there was a liberal display of bunting. As if to impart a greater brightness to the scene, the sun shone radiantly. Inside the church a large congregation assembled the sacred building being completely filled. The eye was at once attracted by the tasteful decoration of the chancel. Choice white flowers and hot-house plants had been neatly arranged by Mr. Lockhart, the head gardener at Walton Hall. The altar looked beautiful with its handsome white frontal and vases filled with flowers. At two o'clock the bridal party entered the church. 'The surpliced choir was already in attendance, and, led tho singing of the hymn "Father of all, to Thee," Mr. Wright presided at the organ. . The bride was charmingly attired in a white satin dress trimmed with Brussels point lace. She wore the veil worn by her mother on the occasion of her marriage and carried a beautiful bouquet of orange blossom, roses and heather. The bridesmaids were Miss Litton and Mias Francis Litton (sisters of the bride), Miss Gwendoline Lloyd (cousin), Miss Maud Andrew and Misses Qynthia and Sylvia Reynolds (nieces). They wore beautiful dresses of pink crepe 'de chine over pink silk slips trimmed with point d'esprit lace and guipure, with pink picture hats. Their bouquets, presented by the bridegroom, were composed of pink Catherine Mermet roses. The bride was given away by her brother-in- law, Mr: Arthur Reynolds. Mr. W. H. Seeker (brother of the bridegrocm) discharged the duties of best man. The officiating clergy were the Rev. F. Clifton Smith, Vicar of Daresbury, and the Rev. E. B. Carleton, curate of Elland, Yorkshire. The service was fully choral, the hymn" 0 perfect love " being sung after the blessing. When the register was being signed the congregation joined in singing " Father of all creating." The wedding party left the church to the familiar strains of MeadeLssohn's " Wedding March" (played by Dr. R. Smith), and the church bells rang out a merry peal. Outside the church a number of school children, dressed in white, strewed flowers in the path of the bride and bride- groom. The reception was held at Uplands, Moore, the residence of the bride's mother, and was largely attended. Later in the day Mr. and Mrs.Secker left for Bournemouth, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was of mignonette green cloth trimmed with oriental embroidery and pale blue silk panne, with hat to match. The presents which were very handsome numbered over 150 and included a silver tray from the tenantry of the Walton Estate; a silver salver and pair of entree dishes from the employes on the same estate, silver hot water jug from the mothers of Hatton, a case of serviette rings from the children and teachers of St. Faith's Sunday school, Preston Brook and a great number of handsome gifts from the villagers and neighbours in Moore, Daresbury, Hatton, Walton and Latchford.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION FRONI WALTON ENIPLOYES.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening Mr. Seeker was the recipient • of a handsome present from the employes on the Walton estate. The gift took the form ofitwo silver entree dishes and a waiter, and attached to each article was a card bearing the inscription :—" With every good wish for health and happiness from the employes on the Walton estate." The presentation was made in the Parish Room,.Higher Walton, the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall presidkag. . Mr. Thomas Parsons said they had assembled to pre- Sent their worthy and esteemed agent with the dishes and the waiter. They wished him health and strength ,to make use of the articles, and-hoped he would belong spared to come amongst them. (Applause). Mr. Joseph Bailey, as a very old employe, made the presentation, and said he regarded it as an honour to have been hsked to do so. He hoped Mr. Seeker's life would be a long and prosperous one. (Applause). In the course of his reply, Mr. Seeker said the gift had been prompted by a good spirit, the same good spirit which had always existed on the estate for more years than he could count. Without that good feeling his work would be doubled, if not trebled, and he sin- cerely thanked all who had been working under him for the past six months, since the loss of one who. was a good friend and a good master. Since his death his work had fallen upon. him (Mr. Seeker.) He thanked all for their good wishes on that great and interesting occasion, and if wishes counted for anything he was sure that the good feeling which had been displayed towards him durinr, the past few weeks not only from neighbouring estates, but from the largest to the small- est tenant, should cause him to be very happy indeed. (Applause.) Messrs. Barlow, Lockhart, and Williams having wished Mr. Seeker "much happiness," The Rev. L. J. T. Daiwall also spoke. He, observed that an occasion of that sort was of great value, as it showed they had in their midst a kind of centre of good feeling and good wishes which was to be highly prized. They felt themselves to be like one family. They had met as tenants, as neighbours, and as householders on the estate, and they felt that the joys of each were the joys of all. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parr Declines to Serve Again.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PIERPOINT TO BE ASKED TO ACCEPT THE OFFICE.</p>
        <p>In compliance with the decision of the Town Council, a deputation, consisting of Aldermen Fairclough and Hutchinson and Councillors Tinnion and Craik, waited upon the Mayor in the Mayor's Parlour at ' the Town Hall. on Monday afternoon, for the purpose of asking him to be Mayor of the Borough for a third year. Each member of the deputation spoke, but his Worship said he did not feel disposed to accept office again ; in fact he had arranged with his family not to do so. During the two years he had been ip office he had seen the work ,1 of the members in Council and in committee, and he ' considered the manner in which it was done was both a benefit and a credit to the town.</p>
        <p>The result of the interview was reported the same evening to a meeting of the General Purposes Com- mittee, when, after some discussion, it was decided that a deputation, consisting of Aldermen Monks and Smet- hurst and Councillors Tinnion and Craik, be appointed to wait upon Councillor William Pierpoint to ask him to accept cake.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-03_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AN ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AN ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football,</p>
        <p>" Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington can look back with satisfaction on the work of the opening month of the season. Four matches have - been played three , won and one lost, which is a much better record than. Warrington usually put up in their earlier engage- ments. The month just commenced will be a busy .one the Club being down for no less than five matches only two of which are at home. They should win three of these; Hunslet, Widnes, and Swinton, and the games with Broughton Rangers and Hull Kingston Rovers may not improbably furnish a :point. '"'Taking; the season to the end of December I notice that. War- rington have eight home .and eight away matches,so that for once the fixtures appear to have been reasonably arranged. There is the same proportion in the con- cluding half of the season, with an extra match at home against St. elens in the South West Lancashire League, the return game being apparently not arranged.</p>
        <p>Two points accrued from Saturday's_encounter_with</p>
        <p>the new comers to the .League, i.. Keighley. Most people expected Warrington to 'win comfortably enough, and they did, but not before the visitors had created a mild sensation by aggregatinsr.,seven points to nil before the interval. This was their last effort however, and at the close Warrington had totted up the respectable sum of 16 points, of which Fish ob- tained seven, Dickenson six, and Isherwood, three, the whole score coming from the threequarter line, which was in a very aggressive mood as thelresult of judicious feeding by the halves, Mahoney and Davies. The game was most interesting throughouto• and few more enjoyable expositions have been given on the ground in recent years.</p>
        <p>As soon as Harris mid Edmunds become available, the tea ni should be exceedingly strong, and a fortnight will probably suffice to complete the cure of both these players. The weak spot is at half-back, hut it cannot be said the club is bad!y represented here with such men on the list as Davies, Edmunds, Duckworth, R. Bate and Mahoney. The latter was brought into the team ex perimentally and it must be said for the young Cardiff threequarter that he filled his new position with great credit. He made an excellent partner for Davies, and the clever way in which he handled the ball and passed it back was very gratifying. He certainly merits further trial at the base of the pack. The forwards again played a rattling game, and they have so far shown form of an uncommonly high order. I hope it will last. Hallam is also at his best, and the outlook generally is very encouraging.</p>
        <p>To-day's engagement is with Broughton Rangers at AT heater's Field, and a fine game should result. Though the Rangers have an inferior record to the Warringtonians so far they proved their .yriettte at Leigh last week, and will enter the 'field to-day with slight odds in their favour. Still, I expect Warring- ton to have an equal share of the game, and if form is maintained they may come off bee.. The unequal strength of the two wings is likely to prove a serious disadvantage at such a- place as Broughton, however, and much will, as usual, depend on the agile Fish.</p>
        <p>The Rangers have selected their team as follows : Poole ; Widdeson, Harry, Wilson, and Hogg; W. James and Riley; Trotter, Beetham, Whitehead,Scott, Ruddick, Templeton, Winsk-ill, and Jewitt or. Warwick.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County trial match for the selection of a team to meet Cheshire will, be played at War- rington on Monday, October 12th. Twelve-aside will he played, and there ought to be some lively scoring between the selected `.` dozens." Fish is, Of course, on the left wing for the Probables,g, while Dickenson and Boardman figure on the opposing side. I hope the crowd will turn 'up in strength and give some encouragement to the Lancashire County Union. There is always - the hope of a County match at Wilderspool some day.</p>
        <p>It was 4.20 before the visitors, in their pale blue knickers and white jerseys Stepped into the enclosure. ! Winning the toss, the Keighley captain wisely decided to play with the wind and sun behind his men in the first half. The sun, for once in a way, shone power fully and seemed to dazzle the eyes of the Warring. ton players to such an extent as to make their catch- ing and passing very erratic at times. The home team went off at a rare pace and Davies and Mahoney, feed- ing the backs smartly, gave the thr3e,-quarters oppor- tunities of showing their quality. On several occa- sions the backs broke away, and seemed likely to score. Isherwood slipped down when he had the line at his mercy, and Dickenson had a similar misfortune. Fish was watched too keenly by Pearson, the ex-Salford player, and Forster at the other side was floored when almost over. For the first quarter of an hour the home team had pretty nearly all their own way, but • the Keighley men warmed to their work and first blood was drawn by Myers, the old internatienal, who. gathered the ball from some loose play and dropped a clever goal. This success put new life into the visitors and encouraged them to do doughtier deeds. A loose pass to Forster from Mahoney was smartly intercepted by Jagger who though tackled by Boardman, succeeded in wriggling over the line and scoring a try. Walker added to the discomfiture of the home team and their supporters by adding two more points from the place kick.,</p>
        <p>With seven points in arrear, matters were looking ominous for Warrington, but the team rose to the occasion and after a vigorous assault on the visitors citadel, Dickenson received a pass front Davies via Mahoney and romped under the goal-posts. Fish had no difficulty in adding two points and when the teams turned round Warrington were two points in arrear. There was a breezy confidence that the home team would come out on top in the second half and it was not long before evidence of this was forthcoming. Mahoney and Davies again opened out the game for the three-quarters, and Dickenson receiving, raced at top speed for the line where he succeeded in ground- ing with a host of opponents around him. Unfortunately he received a nasty kick behind the ear which . rendered him unconscious for a time and necessitated his retirement from the field. Morrison was called upon to take a ,place on the right wing and Forster went centre. The home team were now one point 'ahead and were determined to hold the lead. Although sherthanded they more than held their own and after a clever movement among the backs, Fish got possession and rounding Walker scored under the posts. The same player added the additional points. This did not complete the scoring for the Warrington backs showed their superiority at every turn. Another passing movement looked as though Fish would again have an opportunity of rounding the full back and the Keighley men goirig for the wing man, Isherwood saw his opportunity and decided to run through on his own account and scored a fine try. Some amusement was caused by the sound tackling propensities of Morrison who . shadowed Jagger continually and never allowed him to get away The game on the whole was an open one, and the home team showed much better combination than at Batley.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be the same as last week, with one exception, the substitution of Lunt for Morrison, who has injured his leg.</p>
        <p>"OUR BOYS." Re-opening of Parr Hail by the Warrington Dramatic Society. "Our Boys " have come and gone, which means that amateur dramatics so far as the Warrington Society is concerned, are at an end and will not be revived for another twelve months. The sum total. of a year's work has—practically speaking—been crowded into three nights, and somehow one cannot avoid the suggestion that there is a tremendous ex- penditure of energy for very little result. All this, of course, without any disrespect to the society or disparagement of the members' work. - It would seem_ a mord fitting climax to their painstaking preparation if they went on tour, or at any rate gave more than three performances of the piece in which they have striven so hard to attain perfection. Again Ave must , add, parenthetically, that this is not a serious sug- gestion, but only an enlargement of the view that the annual iierfon-nances the Warrington Dramatic Society represent a mu/turn in parv,.. Probably the members are glad enough to doff their costumes after the last night and reflect that for good or ill,it is all over once more.</p>
        <p>This season the Dramatic Society has forsaken the Court Theatre for the more comfortable and commo- dious Parr Hall, which is resplendent in new paint and gold leaf, and .presents an attractive appearanee in direct contrast to its aforetiine somewhat barn-like interior. We have only one complaint to lodge against the decorators, and that is in respect to the gorgeous- ness of the ceiling, which is so remarkable—for War- rington—as to attract one's eyes heavenward when they should more properly be cast upon the stage. Perhaps a few notices—and this is also a not very serious suggestion—might be posted warning the audience not to allow the beauty of the decorations to distract their attention from the play. Wednesday- witnessed the re-opening of the building with the per- formance of H. J. Byron's well-known and somewhat old-fashioned comedy "Our Boys." We hardly. feel that we can congratulate the Dramatic Society on the revival of this little play, for it falls con- spicuously short of those which have engaged the attentions of the Society in recent years. We look to the members to make anwadvance, however gradual, and it is not possible to compliment them on having done so through the instrumentality of "Our Boys." The piece is not to be compared with "Lady Hunt- worth's Experiment," and lacks the scope which the. undoubted abilities of some of the artistes flemand. True, it is amusing and interesting in the main, but there are tendencies to prosiness which seem insepar- able from many of the older comedies of this class. The Society must be more ambitious and give us a play which will fully tax their capacity and increase their renown. The cast for "Our Boys" was as follows : Sir Geoffry- Champneys (a County Magnate) Talbot Champneys (his son), Perkyn Middlewick (of Devonshire House, a retired butterman) Charles Middlewick (his son) Mr. Sydney Wallhead Kempster (Sir Geoffry's manservant) • Mr. Cecil Bond Poddles (Middlewick's butler) &lt;4l, Mr. B. Fairclough, Jun- Violet Melrose (an heiress) Miss Bessie Pierpoint Mary Melrose (her pair cousin) Miss Margaret Pierpoint Clarissa Champneys Mrs. James Fairclough (Sir (4eoffry's sister) Belinda (a lodging house slave)</p>
        <p>Mr. A. S. Wright</p>
        <p>Mr. Harry Mole Mr. H. M. Ashton</p>
        <p>The story is almost too well-known to require tell- ine• but we Might just say this it is wound round the love affairs of "our boys," who each contrive to fall in. love with the wrong girl and get cut off with the proverbial shilling by their irate parents the aristo- cratic count k magnate and the democratic butterman. A period of penury in London follows, and finally the Spartan parents, relent and all goes merrily as—and to the accompaniment of—the marriage - bell. None of the parts present any very serious difficulty, and they were all very well acted. In fact it was the unifoimitv of excellence in the acting. throughout the piece which rescued it from mediocrity- We might say that all did Well, and with some reason - stop there. for it is more than usually difficult this year to discriminate between individual efforts. A special word of praise is, however, due to Mr. H. M. Ashton, whom we have not seen in a part of any mas.,,ni- tude previously, and who, as Perkyn Middlewick; excelled himself. He was very realistic as the retired. butterman, and the frequent bursts of laughter from. the audience proved that he made the most of his. opportunities. In the closing scene of Act 11. when he delivers his " ulti-pomatumi" Mr. Ashtoii was- excellent, and declaimed with trni- his!: ie power. Mention. . must also be made of: —= • Harry Mosley, whose well-known abilities we further demonstrated as Talbot Champneys, drawling and apparently effeminate heir to an honoilr'- title. Usually Mr. Mosley is the villain of the pieC'e. but nothing in the way of acting appears to cola' amiss to him. He has a talent for the histrionic art' We were glad also to see Mr. Stanley Wright back again, but though he did well, he was not so well suited as in "The- Private Secretary !' and " Liberty.</p>
        <p>Hall." The poverty of the piece in good parts 15- really to blame. There is not much scope as Sir Geofiry Chamoneys, and the same might also he said of Charles Middlewick, who has only to demonstrate his filial regard and manliness to fill the bill. The:</p>
        <p>ladies are also allotted simple parts in " Our Boys," and no fault could be found with the representation of the two cousins, by the •Misses Margaret and Bessie' Pierpoint. Both .were very natural and charming- Mrs. James Fairclough was, _as usual, quite at home, and admirably filled the role of Clarissa Champue,•s, . the baronet's sistev, while Miss Lucy Wenham scored another success in the htunble cast of maidservant. Mr. Cecil Bond and Mr. R. Fairclongh jun., filled tlie. minor parts creditably. Before the raising of the curta;n and during the intervals, Mrs. W. 13. Roberts' Orchestra " discourses sweet music." We must eimplimNit these ladies on their tuneful playing and also on a noticeable improve- ment in vigour and volume of tone. There were good audiences each evening, and especially on the Thursday when the hall was full. We hope the' results will not only be gratifying to the artistes, to' Mr. Browne (stage manager) and Mr. Bullen (hon. secretary), but will also result in the Warrington Infirmary and Nursing Institution receiving substan- tial bauefit.</p>
