        <p>lIELSBY PORTER'S TERRIBLE DEATH Killed While on a Carriage Footboard. On Tuesday Coroner Bate held an inquest at Frodsha m touching the death of Walter Clays, porter at Helsby Station, who on Saturday night was killed on the rail- way between Helsby and Frodsham. He was seen on the footboard passing from one carriage to another. He disappeared while the train was passing over Chester-road bridge. His body bore marks indicating that the bridge must have struck him off the footboard with terrible force. While going to Chester,. deceased walked along the footboard, and had remarked to his friend Brooks that there were a couple in another compartment. Brooks, who admitted having enough of drink, said he slept on the return journey, and awakened to find himself alone in the compartment and Clays on the footboard. Other evidence showed that the window of the com- partment was broken. The Coroner thought it possible that Clays was try- ing to get into another compartment. Brooks's evidence was described as unsatisfactory. A verdict of "Death from misadventure" was etur ned. Presentation to a Warrington Organist. Mr. J. Broadbent, who for two years has officiated as organist at the Church of the Sacred Heart was on Sunday afternoon presented with a handsome marble clock in appreciation of his services. Mr. Broadbent relinquished the position on account of having to leave the town.—Mr. J. Gibbins, the choirmaster, said he was voicing the feelings of their worthy Rector and the choir when he said he had a great regard for Mr. Broadbent. He had always done his best for the choir and the church and they were very sorry to lose him. (Hear, hear.)—The Rev. Father Ryan, who presided, then made the presenta- tion, and expressed the regret all felt at Mr. Broad- bent's retirement. He could not speak too highly of his efforts for the benefit of the choir, and his genial- ity, affability, and gentleness had endeared him to all of them. (Applause.)—Mr. Broadbent, in reply, thanked the choir very heartily. It had been a pleasure to work with them, and he had been glad to do anything he could. He would look upon the time he had spent at the church as the happiest in his life. :--Mr. G. Coggins also spoke in appreciation of Mr. Broadbent, and of the way he and the choirmaster had worked together for the benefit of the members.</p>
